window.PREVIEW_MOCKS["rinpas-2024"] = {"id": "rinpas-2024", "title": "RINPAS 2024", "fullTitle": "RINPAS Ranchi 2024, M.Phil / Ph.D Clinical Psychology entrance (real paper)", "sub": "100 real questions · printed worked key, checked · untimed", "sectionLabels": {"general": "General and Cognitive Psychology", "assessment": "Psychological Assessment", "social": "Social Psychology", "personality": "Personality", "physio": "Biopsychology", "dev": "Developmental Psychology", "clinical": "Clinical Psychology and Therapy", "disorders": "Psychopathology", "research": "Research Methods and Statistics"}, "questions": [{"q": "According to Masters and Johnson, following is the correct sequence of human sexual response:", "options": {"a": "Excitement-Orgasm-Plateau-Resolution", "b": "Excitement-Plateau-Orgasm-Resolution", "c": "Excitement-Arousal-Orgasm-Resolution", "d": "Arousal-Excitement-Orgasm-Resolution"}, "correct": "b", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is b: Masters and Johnson's sequence of human sexual response is excitement, plateau, orgasm, resolution. William Masters and Virginia Johnson (1966) published Human Sexual Response after observing and measuring the bodily reactions of nearly 700 adult volunteers in the laboratory, recording heart rate, blood pressure, breathing and genital blood flow. They described a four-phase cycle, often remembered by the letters EPOR. In excitement, blood flows into the genitals (vasocongestion), the penis becomes erect, the vagina lubricates and heart rate rises. In plateau, arousal climbs further and then levels off near its peak, with rising muscle tension and faster breathing. Orgasm is the brief peak, with rhythmic muscle contractions and release of tension. In resolution, the body returns to its unaroused state; men enter a refractory period in which another orgasm is not possible, while many women can return to orgasm without one. Option b is right because it is the only option that keeps their four phase names in their true order, with plateau between excitement and orgasm rather than after orgasm. Exam trap: Helen Singer Kaplan (1979) added desire as a first phase in her triphasic model (desire, excitement, orgasm), so a sequence that opens with desire or arousal is borrowing from a later model. \"Arousal\" is not one of the four Masters and Johnson phase names at all; it is the everyday word for what happens across excitement and plateau. Also watch for the refractory period being slipped in as a fifth phase; it lies inside the male resolution phase.", "topic": "Human sexual response cycle"}, {"q": "Amit met with an accident and later turned into impulsive, irresponsible and less concerned with the consequences of his actions. He must have suffered damage in:", "options": {"a": "Parietal lobe", "b": "Frontal lobe", "c": "Occipital lobe", "d": "Temporal lobe"}, "correct": "b", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is b: impulsive, irresponsible behaviour with little concern for consequences after a head injury points to damage in the frontal lobe. The frontal lobe sits behind the forehead and is the largest lobe of the brain. Its front part, the prefrontal cortex, plans actions, weighs their outcomes, holds back urges and adjusts behaviour to social rules; together these are called executive functions. The underside of the prefrontal cortex, the orbitofrontal region just above the eye sockets, is especially tied to self-control and social judgement. The classic case is Phineas Gage, a railway foreman whose left frontal lobe was pierced by an iron tamping rod in a blasting accident in 1848. He survived and could still walk, talk and remember, but his physician John Harlow (1848) reported the case and, in an 1868 follow-up, described him as fitful, profane, impatient and unable to keep to his plans, so that friends said he was \"no longer Gage\". Damage to the orbitofrontal area gives this disinhibited, impulsive picture; damage to the dorsolateral prefrontal area disrupts planning and flexible thinking; damage to the medial frontal area leaves the person apathetic. Option b is right because Amit's change is in personality and self-control, not in sensation, vision or memory, and regulating behaviour is the frontal lobe's job. Exam trap: the temporal lobe is tempting because temporal damage can change emotion, as in the placid, hypersexual syndrome that Heinrich Klüver and Paul Bucy (1937) produced in monkeys by removing both temporal lobes; but the textbook pairing of an accident with impulsivity and poor judgement is the frontal lobe and Phineas Gage.", "topic": "Frontal lobe damage and personality"}, {"q": "Reflexes are usually controlled by the:", "options": {"a": "Hypothalamus", "b": "Spinal cord", "c": "Frontal lobe", "d": "Medulla"}, "correct": "b", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is b: reflexes are usually controlled by the spinal cord. A reflex is a fast, automatic, unlearned response to a stimulus, such as pulling your hand off a hot pan. Charles Sherrington (1906), in The Integrative Action of the Nervous System, described the pathway behind it, the reflex arc. It has five links: a receptor in the skin picks up the stimulus; a sensory (afferent) neuron carries the signal to the spinal cord; an interneuron in the grey matter of the cord passes it on; a motor (efferent) neuron carries the command out; and an effector, a muscle or gland, acts. Because the loop closes inside the spinal cord, the brain is left out of the decision. Messages do travel up to the brain, but they arrive after the reflex has already happened, which is why you feel the pain a moment after your hand has jerked away. The simplest arc, the knee-jerk (patellar) reflex, has no interneuron at all: the sensory neuron synapses straight onto the motor neuron, a monosynaptic reflex. When Riya touches a hot iron, her hand pulls back before she has consciously registered the heat; the spinal cord has acted first and told the brain afterwards. Option b is right because the spinal cord is the centre of the classic reflex arc for the limb and body reflexes. Exam trap: some reflexes, such as coughing, sneezing, gagging and swallowing, are run by the medulla in the brainstem, so d looks attractive; but the word \"usually\" points to the spinal cord, which handles the typical reflexes taught in textbooks.", "topic": "Reflex arc"}, {"q": "Which one option is not a compulsive behaviour with obsessive-compulsive disorder?", "options": {"a": "Tapping", "b": "Hoarding", "c": "Washing", "d": "Checking"}, "correct": "a", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is a: tapping is the option that is not a typical compulsion of obsessive-compulsive disorder (OCD). OCD has two parts. Obsessions are unwanted, intrusive thoughts, images or urges that cause anxiety, such as a fear of germs or the doubt that the door is locked. Compulsions are repetitive behaviours or mental acts that the person feels driven to perform in response to an obsession, to reduce the anxiety or prevent a feared event, even though the act is excessive or not realistically connected to it. The classic compulsions are washing or cleaning, checking, ordering and arranging, counting and repeating, plus silent mental rituals such as saying a set phrase. Arjun fears his house will burn down, so he checks the gas knob twenty times before leaving; each check eases the dread for a few minutes, which is exactly why the habit grows. In DSM-5 (2013), OCD moved out of the anxiety disorders into a new chapter, obsessive-compulsive and related disorders. Option a is right because simple tapping is a repetitive motor act more typical of tics or the fidgeting of ADHD than of an anxiety-driven ritual tied to an obsession. Exam trap: hoarding looks like the odd one out because DSM-5 made it a separate diagnosis, but older texts and OCD symptom checklists list hoarding among compulsive behaviours, while washing and checking are the two best known compulsions. Key note: touching and tapping rituals do occur in OCD (especially tic-related OCD), and since DSM-5 (2013) hoarding is its own disorder (hoarding disorder) rather than a symptom of OCD, so b can be argued by DSM-5. The paper's answer is tapping (a), as a repetitive motor act more typical of tics or ADHD fidgeting than of anxiety-driven compulsions, and it stands.", "topic": "OCD compulsions"}, {"q": "Most human characteristics determined by many sets of genes; they are called:", "options": {"a": "Biogenic", "b": "Multigenic", "c": "Polygenic", "d": "Sociogenic"}, "correct": "c", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is c: most human characteristics, determined by many sets of genes, are called polygenic. A gene is a stretch of DNA on a chromosome that carries the code for a protein. Some traits follow a single gene, as Gregor Mendel (1866) showed with the colour and shape of his garden peas, and as in phenylketonuria or Huntington's disease in humans; such traits fall into clear either-or categories. Most human characteristics are different: height, skin colour, weight, intelligence and most personality traits vary smoothly along a continuum. Ronald Fisher (1918) showed mathematically that this continuous, bell-shaped variation is exactly what results when many genes, each with a small effect, add their contributions together. That is polygenic inheritance (\"poly\" means many). When the environment also plays a part, as it almost always does, the trait is called multifactorial. Take Kabir's height: no single \"height gene\" decides it; a great many gene variants each add a little, and his childhood nutrition adds more, so he ends up somewhere along the normal curve rather than in a \"tall\" or \"short\" box. The same logic explains why disorders such as schizophrenia run in families without following simple Mendelian ratios: risk builds up from many small genetic effects plus stress, as the diathesis-stress model proposes. Option c is right because polygenic is the standard genetic term for traits shaped by many genes acting together. Exam trap: \"multigenic\" sounds like a perfect translation of \"many genes\" and is occasionally used loosely, but the textbook term examiners want is polygenic; biogenic and sociogenic describe where a cause comes from (biology or society), not how many genes are involved.", "topic": "Polygenic inheritance"}, {"q": "Mongolism occurs due to:", "options": {"a": "An extra chromosome on pair 23", "b": "An extra chromosome on pair 21", "c": "An extra chromosome on pair 20", "d": "An extra Y chromosome"}, "correct": "b", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is b: the condition is caused by an extra chromosome on pair 21. Humans normally have 46 chromosomes in 23 pairs; pairs 1 to 22 are autosomes and pair 23 is the sex chromosomes (XX or XY). The English physician John Langdon Down (1866) first described the condition, and in 1959 Jérôme Lejeune, with Marthe Gautier and Raymond Turpin, found its cause: a third copy of chromosome 21, giving 47 chromosomes in all. This is trisomy 21. \"Mongolism\", the word in the stem, is an old, offensive term based on a racial comparison and is no longer used; the condition is Down syndrome. About 95 percent of cases arise from nondisjunction, when the two copies of chromosome 21 fail to separate during meiosis, usually in the egg; the rest come from translocation or mosaicism. The risk rises sharply with the mother's age, especially after about 35. Typical features are intellectual disability (usually mild to moderate), a flat facial profile, upward slanting eyes, a single crease across the palm, low muscle tone, heart defects and, later in life, early Alzheimer's disease, because chromosome 21 carries the amyloid precursor protein gene. A baby girl, Anaya, born to a 41-year-old mother, may be picked up by prenatal screening and then confirmed by a karyotype showing three copies of chromosome 21. Option b is right because Down syndrome is trisomy 21, an extra autosome on pair 21. Exam trap: pair 23 is the sex chromosome pair, so an extra chromosome there gives conditions such as Klinefelter syndrome (47,XXY), not Down syndrome; and an extra Y gives 47,XYY.", "topic": "Down syndrome (trisomy 21)"}, {"q": "Which of the following are symptoms seen in a child who has experienced trauma?", "options": {"a": "Aggression", "b": "Good academic performance", "c": "Tendency to play with children", "d": "Self-blame"}, "correct": "d", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is d: self-blame is a characteristic symptom in a child who has experienced trauma. Trauma here means exposure to actual or threatened death, serious injury or sexual violence, by experiencing it, witnessing it or learning that it happened to someone close. In posttraumatic stress disorder (PTSD), DSM-5 (2013) groups the symptoms into four clusters: intrusions (memories, nightmares, flashbacks), avoidance of reminders, negative changes in thinking and mood, and changes in arousal and reactivity. Criterion D, the thinking and mood cluster, includes distorted beliefs about the cause or consequences of the event that lead the person to blame themselves or others. Children are especially prone to this: because young children think egocentrically, they often decide the bad event happened because of them. Kabir, 8, saw his father beat his mother and now insists it happened because he was naughty that evening; he feels guilty and ashamed. Other childhood signs include repetitive play that re-enacts the event, frightening dreams without clear content, clinging, regression in toileting or speech, irritability, poor concentration and falling school marks. DSM-5 also has a separate preschool subtype for children aged 6 and under. Option d is right because self-blame is an internal, trauma-specific symptom named in the DSM-5 criteria, while the other options describe either healthy functioning or a general behaviour problem. Exam trap: options b and c describe good adjustment (doing well at school, playing with peers), which are protective signs, not symptoms. Key note: aggression (a) is also a common trauma reaction in children; the paper wants the most characteristic internal symptom, so d stands.", "topic": "Childhood trauma symptoms"}, {"q": "Which of the following may occur in Expressive Language Disorder?", "options": {"a": "Limited amount of speech", "b": "Difficulty learning new words", "c": "Difficulty finding the right word", "d": "All of these"}, "correct": "d", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is d: limited speech, difficulty learning new words and difficulty finding the right word can all occur in expressive language disorder. Language has two sides. Receptive language is understanding what others say; expressive language is putting your own thoughts into words and sentences. In DSM-IV (1994), expressive language disorder meant that a child's expressive language was clearly below both their nonverbal intelligence and their understanding of language, enough to interfere with school or social life. Its typical signs are a small vocabulary and a limited amount of speech, short and simplified sentences, errors in grammar and tense, trouble recalling or finding words, and slowness in learning new words. Comprehension stays relatively intact, which separates it from the mixed receptive-expressive type. DSM-5 (2013) merged both types into a single diagnosis, language disorder, within the communication disorders, defined by reduced vocabulary, limited sentence structure and impaired discourse (the ability to link sentences to explain or describe something). Meera, 5, understands instructions perfectly and follows stories, but speaks in two or three word phrases, often says \"that thing\" because she cannot retrieve a name, and needs many repetitions to learn a new word such as \"umbrella\"; her hearing and intelligence are normal. Option d is right because limited speech, difficulty learning new words and word-finding difficulty are each listed features of expressive language disorder. Exam trap: do not confuse expressive language disorder with speech sound disorder (trouble producing the sounds of words) or with stuttering (trouble with the flow of speech); expressive language disorder is about vocabulary and sentence building, not pronunciation or fluency.", "topic": "Expressive language disorder"}, {"q": "Which of the following is NOT a physical cause often associated with Phonological disorder?", "options": {"a": "A hearing impairment", "b": "Cleft palate", "c": "Small frontal lobes", "d": "Cerebral palsy"}, "correct": "c", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is c: small frontal lobes are not a recognised physical cause of phonological disorder. Phonological disorder was the DSM-IV (1994) name; DSM-5 (2013) calls it speech sound disorder. It means persistent difficulty producing the sounds of speech, so that speech is hard to understand: sounds are left out (\"ca\" for \"cat\"), swapped (\"wabbit\" for \"rabbit\") or distorted, beyond what is expected for the child's age. Most cases have no known physical cause and are thought to reflect difficulty learning the sound rules of the language. But criterion D of DSM-5 speech sound disorder names the congenital or acquired conditions that can themselves produce speech sound problems: cerebral palsy (poor muscle control of the lips, tongue and jaw), cleft palate (a gap in the roof of the mouth that lets air escape through the nose), deafness or hearing loss (the child cannot hear sounds clearly enough to copy them) and traumatic brain injury. Arjun, 6, says \"tat\" for \"cat\" and \"wed\" for \"red\"; before diagnosing, the clinician checks his hearing, examines his palate and rules out a motor problem such as cerebral palsy. Option c is right because \"small frontal lobes\" is not a recognised physical cause of speech sound problems in any diagnostic system, while the other three are standard examples. Exam trap: all three real causes involve the ear, the mouth or the motor control of the mouth; an option built on a vague claim about brain size is the odd one out. Key note: the printed answer in the coaching copy is d (cerebral palsy), but DSM-5 lists cerebral palsy among the physical conditions behind speech sound problems, so c is used here.", "topic": "Speech sound (phonological) disorder"}, {"q": "Stuttering is a disturbance in the normal fluency and time patterning of speech that is inappropriate for the individual's age. It involves which of the following?", "options": {"a": "Frequent repetitions or prolongations of sounds", "b": "Pauses within words", "c": "Filled or unfilled pause in speech", "d": "All of these"}, "correct": "d", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is d: stuttering involves repetitions or prolongations of sounds, pauses within words and filled or unfilled pauses, so all of these. DSM-5 (2013) calls stuttering childhood-onset fluency disorder. Fluency is the smooth, effortless flow and timing of speech. DSM-5 lists seven kinds of disturbance, one or more of which must occur often: repetitions of sounds and syllables (\"b-b-ball\"); prolongations of sounds (\"sssssun\"); broken words, that is, pauses within a word (\"pen ... cil\"); audible or silent blocking, which are filled or unfilled pauses in speech; circumlocutions, swapping in other words to avoid a feared one; words produced with excess physical tension; and repetitions of whole one-syllable words (\"I-I-I want\"). The problem must cause anxiety about speaking or limit communication, school or work, must begin in the early developmental period, and must not be better explained by a motor or sensory problem or another condition. Onset is usually between 2 and 7 years; most children recover, and boys are affected more often than girls, especially in cases that persist. Stuttering often worsens under pressure, such as speaking to strangers, and tends to ease when singing, reading in unison or talking to a pet. Arjun, 7, gets stuck on the first sound of his name when the teacher calls the register, then starts avoiding answering at all; that mix of blocks and avoidance is typical. Option d is right because each of options a, b and c is one of the DSM-5 symptoms of fluency disorder. Exam trap: a pause within a word (a broken word) and a silent block sound like ordinary hesitation rather than stuttering, but both are listed DSM-5 features, so do not drop them.", "topic": "Stuttering (fluency disorder)"}, {"q": "Which of the following is an example of an open-ended question as it relates to asking about family history?", "options": {"a": "Was your family dysfunctional?", "b": "Were you abused in your home?", "c": "What were things like for you growing up?", "d": "Were you the victim of any kind of abuse growing up?"}, "correct": "c", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is c: \"What were things like for you growing up?\" is the open-ended question. An open-ended question cannot be answered with a single word such as yes or no; it invites the client to tell their story in their own words. A closed question asks for a specific fact or a yes or no reply. Both have a place in a clinical interview. Open questions, which usually begin with what, how or \"tell me about\", are used early to build rapport and let important material surface; closed questions come later to pin down details such as dates, frequency or risk. Allen Ivey (1968), whose microcounselling approach trains interviewers one skill at a time, made open and closed questioning two of the basic attending skills. Family history is a sensitive area, so a good opening question is broad and neutral: it avoids jargon, does not assume anything happened and does not push the client towards a particular answer. When Riya is asked, \"What were things like for you growing up?\", she can start wherever she likes: her strict father, moving cities, a close grandmother. If she wants to mention abuse, she can, without being led there. Option c is right because it is the only question that cannot be answered with yes or no and it leaves the content entirely to the client. Exam trap: option d sounds open because it says \"any kind of abuse\", but it still invites only yes or no, and it is a leading question that suggests abuse. Option a adds a judgemental label, \"dysfunctional\", which a client may reject or misunderstand.", "topic": "Open-ended interview questions"}, {"q": "Which of the following is NOT the specifications of the POCSO ACT, 2012?", "options": {"a": "Recording the statement of the child at the residence of the child or at the place of his choice", "b": "Police officer to not be in uniform while recording the statement of the child", "c": "No aggressive questioning or character assassination of the child", "d": "The child can be detained in the police station in the night"}, "correct": "d", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is d: detaining the child in the police station at night is not a specification of the POCSO Act, 2012; the Act forbids it. The Protection of Children from Sexual Offences (POCSO) Act, 2012 is India's law on sexual offences against anyone under 18. Besides defining offences such as penetrative and non-penetrative sexual assault, sexual harassment and using a child for pornography, it sets out child-friendly procedures so that reporting and trial do not traumatise the child a second time. Section 24 covers recording the child's statement. It is to be recorded at the child's residence, or where the child usually lives, or at a place of the child's choice; as far as practicable by a woman police officer not below the rank of sub-inspector; and the officer must not be in uniform. The officer must make sure the child does not come into contact with the accused, and section 24 says plainly that no child is to be detained in the police station in the night for any reason. Section 33 governs the Special Court: the child should not be called repeatedly to testify, breaks are allowed, and the court must not permit aggressive questioning or character assassination of the child, so that the child's dignity is protected throughout the trial. Meera, 11, reports abuse by a neighbour; a woman sub-inspector in plain clothes records her statement at home with her mother present, and Meera sleeps at home that night. Option d is right because the Act bars night detention of a child at a police station, so d is the one statement that is not a POCSO specification. Exam trap: a, b and c all sound like mere courtesies, but each is a written provision of the Act; c comes from section 33, which covers the trial, not the police statement.", "topic": "POCSO Act child-friendly procedures"}, {"q": "In autistic spectrum disorder, when an individual exhibits immediate imitation of words or sounds they have just heard, this is known as:", "options": {"a": "Echoastic disorder", "b": "Phonological inhibition", "c": "Echolalia", "d": "Grapheme dysfunction"}, "correct": "c", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is c: immediate imitation of words or sounds just heard is called echolalia. Echolalia (from the Greek words for echo and speech) is the repetition of another person's words or phrases. In immediate echolalia the person repeats what was just said, straight away; in delayed echolalia they repeat phrases heard hours or days earlier, such as lines from a television advertisement. Leo Kanner (1943), in the first description of autism, noted echolalia in several of his eleven children. DSM-5 (2013) lists it under criterion B1 of autism spectrum disorder as an example of stereotyped or repetitive speech, alongside idiosyncratic phrases. Echolalia is not always meaningless. Studies of autistic children have shown that echoed phrases can serve real purposes: taking a turn in conversation, saying yes, making a request or calming oneself. Ask Kabir, 4, \"Do you want juice?\" and he answers \"Do you want juice?\" when he means yes. Speech therapists often build on this by modelling the reply they want him to echo (\"Juice, please\"). Echolalia is also normal for a short time in toddlers learning to talk, and it appears in Tourette syndrome, catatonia and some dementias. Its partner for movement is echopraxia, the automatic imitation of another person's actions. Option c is right because echolalia is the established clinical term for repeating words or sounds just heard. Exam trap: \"echoastic\" sounds like a real word because it borrows \"echo\", but no such disorder exists; and do not mix up echolalia (speech) with echopraxia (movement) or with palilalia (repeating your own words).", "topic": "Echolalia in autism"}, {"q": "When an individual with multiple cognitive disabilities has extraordinary proficiency in one isolated skill, this is known as:", "options": {"a": "Rainman syndrome", "b": "Asperger ability", "c": "Intellectual isolation", "d": "Savant syndrome"}, "correct": "d", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is d: extraordinary skill in one isolated area alongside wider cognitive disability is savant syndrome. A savant has a developmental disability, most often autism or intellectual disability, together with an \"island\" of ability far above both their own general level and, often, that of the general population. John Langdon Down (1887), the physician who also described Down syndrome, coined the phrase \"idiot savant\" in his lectures, using \"idiot\" in its old technical sense of severe intellectual disability; that label is now offensive and the condition is called savant syndrome. The psychiatrist Darold Treffert (1989), in his book Extraordinary People, made the condition widely known. He estimated that about half of savants are autistic and that roughly one autistic person in ten shows some savant ability. The skills fall in a narrow set of areas: calendar calculation (naming the weekday of any date), music (playing a piece after a single hearing), art (drawing a city skyline from memory), rapid arithmetic, and mechanical or spatial skill, all supported by exceptional memory. Kabir, 14, has an intellectual disability and struggles to count change, yet he can tell you instantly that 3 March 1995 was a Friday. Option d is right because savant syndrome is the accepted name for this combination of disability and isolated brilliance. Exam trap: \"Rain Man\" is the 1988 film in which Dustin Hoffman played an autistic savant, inspired partly by Kim Peek, a real man with an astonishing memory; it is a film title, not a diagnosis. And a prodigy, who excels without any disability, is not a savant.", "topic": "Savant syndrome"}, {"q": "Autism spectrum disorder consists of the following conditions:", "options": {"a": "Asperger's syndrome", "b": "Rett's syndrome", "c": "Childhood disintegrative disorder", "d": "All of these"}, "correct": "d", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is d: the paper counts Asperger's syndrome, Rett's syndrome and childhood disintegrative disorder all within the autism spectrum. These three, with autistic disorder and PDD not otherwise specified (PDD-NOS), were the five pervasive developmental disorders of DSM-IV-TR (2000). Asperger's disorder, named after Hans Asperger (1944), meant autistic social difficulty and narrow interests without delay in language or intelligence. Childhood disintegrative disorder, described by Theodor Heller (1908), meant at least two years of normal development followed by marked loss of language, social, play, toileting or motor skills. Rett's disorder, described by Andreas Rett (1966), affects mainly girls: after early normal development, head growth slows, speech and purposeful hand use are lost, and constant hand-wringing movements appear. Lorna Wing and Judith Gould (1979) had argued that autistic conditions form a continuum, and DSM-5 (2013) replaced the separate boxes with one diagnosis, autism spectrum disorder (ASD), defined by deficits in social communication and interaction plus restricted, repetitive behaviour, with severity rated by level of support. Riya, 3, who loses her words and begins constant hand-wringing, would once have been placed in the pervasive developmental group. Option d is right because, under the pervasive developmental disorder grouping this question follows, all three conditions sit within the autism family. Exam trap: picking one condition alone, such as Asperger's, the best known, misses that the question lists members of the old umbrella; with \"all of these\" on offer, the examiner wants the whole group. Key note: under DSM-IV-TR the \"pervasive developmental disorders\" umbrella held five conditions (autistic disorder, Asperger's, Rett's, childhood disintegrative disorder, PDD-NOS). DSM-5 (2013) folded autistic disorder, Asperger's, CDD and PDD-NOS into ASD but dropped Rett's as a separate disorder (it is a genetic MECP2 condition). So strictly by DSM-5 Rett's is not part of ASD; the paper's \"all of these\" is the old PDD answer and stands.", "topic": "Autism spectrum and PDD subtypes"}, {"q": "Behaviours/symptoms that are not included in DSM-5 autism spectrum disorder:", "options": {"a": "Problems with play/imagination", "b": "Impairments in imaginative play", "c": "Lack of functional play skills", "d": "Difficulty distinguishing fantasy from reality"}, "correct": "c", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is c: lack of functional play skills is the option not included in the DSM-5 description of autism spectrum disorder. DSM-5 (2013) defines autism spectrum disorder by two domains. Criterion A is persistent deficits in social communication and social interaction, in three areas: social-emotional reciprocity (A1), nonverbal communicative behaviour such as eye contact and gesture (A2), and developing, maintaining and understanding relationships (A3). A3 is where play and imagination appear: it includes difficulties in sharing imaginative play or in making friends, and absence of interest in peers. Criterion B covers restricted, repetitive behaviour, interests or activities, such as lining up toys or spinning objects, which is where unusual play also shows up. Imagination was one strand of the older triad of impairments described by Lorna Wing and Judith Gould (1979): social interaction, communication and imagination. Functional play, by contrast, is a developmental term: using an object for its intended purpose, such as pushing a toy car along the floor or holding a toy cup to a doll's lips. In typical development it comes before pretend play. Arjun, 4, spins the wheels of his toy car for long periods and never joins his cousins' game of shop; DSM-5 would place the spinning under criterion B and the missing shared pretend play under A3. Option c is right because \"functional play skills\" is not DSM-5's wording, while the imagination-related options sit close to what criterion A3 describes. Exam trap: all four options sound like autism symptoms, so look for the one built from developmental psychology vocabulary rather than DSM-5's own phrasing. Key note: none of the options quotes DSM-5 word for word and options a, b and d describe imagination problems close to A3's wording, while \"lack of functional play skills\" is not DSM-5's wording; the printed answer c stands.", "topic": "DSM-5 autism criteria: play"}, {"q": "All of the symptoms are symptoms of Mr. S who had damage to Wernicke's area except:", "options": {"a": "Inability to speak", "b": "Inability to comprehend", "c": "Neologism", "d": "Lesion or damage in the area of left hemisphere"}, "correct": "d", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is d: a lesion in the left hemisphere is not a symptom of Mr. S's Wernicke's area damage; it is the cause. Carl Wernicke (1874) described patients with damage to the back of the left superior temporal gyrus, next to the primary auditory cortex. This region, Wernicke's area, links heard words to their meanings. When it is damaged, the result is Wernicke's (receptive or fluent) aphasia. Speech flows at a normal rate, with normal grammar and melody, but it is empty of meaning. The person produces paraphasias, wrong sounds or wrong words (\"spoon\" for \"fork\"), and neologisms, made-up words such as \"blimter\". Comprehension of spoken and written language is poor, and so is repetition. Typically the person is unaware of the problem and may be puzzled or irritated that others cannot follow. This contrasts with Broca's aphasia (Paul Broca, 1861), where speech is slow, effortful and telegraphic but comprehension is fairly good. Mr. S, after a stroke, chats fluently, says \"the gorbish is on the tebble\" and cannot follow a simple instruction such as \"close your eyes\". Option d is right because a lesion in the left hemisphere describes where the damage is, not anything Mr. S experiences or shows; it is the cause, not a symptom. Exam trap: options b and c are true symptoms, so students go hunting among them for a false detail; the trick is to notice that d is a different kind of statement altogether. Key note: strictly, \"inability to speak\" (a) is not a feature either, because Wernicke's speech is fluent; but d is not a symptom at all, it is the cause (the left temporal lesion), so d stands.", "topic": "Wernicke's aphasia"}, {"q": "The disturbance of an ability to speak, read and write is known as:", "options": {"a": "Aphasia", "b": "Apraxia", "c": "Blindsight", "d": "Spatial Neglect"}, "correct": "a", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is a: a disturbance of the ability to speak, read and write is aphasia. Aphasia is a loss or impairment of language caused by brain damage, usually a stroke in the left hemisphere, in someone whose language was previously normal. It affects language itself, not the muscles of speech, so it shows up across speaking, understanding, reading (alexia) and writing (agraphia), and even in sign language in deaf signers. Paul Broca (1861) described his patient \"Tan\", who could say only one syllable after damage to the left inferior frontal lobe: this is Broca's (expressive, non-fluent) aphasia, with slow, effortful, telegraphic speech but fair comprehension. Carl Wernicke (1874) described the opposite pattern after damage to the left posterior temporal lobe: Wernicke's (receptive, fluent) aphasia, with fluent but meaningless speech and poor comprehension. Damage to the arcuate fasciculus, the fibre bundle joining the two areas, gives conduction aphasia, in which repetition is especially poor; damage to the whole language zone gives global aphasia. Meera, 62, has a left-hemisphere stroke; she now struggles to name objects, cannot write her grocery list and finds the newspaper hard to read, although the muscles of her hand and mouth work normally. Option a is right because aphasia is the term for an acquired language disturbance that covers speech, reading and writing. Exam trap: apraxia also follows left-hemisphere damage and often coexists with aphasia, so the two are confused; but apraxia is a problem planning skilled movements (such as waving goodbye on request), not a problem with language. Do not confuse aphasia with dysarthria either, which is slurred speech from weak or poorly controlled speech muscles.", "topic": "Aphasia"}, {"q": "Cognitive Behaviour Therapy works on:", "options": {"a": "Thought", "b": "Behaviour", "c": "Mood State", "d": "Both (a) and (b)"}, "correct": "d", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is d: cognitive behaviour therapy works on both thought and behaviour. Cognitive behaviour therapy (CBT) joined two traditions. Behaviour therapy, built on the learning research of Ivan Pavlov (1927) and B. F. Skinner (1938), changed actions directly through exposure, reinforcement and skills training. Aaron Beck (1960s), working with depressed patients, found that they had automatic negative thoughts about themselves, the world and the future (the cognitive triad), and built cognitive therapy to test and change those thoughts. Albert Ellis (1955) developed a parallel approach, rational emotive behaviour therapy. The CBT model holds that thoughts, feelings, behaviour and bodily sensations influence one another in a loop. Of these, thoughts and behaviour are the parts a person can change deliberately, so they are the two targets, and mood shifts as a result. Cognitive techniques include identifying automatic thoughts, thought records, examining the evidence and behavioural experiments; behavioural techniques include activity scheduling, graded exposure and problem solving. Riya, 20, thinks \"I'm useless, I'll fail anyway\" and stops attending classes, so her mood sinks further. Her therapist helps her test the thought against her past marks and schedules short study sessions; as her thinking and behaviour change, her low mood lifts. Option d is right because CBT intervenes directly on thoughts and on behaviour, the two halves of its name. Exam trap: option c, mood state, is what clients come in wanting to change, so it is tempting; but CBT does not work on mood directly. It changes mood indirectly, by working on the thoughts and behaviours that keep the mood going.", "topic": "Targets of CBT"}, {"q": "Which of the following is considered as the central tenet of Client Centered Therapy?", "options": {"a": "Congruence", "b": "Empathy", "c": "Unconditional Positive Regard", "d": "All of these"}, "correct": "d", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is d: congruence, empathy and unconditional positive regard are all central tenets of client-centred therapy. Carl Rogers (1957) proposed that therapy works not because of techniques but because of the relationship. In his paper on the necessary and sufficient conditions of therapeutic personality change he listed six conditions; the three the therapist must provide are known as the core conditions. Congruence, or genuineness, means the therapist is real: what they feel inside matches what they show, without a professional mask. Unconditional positive regard means warm acceptance of the client as a person, without conditions of worth, so the client does not have to earn approval. Accurate empathic understanding means sensing the client's private world as if it were one's own, without losing the \"as if\" quality, and communicating that understanding back. Rogers argued that when a client experiences these conditions over time, they drop defensive distortions, accept experiences they had denied and move from incongruence towards self-actualisation. Arjun, 19, tells his counsellor he wants to leave engineering for music and expects a lecture; instead she listens closely, reflects his excitement and fear accurately, does not judge his choice and is honest about her own reactions. Feeling accepted, he begins to explore what he actually wants rather than what his parents expect. Option d is right because Rogers treated all three as essential and interdependent, so none alone is the central tenet. Exam trap: empathy is the condition most often quoted, so students pick b, and some texts single out congruence as the most basic; when \"all of these\" is offered alongside the three core conditions, it is the answer.", "topic": "Rogers' core conditions"}, {"q": "Functional Analysis is considered under:", "options": {"a": "Classical Conditioning", "b": "Humanistic Principles", "c": "Operant Conditioning", "d": "Adlerian Principles"}, "correct": "c", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is c: functional analysis is considered under operant conditioning. B. F. Skinner (1938) showed that operant behaviour is controlled by its consequences: behaviour followed by reinforcement becomes more likely, and behaviour followed by punishment or no reward becomes less likely. Functional analysis applies this idea to a person's problem behaviour. Instead of asking only what the behaviour looks like, it asks what function it serves, that is, what keeps it going. The simplest form is the ABC model: Antecedents (what happens just before), Behaviour (described in observable terms) and Consequences (what follows). Frederick Kanfer and George Saslow (1965) expanded behavioural analysis into the SORKC model (stimulus, organism, response, contingency, consequence). Common functions are gaining attention, escaping a demand, getting a tangible item and automatic (sensory) reinforcement. Once the function is clear, treatment removes the reinforcer for the problem behaviour and teaches a better behaviour that earns the same result. Kabir, 6, is told to start his homework every evening (antecedent), throws his book (behaviour) and is sent to his room, so homework is avoided (consequence). The function is escape, so the time-out actually rewards him; the plan is to break homework into short tasks, praise effort and let him ask for a break with a card. Option c is right because functional analysis comes from Skinner's operant analysis of behaviour and its consequences. Exam trap: classical conditioning also belongs to behaviour therapy, so a looks plausible; but classical conditioning pairs stimuli to produce reflexive responses and does not analyse consequences, which are the heart of functional analysis.", "topic": "Functional analysis (ABC)"}, {"q": "In psychodynamic therapy, analysis of resistance is where:", "options": {"a": "The client is asked to talk about anything that comes to mind", "b": "The client obstructs the analysis and the therapist explores reasons for this", "c": "The therapist interprets the dreams of the client", "d": "The client transfers their feelings towards significant others onto the therapist"}, "correct": "b", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is b: analysis of resistance is where the client obstructs the analysis and the therapist explores the reasons for this. Sigmund Freud and Josef Breuer (1895), in Studies on Hysteria, noticed that patients struggled against remembering painful material even while asking to be cured. Freud called this opposition resistance: anything in the patient that blocks the work of treatment and access to the unconscious. It is the defence of repression showing itself in the consulting room. Resistance can look like arriving late, missing sessions, forgetting to pay, long silences, changing the subject just as something painful comes up, intellectualising, or suddenly feeling cured (a flight into health). Freud came to see resistance not as a nuisance but as information: what the patient avoids points to what matters most. So the analyst gently points out the obstruction and explores with the patient what it is protecting against, rather than pushing past it. Kabir, three months into therapy, arrives fifteen minutes late every time his father is due to come up; the therapist notes the pattern and wonders aloud what it might be like to talk about his father, opening the topic he has been avoiding. Option b is right because it describes both halves of the technique: the client's obstruction and the therapist's exploration of why it happens. Exam trap: the other three options are also psychoanalytic techniques, so they all sound right; match each to its name. Saying whatever comes to mind is free association, interpreting dreams is dream analysis, and redirecting feelings about significant others onto the therapist is transference.", "topic": "Analysis of resistance"}, {"q": "The link between Nervous System and Endocrine System is possible through:", "options": {"a": "Corpus Callosum", "b": "Hypothalamus", "c": "Reticular formation", "d": "Cerebellum"}, "correct": "b", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is b: the link between the nervous system and the endocrine system is the hypothalamus. The hypothalamus is a small structure, about the size of an almond, at the base of the brain just below the thalamus. It controls hunger, thirst, body temperature, sexual behaviour and the stress response, and it does much of this through hormones. It sits directly above the pituitary gland, often called the master gland, and is joined to it by a stalk. The link works in two ways. First, some hypothalamic neurons make oxytocin and vasopressin (antidiuretic hormone) and send them down their axons into the posterior pituitary, which releases them into the blood. Second, the hypothalamus secretes releasing and inhibiting hormones into a tiny network of blood vessels, the hypophyseal portal system, which carries them to the anterior pituitary; there they switch on or off the hormones that control the thyroid, adrenal glands, gonads and growth. Geoffrey Harris (1955) established this neural control of the anterior pituitary. A clear example is the hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal (HPA) axis. Before her board exam, Riya's hypothalamus releases corticotropin-releasing hormone, her pituitary releases adrenocorticotropic hormone (ACTH), and her adrenal cortex releases cortisol, which raises her blood sugar and keeps her alert: a nervous signal (worry) has become a hormonal one. Option b is right because the hypothalamus is the brain structure that turns neural signals into hormonal ones through its control of the pituitary. Exam trap: the pituitary is the gland that gives orders to the other glands, but it is itself ruled by the hypothalamus, so the bridge between the two systems is the hypothalamus.", "topic": "Hypothalamus and endocrine link"}, {"q": "In Neurons, Axons are insulated by:", "options": {"a": "Nerve fibre", "b": "Ganglion", "c": "Myelin Sheath", "d": "Sylvian Sheath"}, "correct": "c", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is c: in neurons, axons are insulated by the myelin sheath. A neuron has three main parts: dendrites that receive signals, a cell body (soma) that holds the nucleus, and an axon, a long fibre that carries the electrical signal, the action potential, away from the cell body to other cells. Many axons are wrapped in myelin, a fatty white layer made by glial cells: oligodendrocytes in the brain and spinal cord, and Schwann cells in the peripheral nerves. Myelin is laid down in segments, with small bare gaps between them called nodes of Ranvier. Because the fatty wrapping stops current leaking out, the action potential jumps from node to node instead of creeping along every millimetre of membrane. This is saltatory conduction (from the Latin saltare, to jump). It lets thick myelinated fibres conduct at about 100 metres per second or more, many times faster than thin unmyelinated fibres, and it saves energy. Myelin is also why white matter looks white. Myelination continues through childhood and adolescence, with the prefrontal cortex finishing last. When myelin is damaged, signals slow or fail. Meera, 30, develops blurred vision in one eye and tingling legs; an MRI shows patches of lost myelin in her brain and spinal cord, the picture of multiple sclerosis, an autoimmune attack on central myelin. Option c is right because the myelin sheath is the insulating cover of the axon. Exam trap: \"Sylvian sheath\" sounds anatomical because of the Sylvian fissure (the lateral sulcus separating the temporal lobe from the frontal and parietal lobes), but no such sheath exists.", "topic": "Myelin sheath"}, {"q": "Miss. B tells her partner she needs more appreciation. What module of dialectical behaviour therapy is she working on?", "options": {"a": "Mindfulness", "b": "Emotion regulation", "c": "Distress tolerance", "d": "Interpersonal effectiveness"}, "correct": "d", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is d: Miss B, asking her partner for more appreciation, is working on interpersonal effectiveness. Dialectical behaviour therapy (DBT) was developed by Marsha Linehan (1993) for chronically suicidal people with borderline personality disorder. \"Dialectical\" refers to balancing two opposites: accepting oneself as one is while working to change. DBT combines individual therapy, phone coaching, a therapist consultation team and a weekly skills group that teaches four modules. Mindfulness, the core module, teaches observing and describing the present moment without judgement and finding \"wise mind\" between reasonable mind and emotion mind. Distress tolerance teaches surviving a crisis without making it worse, through distraction, self-soothing and radical acceptance. Emotion regulation teaches naming emotions, reducing vulnerability to them and acting opposite to an unhelpful urge. Interpersonal effectiveness teaches asking for what you need and saying no while keeping both the relationship and your self-respect. Its best known skill is DEAR MAN: Describe the situation, Express feelings, Assert the request, Reinforce (explain the benefit to the other person), stay Mindful, Appear confident and Negotiate. GIVE (gentle, interested, validate, easy manner) protects the relationship, and FAST (fair, no needless apologies, stick to values, truthful) protects self-respect. Miss B might say, \"When you didn't mention the dinner I cooked, I felt invisible. I'd like you to tell me when you notice the effort I make; it would help me feel closer to you.\" Option d is right because making a clear request of another person is the heart of the interpersonal effectiveness module. Exam trap: emotion regulation (b) tempts because her need is emotional, but regulating her own feelings is internal work; telling someone else what she needs is interpersonal.", "topic": "DBT skills modules"}, {"q": "Mr. V always seems to focus on negative events in his life, even when something good happens for him. For example, when Mr. V got an A+ on his math test, he immediately assumed the test was too easy, otherwise he would not have gotten a good grade. Mr. V is most likely engaging in which type of cognitive distortion?", "options": {"a": "Catastrophizing", "b": "Disqualifying the positive", "c": "All-or-nothing thinking", "d": "Shaping"}, "correct": "b", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is b: Mr. V is disqualifying the positive, because he turns a good result (an A+) into something that does not count. A cognitive distortion is a habitual error in thinking that makes a person see events more negatively than the facts allow. Aaron Beck (1963) first described such errors in depressed patients, for example arbitrary inference, selective abstraction, overgeneralisation and magnification or minimisation. David Burns (1980), building on Beck in the self-help book Feeling Good, gave the list its everyday names, including 'disqualifying the positive'. In this distortion the person does not simply overlook good news; he actively explains it away, so it cannot challenge his negative belief about himself. 'The test was too easy', 'they were only being polite' and 'it was just luck' are typical lines. The result is that a negative self-view survives however much contrary evidence arrives, which helps keep depression going. In CBT the therapist helps the client catch the thought, weigh the evidence for and against it, and write a more balanced alternative. For example, when Riya's supervisor praises her case report and she thinks 'he says that to everyone', she is disqualifying the positive; a balanced reply would be 'he praised specific parts, so some of it really was good'. Option b is right because Mr. V meets a success and at once reinterprets it so that it says nothing good about him. Exam trap: examiners place catastrophising and all-or-nothing thinking beside it. Catastrophising predicts disaster from a small event; all-or-nothing thinking sorts things into two extremes. Neither involves dismissing a success. Also keep it apart from the mental filter, which only dwells on the negative, whereas disqualifying the positive actively converts good news into nothing.", "topic": "Cognitive distortions (Burns)"}, {"q": "The reality card of Rorschach:", "options": {"a": "Card no. 6", "b": "Card no. 4", "c": "Card no. 5", "d": "Card no. 7"}, "correct": "c", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is c: Card V is called the reality card of the Rorschach. Hermann Rorschach (1921), a Swiss psychiatrist, published ten symmetrical inkblots in Psychodiagnostik, always shown in the same fixed order. Five are achromatic (I, IV, V, VI, VII), two are black and red (II, III) and three are multicoloured (VIII, IX, X). The person says what each blot might be, and the examiner then scores location, determinants, content, form quality and whether the response is a popular one. Card V is a compact, solid black blot that almost everyone sees as a bat or a butterfly. It is the easiest card in the set: the outline is clear and there is no colour to unsettle the person. Because the popular response is so obvious, failing to see it, or giving a strange answer that does not fit the shape, raises a question about the person's reality testing, that is, how closely perception follows the actual stimulus. For example, if Kabir says 'a bat, here are the wings and the feelers' on Card V, his conventional perception is intact; if he says 'a cut-open stomach leaking', the clinician will look closely at his form quality on the other cards too. Option c is right because Card V draws the most common popular response, so it checks the person's hold on reality. Exam trap: other cards carry nicknames too, and examiners swap them. Card IV is the 'father card' (a large, heavy figure linked to authority), Card VII the 'mother card', and Card VI the 'sex card' because of its shape. These nicknames come from clinical tradition rather than from Rorschach's own scoring rules, so learn them as a set.", "topic": "Rorschach card nicknames"}, {"q": "A researcher uses an experimental design to study the effect of music on memory. In this experiment, the Independent Variable is:", "options": {"a": "Music", "b": "Memory", "c": "Both music and memory", "d": "Neither music nor memory"}, "correct": "a", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is a: music is the independent variable, the factor the researcher changes to see its effect. In an experiment the researcher manipulates one variable and measures another, while holding everything else constant. The independent variable (IV) is the one the experimenter manipulates or varies; its different values are called levels or conditions (for example, music versus silence, or classical versus rock versus none). The dependent variable (DV) is the outcome that is measured, the behaviour expected to 'depend' on the IV. Here the DV is memory, measured perhaps as the number of words recalled. Anything else that could affect recall, such as time of day, list difficulty or participants' age, is an extraneous variable that must be controlled, so that any difference in memory can be credited to the music. A simple rule: the IV is the cause the researcher sets, the DV is the effect the researcher records. Stems often follow the pattern 'the effect of IV on DV', and this one reads 'the effect of music (IV) on memory (DV)'. For example, Meera gives one group of students a word list to learn while Indian classical music plays and another group the same list in silence, then counts how many words each student recalls. She decided who heard music, so music is the IV; the recall scores are the DV. Option a is right because music is what the researcher manipulates, and memory is what is measured as the result. Exam trap: examiners reverse the pair and offer the DV (memory) as the IV. Another trick is 'both', which forgets that one variable cannot be both the manipulated cause and the measured outcome in the same experiment. Note too that a purely correlational study has no true IV, because nothing is manipulated.", "topic": "Independent and dependent variables"}, {"q": "In a normal distribution, most scores are located:", "options": {"a": "At the high end of the distribution", "b": "At the low end of the distribution", "c": "In the centre of the distribution", "d": "At the extreme of the distribution"}, "correct": "c", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is c: in a normal distribution most scores cluster in the centre, around the mean. The normal distribution, or normal probability curve, is a symmetrical, bell-shaped curve. Abraham de Moivre (1733) first derived it and Carl Friedrich Gauss (1809) used it for errors of measurement (hence 'Gaussian curve'). Its key properties are these: the mean, median and mode are equal and sit at the centre; the curve is symmetrical, so skewness is zero; the tails come ever closer to the baseline without touching it (asymptotic); and fixed proportions of scores fall within each standard deviation (SD) of the mean. About 68 per cent of scores lie within 1 SD either side of the mean, about 95 per cent within 2 SD and about 99.7 per cent within 3 SD. So most people are near average, and very high or very low scores are rare (on IQ, mean 100 and SD 15, about 68 per cent score 85 to 115). If Arjun's class takes a well-made test and the scores are normally distributed, most students will score near the class average, with a few very high and a few very low scorers out in the tails. Option c is right because the peak of the bell, where most cases fall, is the centre, where the mean, median and mode coincide. Exam trap: 'at the high end' or 'at the low end' describes a skewed distribution. In negative skew the scores pile up at the high end with a tail to the left (a very easy test); in positive skew they pile up at the low end with a tail to the right (a very hard test). The extremes, or tails, of a normal curve hold the fewest scores.", "topic": "Normal curve properties"}, {"q": "Damage to the hippocampus is most likely to impair:", "options": {"a": "Memory", "b": "Balance", "c": "Sleep", "d": "Breathing"}, "correct": "a", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is a: damage to the hippocampus mainly impairs memory, especially the ability to form new long-term memories. The hippocampus is a curved structure in the medial temporal lobe, named after the Greek word for seahorse, and it is part of the limbic system. Its main job is consolidation: turning new experiences and facts (declarative or explicit memory) into lasting long-term memories, which are later stored across the cortex. It also builds maps of space; John O'Keefe (1971) found place cells in the rat hippocampus that fire when the animal is in one particular location. The classic evidence is the patient H.M. (Henry Molaison). In 1953 the surgeon William Beecher Scoville removed much of both medial temporal lobes, including most of the hippocampus, to stop his epilepsy. Scoville and Brenda Milner (1957) reported severe anterograde amnesia: H.M. could not form new declarative memories, yet his intelligence, short-term memory, older memories and procedural learning were spared (he improved at mirror drawing while denying he had ever practised). So the hippocampus is needed for new explicit memories, not for every kind of memory. For example, if Kabir suffers hippocampal damage after encephalitis, he may chat normally and recall his childhood, but forget a visitor within minutes and fail to learn the route to his new ward. Option a is right because the hippocampus is the brain's key structure for consolidating new long-term memories. Exam trap: examiners offer the jobs of nearby structures. Balance belongs to the cerebellum and the vestibular system, sleep to the hypothalamus, reticular formation and pons, and breathing to the medulla. Also do not confuse the hippocampus (memory) with the amygdala (fear and emotional memory), its neighbour in the limbic system.", "topic": "Hippocampus and memory"}, {"q": "Which part of the limbic system is involved in an animal's feeling of hunger?", "options": {"a": "Thalamus", "b": "Pons", "c": "Hippocampus", "d": "Hypothalamus"}, "correct": "d", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is d: the hypothalamus is the part of the limbic system that controls hunger. The hypothalamus is a small structure below the thalamus that keeps the body's internal balance (homeostasis): hunger, thirst, temperature, sex and the hormones of the pituitary. It is usually counted with the limbic system because it carries out the bodily side of emotion and motivation. Two of its regions were long taught as a dual centre for eating. Albert Hetherington and Stephen Ranson (1940) found that lesions of the ventromedial hypothalamus (VMH) made rats overeat and grow obese, so the VMH was called the satiety centre. Then the Indian physiologist Bal Krishan Anand, working with John Brobeck (1951), showed that lesions of the lateral hypothalamus (LH) made rats stop eating (aphagia) and drinking, to the point of starving unless fed by tube; stimulating the LH made them eat. So the LH became the feeding centre, and Eliot Stellar (1954) built both findings into the dual-centre theory. Modern work adds hormones that act on the hypothalamus, especially its arcuate nucleus: ghrelin from the empty stomach signals hunger, while leptin from fat stores and insulin from the pancreas signal fullness. Option d is right because hunger and satiety signals are integrated in the hypothalamus, chiefly its lateral and ventromedial regions. Exam trap: the classic pairing is easy to reverse. Lateral hypothalamus damage stops eating (it is the feeding or 'start' centre); ventromedial damage causes overeating (it is the satiety or 'stop' centre). Also, the thalamus sounds similar but is a sensory relay, not a drive centre.", "topic": "Hypothalamus and hunger"}, {"q": "Which area of the brain-stem controls heart rate and breathing?", "options": {"a": "Reticular formation", "b": "Medulla", "c": "Pons", "d": "Thalamus"}, "correct": "b", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is b: the medulla oblongata controls heart rate and breathing. The brainstem has three parts: the medulla (lowest, where it joins the spinal cord), the pons above it and the midbrain on top. The medulla holds the vital centres: the cardiovascular centre, which adjusts heart rate and blood pressure, and the respiratory centre, which generates the basic rhythm of breathing. It also runs reflexes such as swallowing, coughing, sneezing and vomiting. These centres work automatically, so the heart keeps beating and breathing continues even in deep sleep or coma. This is why damage to the medulla is the brain injury most likely to kill, and why overdoses of alcohol, opioids or barbiturates kill mainly by silencing its respiratory centre. The medulla is also where the main motor pathways (the pyramids) cross from one side to the other, so each hemisphere controls the opposite side of the body. For example, if Arjun suffers a severe blow to the base of the skull and swelling presses on the medulla, doctors watch his breathing and pulse most closely, because those functions fail first. Option b is right because the vital centres for heartbeat and respiration lie in the medulla. Exam trap: the pons also helps with breathing, since its centres fine-tune the rhythm, which tempts students to pick c. But the basic, life-sustaining control of heart rate and breathing is the medulla's. The reticular formation runs through the whole brainstem core and governs arousal and alertness, not heart rate as such.", "topic": "Medulla vital centres"}, {"q": "Which structure allows communication between the hemispheres?", "options": {"a": "Reticular formation", "b": "Temporal lobe", "c": "Contralateral bridge", "d": "Corpus callosum"}, "correct": "d", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is d: the corpus callosum is the band of fibres that lets the two cerebral hemispheres communicate. It is the largest commissure (crossing pathway) in the brain, a thick bundle of about 200 million axons of white matter lying in the midline above the thalamus. The smaller anterior commissure also links the two sides. Through the corpus callosum, what one hemisphere perceives or learns is quickly shared with the other. The classic evidence comes from split-brain patients. To control severe epilepsy, surgeons cut the corpus callosum. Roger Sperry and Michael Gazzaniga (1962 onwards) then flashed pictures to only one visual field. When an object was shown to the right hemisphere (left visual field), the patient could not name it, because speech is usually in the left hemisphere, yet his left hand could pick the object out by touch. Each hemisphere was now working on its own. Sperry shared the Nobel Prize in 1981 for this work. For example, if Meera, a split-brain patient, holds a key in her left hand out of sight, she can find it again among other objects with that hand but cannot say 'key', because the information stays in her right hemisphere. Option d is right because the corpus callosum is the main pathway joining and integrating the two hemispheres. Exam trap: 'contralateral bridge' is an invented term built from two real ideas (contralateral means opposite side; pons means bridge) so that it sounds plausible. The temporal lobe is a region inside each hemisphere, and the reticular formation is a brainstem arousal network; neither connects the hemispheres.", "topic": "Corpus callosum and split brain"}, {"q": "__________ is the ability to sense the position and movement of our body parts.", "options": {"a": "Proprio-action", "b": "Proprioception", "c": "Proprioliction", "d": "Proprioprediction"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: proprioception is the sense of the position and movement of our own body parts. The word comes from Latin proprius, 'one's own', plus reception. Charles Sherrington (1906), in The Integrative Action of the Nervous System, coined the term for signals arising from within the body's own tissues. The receptors, called proprioceptors, sit in muscles (muscle spindles, which sense stretch), tendons (Golgi tendon organs, which sense tension) and joints. Their signals travel up the spinal cord in the dorsal column pathway to the thalamus and somatosensory cortex, and also to the cerebellum, which uses them to make movement smooth and accurate. Proprioception is often grouped with kinaesthesis (the sense of movement), while the vestibular sense of the inner ear adds balance and head position. Together they let us act without watching our limbs. For example, Riya can touch her nose with her eyes closed, or climb stairs while reading a message on her phone, because proprioceptors tell her brain where her hand and feet are. The neurologist's finger-to-nose test checks exactly this. People who lose proprioception through nerve damage can still move, but they must watch their limbs constantly to control them. Option b is right because proprioception is the established name for sensing body position and movement from receptors in muscles, tendons and joints. Exam trap: the other three options are invented words built on the same 'proprio-' stem to look familiar. Also do not confuse proprioception with the vestibular sense (balance and head motion from the semicircular canals and otolith organs) or with interoception (sensing the internal state of the organs, such as hunger or a racing heart).", "topic": "Proprioception"}, {"q": "Which of the following is NOT a set of opponent colours according to opponent process theory?", "options": {"a": "Red/blue", "b": "Red/green", "c": "Yellow/blue", "d": "White/black"}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: red and blue are not an opponent pair; the three opponent pairs are red-green, blue-yellow and black-white. Two classic theories explain colour vision. The trichromatic (Young-Helmholtz) theory, from Thomas Young (1802) and Hermann von Helmholtz (1852), says the retina has three receptor types, most sensitive to short (blue), medium (green) and long (red) wavelengths, and that colour is coded by the ratio of their activity. This works at the level of the cones. Ewald Hering (1878) noticed what this could not explain: nobody sees a 'reddish green' or a 'bluish yellow', and staring at red leaves a green afterimage. He proposed opponent processes: three channels, each signalling one of two opposing colours. A cell excited by red is inhibited by green, one excited by blue is inhibited by yellow, and a third codes black versus white (brightness). Russell De Valois (1966) later recorded such opponent cells in the lateral geniculate nucleus of monkeys. Today the two theories are joined in a dual-process view: trichromatic at the cones, opponent coding in later retinal and thalamic cells. For example, if Kabir stares at a red circle for 30 seconds and then looks at a white wall, he sees a green circle, because the fatigued red side of the channel lets its opponent, green, dominate. Option a is right because red's opponent is green, not blue, so red/blue is not one of Hering's pairs. Exam trap: red, green and blue are the three primaries of the trichromatic theory, so students mix up the two theories and pick a primary pair. Remember: trichromatic names three colours (red, green, blue); opponent process names three pairs (red-green, blue-yellow, black-white).", "topic": "Opponent process colour theory"}, {"q": "Which of the following is a Central Nervous System (CNS) depressant?", "options": {"a": "Alcohol", "b": "Nicotine", "c": "LSD", "d": "Caffeine"}, "correct": "a", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is a: alcohol is a central nervous system (CNS) depressant. Psychoactive drugs are grouped by their main effect on the brain: depressants slow neural activity, stimulants speed it up, opioids relieve pain and produce euphoria, and hallucinogens distort perception. Depressants, also called sedative-hypnotics, include alcohol, benzodiazepines (such as diazepam) and barbiturates. Alcohol works mainly by enhancing the inhibitory transmitter GABA at the GABA-A receptor and by blocking excitatory glutamate (NMDA) receptors. At low doses it reduces inhibitions, so people may seem lively and talkative, which is why many people wrongly think it is a stimulant. That early 'lift' is disinhibition: alcohol first depresses the brain's restraining circuits. Higher doses bring slurred speech, poor coordination, slow reactions and memory blackouts, and very high doses bring coma and death by suppressing the medulla's breathing centre. Mixing alcohol with other depressants is especially dangerous, because their effects add up. For example, if Arjun drinks heavily at a wedding and then takes a sleeping pill (a benzodiazepine), both drugs boost GABA, and his breathing can slow to a dangerous level. Option a is right because alcohol slows CNS activity through GABA enhancement, the defining feature of a depressant. Exam trap: the cheerful, talkative phase of drinking makes alcohol look like a stimulant, but it is disinhibition, not stimulation. Caffeine and nicotine are stimulants, and LSD is a hallucinogen. Note also that 'depressant' means slowing the nervous system, not causing clinical depression.", "topic": "Psychoactive drug classes"}, {"q": "_____ waves are to wakefulness as _____ waves are to deep sleep.", "options": {"a": "Alpha, beta", "b": "Beta, delta", "c": "Alpha, delta", "d": "Beta, theta"}, "correct": "c", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is c: alpha waves go with (relaxed) wakefulness and delta waves with deep sleep. The electroencephalogram (EEG) records the brain's electrical activity through electrodes on the scalp. Hans Berger (1929) recorded the first human EEG and named the alpha rhythm, the regular wave seen when a person is awake but relaxed with eyes closed (about 8 to 12 Hz; some texts give 8 to 13). When the eyes open or the person concentrates, alpha gives way to faster, low-amplitude beta waves (about 13 to 30 Hz) of alert, active thinking. As sleep begins (N1), theta waves (4 to 7 Hz) appear; N2 shows sleep spindles and K-complexes; and in N3, the deepest slow-wave sleep, the EEG is dominated by large, slow delta waves (below 4 Hz). REM sleep returns to a fast, low-voltage, waking-like pattern. So the analogy pairs the classic waking rhythm with the classic deep-sleep rhythm. For example, when Meera lies back with her eyes closed before an exam, her EEG shows alpha; an hour into the night, in deep sleep, it shows slow delta waves and she is very hard to wake. Option c is right because alpha is the hallmark rhythm of waking rest and delta is the hallmark of deep (N3) sleep. Exam trap: theta (option d) belongs to drowsiness and light N1 sleep, not deep sleep, and option a pairs two waking rhythms. Key note: beta is also a waking rhythm, so option b (beta, delta) can be argued, since alert wakefulness shows beta. The paper's pairing of alpha, the classic resting waking rhythm that Berger first described, with delta stands.", "topic": "EEG waves and sleep"}, {"q": "The body's primary circadian pacemaker is the:", "options": {"a": "Pineal gland", "b": "Hippocampus", "c": "Suprachiasmatic nucleus", "d": "Amygdala"}, "correct": "c", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is c: the suprachiasmatic nucleus (SCN) is the body's primary circadian pacemaker. A circadian rhythm (Latin circa diem, 'about a day') is a cycle of roughly 24 hours, such as sleep and waking, body temperature and hormone release. The SCN is a tiny pair of nuclei in the hypothalamus, sitting just above the optic chiasm, which gives it its name. Light reaches it directly from the retina along the retinohypothalamic tract, so daylight resets the clock each day; such resetting cues are called zeitgebers ('time givers'). The SCN in turn signals the pineal gland to release melatonin in darkness, which promotes sleep. The evidence is strong. In 1972 two teams (Robert Moore with Victor Eichler, and Friedrich Stephan with Irving Zucker) found that destroying the SCN in rats abolished their daily rhythms: the rats slept the usual total amount, but at random times. Martin Ralph (1990) and colleagues transplanted SCN tissue between hamsters, and the recipients took on the donor's rhythm. Even isolated SCN cells keep firing in a daily cycle, so the clock is built in. For example, when Kabir flies from Delhi to New York, his SCN is still set to Indian time, so he is wide awake at 3 a.m.; jet lag lasts until daylight resets his clock. Option c is right because the SCN generates the master 24-hour rhythm and keeps the body's other clocks in step. Exam trap: the pineal gland is the usual distractor because it secretes melatonin, the 'darkness hormone'. But the pineal only carries out the SCN's timing signal; the pacemaker itself is the SCN.", "topic": "Circadian rhythm and SCN"}, {"q": "What did Freud call the hidden psychological content of a dream?", "options": {"a": "Manifest content", "b": "Literal content", "c": "Latent content", "d": "Soporific content"}, "correct": "c", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is c: Freud called the hidden psychological meaning of a dream its latent content. Sigmund Freud (1900), in The Interpretation of Dreams, called dreams 'the royal road to a knowledge of the unconscious'. He held that every dream is a disguised wish fulfilment. During sleep the ego's guard relaxes and repressed wishes, often sexual or aggressive, press for expression; because they would disturb sleep if shown openly, a mental censor disguises them. Freud therefore split the dream into two layers. The manifest content is the dream as the dreamer remembers and reports it, the surface story. The latent content is the hidden meaning beneath, the unconscious wishes and thoughts. The process that turns latent into manifest is the dream work: condensation (several ideas fused into one image), displacement (emotion shifted from an important object to a trivial one), symbolisation (thoughts shown as concrete images) and secondary revision (tidying the result into a coherent story). The analyst works backwards, using the dreamer's free associations to each element, to uncover the latent content. For example, Riya dreams that she misses a train while her younger brother boards it. The train story is the manifest content; in a Freudian reading the latent content might be her hidden resentment that her brother gets chances she does not. Option c is right because 'latent' means hidden, and Freud used it for the disguised unconscious wish behind the dream. Exam trap: examiners reverse the two terms. Manifest means shown openly (the remembered storyline); latent means lying hidden. 'Literal' and 'soporific' (sleep-inducing) are not Freud's dream categories at all.", "topic": "Freud's dream content"}, {"q": "Which of the following is NOT associated with REM sleep?", "options": {"a": "Decreased limbic system activity", "b": "Increased heart rate", "c": "Genital arousal", "d": "Dreaming"}, "correct": "a", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is a: decreased limbic system activity is not a feature of REM sleep; in REM the limbic system, especially the amygdala, becomes more active. Eugene Aserinsky and Nathaniel Kleitman (1953) noticed bursts of rapid eye movements in sleepers and, with William Dement, linked them to dreaming: people woken from REM usually report vivid, story-like dreams. REM is called paradoxical sleep because the brain looks awake while the body is deeply asleep. Its main signs are rapid eye movements; a fast, low-voltage EEG like waking; faster and irregular heart rate, blood pressure and breathing; genital arousal (penile erection and clitoral engorgement) in almost every REM period, whatever the dream content; and atonia, near-paralysis of the skeletal muscles, which stops us acting out our dreams. Brain imaging (Pierre Maquet and colleagues, 1996) shows that in REM the limbic and paralimbic areas, including the amygdala and anterior cingulate, are highly active, while parts of the dorsolateral prefrontal cortex are quieter. This helps explain why REM dreams are emotional, often fearful, yet illogical and accepted without question. For example, if Arjun is woken during REM, his heart may be racing and he may describe a vivid chase dream; woken from deep N3 sleep, he is groggy and reports little. Option a is right because the limbic system is more, not less, active in REM sleep. Exam trap: students hear 'sleep' and assume everything slows down. That is true of N3 (slow heart rate, regular breathing, delta waves), but REM is a highly activated state. What switches off in REM is the motor output to the muscles, and partly the prefrontal cortex, not the emotional brain.", "topic": "REM sleep features"}, {"q": "_____ parenting combines high responsiveness with low demandingness.", "options": {"a": "Authoritative", "b": "Permissive", "c": "Rejecting-neglecting", "d": "Authoritarian"}, "correct": "b", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is b: permissive parenting combines high responsiveness (warmth) with low demandingness (control). Diana Baumrind (1966, 1971) observed preschool children at home and at nursery school, interviewed their parents, and described three parenting styles. Eleanor Maccoby and John Martin (1983) then placed the styles on two independent dimensions: demandingness (rules, supervision, expecting mature behaviour) and responsiveness (warmth, acceptance, attention to the child's needs). Crossing the two gives four styles. Authoritative parenting is high on both: warm, with clear rules that are explained. Authoritarian parenting is high in demands but low in warmth: strict obedience, 'because I said so', and often harsh punishment. Permissive (indulgent) parenting is high in warmth but low in demands: loving and accepting, with few rules or expectations. Rejecting-neglecting (uninvolved) parenting, added by Maccoby and Martin, is low on both. In many Western samples authoritative parenting predicts the best outcomes (self-reliance, school success, social skill); children of permissive parents tend to be more impulsive, less self-controlled and less persistent, though often confident. For example, Kabir's parents adore him and praise everything he does, but let him skip homework, stay up as late as he likes and eat whatever he wants: that is permissive parenting. Option b is right because high responsiveness with low demandingness is the exact definition of the permissive style. Exam trap: permissive and neglectful parents both set few limits, so students confuse them; the difference is warmth, high in permissive and low in neglectful. Likewise authoritative and authoritarian look alike on the page: authoritative is high on both dimensions, authoritarian is high demand with low warmth.", "topic": "Baumrind's parenting styles"}, {"q": "The \"strange situation\" is a test of:", "options": {"a": "Attachment", "b": "Accommodation", "c": "Assimilation", "d": "Arbitration"}, "correct": "a", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is a: the Strange Situation is a laboratory test of the attachment between an infant and a caregiver. John Bowlby (1969) argued that infants are biologically prepared to form an attachment, a lasting emotional bond, to a caregiver who serves as a secure base. Mary Ainsworth (1969), who had worked with Bowlby, devised the Strange Situation with Barbara Wittig to measure the quality of that bond; the full account came in Patterns of Attachment (1978). A 12 to 18 month old passes through eight short episodes of about three minutes each: exploring a playroom with the caregiver, meeting a stranger, being left with the stranger, a reunion, being left alone, the stranger returning, and a second reunion. Observers score exploration, distress at separation, reaction to the stranger and, above all, behaviour at reunion. Ainsworth found three patterns: secure (type B, about two thirds of her sample: upset when the mother leaves, quickly comforted on her return), insecure-avoidant (type A: little distress, ignores the mother on reunion) and insecure-resistant or ambivalent (type C: very distressed, seeks contact yet angrily resists it). Mary Main and Judith Solomon (1986) added a disorganised pattern (type D). For example, if baby Meera cries when her mother leaves but crawls to her and settles within seconds on reunion, she is classed as securely attached. Option a is right because the procedure was designed specifically to classify the quality of infant attachment. Exam trap: two distractors are Jean Piaget's (1936) terms. Assimilation and accommodation are processes of cognitive adaptation and have nothing to do with the Strange Situation. Also, the Strange Situation is Ainsworth's, not Bowlby's or Harry Harlow's (1958), even though all three studied attachment.", "topic": "Strange Situation (Ainsworth)"}, {"q": "Piaget believed that object permanence is typically attained during which stage?", "options": {"a": "Formal operations", "b": "Concrete operations", "c": "Pre-operational", "d": "Sensorimotor"}, "correct": "d", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is d: Piaget believed object permanence is attained in the sensorimotor stage, from birth to about 2 years. Jean Piaget (1936, 1954) described four stages of cognitive development: sensorimotor (birth to 2), preoperational (2 to 7), concrete operational (7 to 11) and formal operational (11 or 12 onwards). Object permanence is the understanding that objects go on existing when they cannot be seen, heard or touched. Piaget tested it by hiding a toy under a cloth while the infant watched. Before about 8 months, an infant who sees a toy covered acts as if it has vanished ('out of sight, out of mind'). In substage 4 (8 to 12 months) the infant searches for a fully hidden object but makes the A-not-B error: after finding the toy under cloth A several times, she keeps searching at A even after watching it being moved to B. By substage 6 (18 to 24 months), with mental representation, she can follow invisible moves and the concept is complete. Later work, notably Renée Baillargeon's (1987) looking-time studies, found signs of object permanence as early as 3 to 4 months, suggesting Piaget underestimated infants; but the stage he placed it in is still the sensorimotor. For example, at 6 months baby Arjun stops reaching when his rattle is covered with a towel; at 10 months he pulls the towel off to get it. Option d is right because object permanence is the major achievement of Piaget's sensorimotor period. Exam trap: examiners offer preoperational because object permanence is completed near age 2, the boundary. Conservation, not object permanence, is the hallmark of the concrete operational stage, and abstract hypothetical thinking marks formal operations.", "topic": "Piaget's object permanence"}, {"q": "Fluid intelligence tends to _____ with age while crystallized intelligence tends to _____ with age.", "options": {"a": "increase, decrease", "b": "increase, stay the same", "c": "decrease, increase", "d": "decrease, stay the same"}, "correct": "c", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is c: fluid intelligence tends to decrease with age, while crystallised intelligence tends to increase. Raymond Cattell (1963), later with his student John Horn (1966), split general intelligence into two broad abilities. Fluid intelligence (Gf) is the capacity to reason and solve new problems that do not depend on learned knowledge: seeing patterns, abstract reasoning, working out unfamiliar tasks. It is measured by tests such as John Raven's Progressive Matrices (1938). It relies heavily on processing speed and working memory, peaks in early adulthood (around the early twenties) and then declines gradually. Crystallised intelligence (Gc) is the store of knowledge, vocabulary and skills gained through education and experience, measured by vocabulary and general information tests. It keeps rising into late middle age and holds up well into the sixties. In Cattell's investment theory, fluid ability is 'invested' in learning, and what is learned crystallises as Gc. Longitudinal work such as K. Warner Schaie's Seattle Longitudinal Study (begun 1956) broadly supports the pattern, though most declines come later and are smaller than early cross-sectional studies suggested. For example, Meera's 68-year-old grandfather beats her easily at crosswords and knows far more history, but she solves a new puzzle app or a matrix-reasoning test much faster. Option c is right because Gf falls from early adulthood while Gc continues to grow with experience. Exam trap: the pair is easy to reverse; remember 'fluid flows away, crystals build up'. Option d is tempting because Gc does level off and finally dips in very old age, but across most of adulthood it rises.", "topic": "Fluid and crystallised intelligence"}, {"q": "If a proposed intelligence test turned out to really be a test of patience rather than intelligence, it would be criticized for having:", "options": {"a": "Low reliability", "b": "Low construct validity", "c": "Low consistency", "d": "Low consistent validity"}, "correct": "b", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is b: a test that claims to measure intelligence but really measures patience has low construct validity. Validity is the degree to which a test measures what it claims to measure. Lee Cronbach and Paul Meehl (1955) introduced construct validity for tests of abstract qualities (constructs) such as intelligence, anxiety or self-esteem, which cannot be observed directly. A test has construct validity when its scores behave as the theory of the construct predicts: they correlate with other measures of the same construct (convergent validity), do not correlate strongly with measures of different constructs (discriminant validity, Donald Campbell and Donald Fiske, 1959), and differ in expected ways between groups. If an 'intelligence' test really tracks how long people are willing to persist, its scores will correlate with patience measures rather than reasoning measures, so it captures the wrong construct. The test may still be highly reliable, because a patient person will score high every time. Reliability means consistency; validity means the scores mean what they claim. A test can be reliable without being valid, but not valid without being reliable. For example, suppose Kabir designs an 'IQ test' made of long, boring sorting tasks with no time limit, so those who stick at it longest score highest. Scores are stable from week to week, but they reflect persistence, not reasoning: the test is reliable yet lacks construct validity. Option b is right because the test measures a different construct (patience) from the one it claims to measure (intelligence). Exam trap: examiners offer reliability or consistency, which concern whether scores are stable, not what they mean. 'Consistent validity' is not a recognised type of validity; it blends two terms to look familiar.", "topic": "Construct validity"}, {"q": "Which of the following is not a cause of forgetting?", "options": {"a": "Interference", "b": "Decay", "c": "Selective attention", "d": "Retrieval inhibition"}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: selective attention is not a cause of forgetting, because information that was never attended was never stored, so it cannot be forgotten. Forgetting means losing access to information that was once encoded into memory. The main explanations are these. Decay (trace decay) theory says memory traces fade with time and disuse, as in Edward Thorndike's (1914) law of disuse. Interference theory says other learning disrupts recall; John McGeoch (1932) argued that interference, not the mere passing of time, causes most forgetting. In proactive interference old learning blocks new learning; in retroactive interference new learning disrupts old. Retrieval failure, or cue-dependent forgetting (Endel Tulving, 1974), means the memory is stored but the right cue is missing, as in the tip-of-the-tongue state. Retrieval inhibition means recalling some items actively suppresses related ones; Michael Anderson (1994), with Robert and Elizabeth Bjork, called this retrieval-induced forgetting. Motivated forgetting (repression) and failed consolidation complete the list. Selective attention, by contrast, works at the very start, at encoding: it decides what enters memory in the first place, as in Donald Broadbent's (1958) filter model. Unattended material is never learned, so failing to recall it is a failure to encode, not forgetting. For example, if Riya was texting during a lecture and cannot recall its main point, she never encoded it; if she learned it well but cannot recall it a month later because of newer lectures on similar topics, that is interference. Option c is right because selective attention governs what gets encoded, not why stored information is later lost. Exam trap: retrieval inhibition sounds like an attention process, but it is a genuine forgetting mechanism that acts on memories already stored.", "topic": "Theories of forgetting"}, {"q": "In a 3 x 4 contingency table, using Chi-square test, the degrees of freedom associated with the Chi-square will be:", "options": {"a": "12", "b": "9", "c": "7", "d": "6"}, "correct": "d", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is d: a 3 x 4 contingency table has 6 degrees of freedom. The chi-square test, developed by Karl Pearson (1900), compares observed frequencies with the frequencies expected if there were no relationship. In the test of independence, two categorical variables are cross-tabulated in a contingency table, and chi-square asks whether they are associated. Degrees of freedom (df) are the number of values that are free to vary once certain totals are fixed. In a contingency table the row and column totals are fixed, so df = (rows minus 1) x (columns minus 1). Working: (3 minus 1) x (4 minus 1) = 2 x 3 = 6. The logic is that in each row, once all but one cell are filled, the last cell is set by the row total, and in each column the last cell is set by the column total, so only a 2 by 3 block of cells is free. Ronald Fisher (1922) showed that this is the correct df for a contingency table, correcting Pearson's earlier rule. For a goodness-of-fit test on one variable with k categories, df = k minus 1 instead. For example, Arjun classifies 300 students by three streams (arts, commerce, science) and four preferred study times (morning, afternoon, evening, night). His table is 3 x 4, so he looks up the critical chi-square for df = 6, which is 12.59 at the .05 level. Option d is right because (3 minus 1) x (4 minus 1) = 6. Exam trap: 12 (option a) is the number of cells, 3 x 4, not the degrees of freedom. 7 comes from adding rows and columns, and 9 from subtracting one from only one dimension (3 x 3). Always subtract one from each dimension, then multiply.", "topic": "Chi-square degrees of freedom"}, {"q": "Which of the following is classed as a basic emotion?", "options": {"a": "Guilt", "b": "Shame", "c": "Jealousy", "d": "Disgust"}, "correct": "d", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is d: disgust is classed as a basic emotion. Basic emotions are thought to be innate, universal across cultures and quick to arise, each with its own facial expression and bodily pattern. Charles Darwin (1872), in The Expression of the Emotions in Man and Animals, first argued that emotional expressions evolved and are shared across humans and animals. Paul Ekman (1972) and Wallace Friesen tested this by showing photographs of faces to people in many countries, including the Fore of Papua New Guinea, who had little contact with outsiders. Six expressions were recognised everywhere: happiness, sadness, fear, anger, surprise and disgust; contempt was added later. Robert Plutchik (1980) proposed eight primary emotions on a wheel (joy, trust, fear, surprise, sadness, disgust, anger, anticipation). Disgust appears on both lists. It evolved to protect us from contamination and disease, with its wrinkled nose, raised upper lip and urge to pull away from rotten food. Guilt, shame and jealousy are different. They are self-conscious or complex emotions which, as Michael Lewis (1992) described, appear only from the second year of life onwards, once the child has a sense of self and some grasp of social rules, and they lack a single universal facial expression. For example, baby Kabir screws up his face at bitter medicine in his first months (disgust), but shows guilt after breaking a vase only as a toddler who understands rules. Option d is right because disgust is one of Ekman's six universal basic emotions. Exam trap: guilt and shame are strong, common feelings, so they sound basic, but they depend on self-evaluation against social standards, which makes them secondary emotions. Carroll Izard (1977) did list shame and guilt among his fundamental emotions, yet disgust is the only option found on every list.", "topic": "Basic emotions (Ekman)"}, {"q": "Which 3 structures make up the HPA axis?", "options": {"a": "Hypothalamus, pineal gland, anterior gyrus", "b": "Hippocampus, parietal lobe, affective cortex", "c": "Hippocampus, parasympathetic nervous system, adenoids", "d": "Hypothalamus, pituitary, adrenal glands"}, "correct": "d", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is d: the HPA axis is made up of the hypothalamus, the pituitary gland and the adrenal glands. HPA stands for hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal, and it is the body's slower, hormonal stress system. When the brain appraises a threat, the hypothalamus (its paraventricular nucleus) releases corticotropin-releasing hormone (CRH). CRH travels to the anterior pituitary, which releases adrenocorticotropic hormone (ACTH) into the blood. ACTH reaches the adrenal cortex, the outer layer of the adrenal glands above the kidneys, which releases cortisol, the main human stress hormone. Cortisol raises blood sugar, supplies energy and damps down inflammation; it then feeds back on the hypothalamus and pituitary to switch the system off (negative feedback). The faster system is the sympathetic-adrenal-medullary pathway, in which sympathetic nerves make the adrenal medulla release adrenaline within seconds; Walter Cannon (1915) called this the fight-or-flight response. Hans Selye (1936, 1956) described the general adaptation syndrome of alarm, resistance and exhaustion, and the HPA axis sustains the resistance stage. Long-term high cortisol harms health, including the immune system and the hippocampus. For example, during weeks of exam preparation Meera's HPA axis keeps her cortisol high; she copes, but she catches every cold going, because chronic cortisol suppresses immunity. Option d is right because the axis is named after its three linked glands: hypothalamus, pituitary and adrenal. Exam trap: the initials tempt students towards any option starting with H, P and A. Hippocampus, parietal lobe and parasympathetic nervous system begin with the right letters but are not stages of the hormone chain. The hippocampus helps regulate the axis by feedback, but it is not one of its three parts.", "topic": "HPA axis and stress"}, {"q": "According to Eysenck, extraverts seek to _____ their arousal while introverts seek to _____ their arousal.", "options": {"a": "decrease, increase", "b": "hide, reveal", "c": "increase, decrease", "d": "reveal, hide"}, "correct": "c", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is c: according to Hans Eysenck, extraverts seek to increase their arousal, while introverts seek to decrease it. Hans Eysenck (1967), in The Biological Basis of Personality, linked his extraversion-introversion dimension to the brain. He proposed that the ascending reticular activating system (ARAS) in the brainstem sets each person's baseline level of cortical arousal, and that everyone feels and performs best at a moderate, optimal level, an idea close to the Yerkes-Dodson law (Robert Yerkes and John Dodson, 1908). Introverts, Eysenck argued, have a higher resting arousal, so they already sit near or above their optimum; extra stimulation such as noise, crowds or novelty overloads them, and they choose quiet settings to bring arousal down. Extraverts have a lower resting arousal, so they are under-aroused and seek parties, risk, variety and loud music to push it up. The evidence fits: in the lemon juice test, introverts salivate more than extraverts when drops of lemon juice are placed on the tongue, showing stronger physiological reactivity, and introverts are also more sensitive to pain and faint stimuli and condition more easily. Jeffrey Gray (1970) later revised the biology in his reinforcement sensitivity theory. For example, after a long week Arjun, an extravert, recharges at a crowded cricket match, while his introverted friend Kabir recharges by reading alone in a quiet room. Option c is right because extraverts are chronically under-aroused and seek stimulation to raise arousal, while introverts are over-aroused and avoid it. Exam trap: it seems natural to assume extraverts are 'more aroused' because they look lively. In Eysenck's theory the opposite is true: their lively behaviour makes up for low cortical arousal.", "topic": "Eysenck's arousal theory"}, {"q": "The 'reward centre' of brain is located in:", "options": {"a": "Ventral tegmental area", "b": "Hypothalamus", "c": "Nucleus accumbens", "d": "Prefrontal cortex"}, "correct": "c", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is c: the nucleus accumbens, in the ventral striatum, is the structure textbooks call the brain's reward centre. The idea began with James Olds and Peter Milner (1954) at McGill University. They placed electrodes in rats' brains and let the rats press a lever to stimulate themselves. With electrodes in the septal area and along the medial forebrain bundle, rats pressed thousands of times an hour, ignoring food until they were exhausted. Later work traced this effect to the mesolimbic dopamine pathway: dopamine neurons in the ventral tegmental area (VTA) of the midbrain send axons through the medial forebrain bundle to the nucleus accumbens. Food, sex, music and praise all raise dopamine there, and addictive drugs such as cocaine and amphetamine raise it far more, which is why this circuit sits at the heart of theories of addiction. Kent Berridge and Terry Robinson (1998) added that accumbens dopamine signals wanting a reward more than liking it. Example: when Kabir hears the alert sound of his favourite game, dopamine release in his nucleus accumbens rises before he even opens the app, and he feels the urge to check. Option c is right because the nucleus accumbens is the target where reward signals arrive and are turned into motivated action, and it is the structure named the reward or pleasure centre. Exam trap: the VTA and the nucleus accumbens are always named together, so students pick the starting point of the pathway (VTA) instead of its target (nucleus accumbens). Key note: the dopamine neurons start in the VTA, so a is part of the reward system too; exams name the nucleus accumbens as the reward centre, so c stands.", "topic": "Brain reward centre"}, {"q": "The role of the hypothalamus is:", "options": {"a": "It receives peripheral sensory input and relays it to the cerebral cortex", "b": "It provides visceral control of body homeostasis", "c": "It uses past experiences to analyse and act on visual/auditory inputs", "d": "It integrates sensory information from association areas and performs abstract intellectual functions"}, "correct": "b", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is b: the hypothalamus provides visceral control of the body's homeostasis. Homeostasis means keeping the internal environment steady, a term Walter Cannon (1932) popularised in The Wisdom of the Body. The hypothalamus is a small cluster of nuclei in the diencephalon, just below the thalamus, forming the floor and lower walls of the third ventricle. It works as the body's thermostat and control panel through two routes. First, it drives the autonomic nervous system, turning sympathetic and parasympathetic activity up or down. Second, it controls the endocrine system through the pituitary: it releases hormones into the portal blood that steer the anterior pituitary, and its own neurons make oxytocin and vasopressin, which the posterior pituitary stores and releases. Its nuclei regulate body temperature, hunger and satiety (the lateral and ventromedial areas), thirst and water balance, sleep and daily rhythms (the suprachiasmatic nucleus), sexual behaviour and the stress response. Students remember it by the four Fs: feeding, fighting, fleeing and mating. Example: Meera walks home in the Delhi heat; her hypothalamus senses the warmer blood and starts sweating and widening the skin's blood vessels, makes her thirsty, and releases vasopressin so her kidneys save water. Option b is right because every job the hypothalamus does is about keeping visceral (internal organ) states within safe limits. Exam trap: the thalamus and the hypothalamus sit next to each other in the diencephalon, and option a gives the thalamus's job as a sensory relay to catch anyone who mixes up the two names.", "topic": "Hypothalamus and homeostasis"}, {"q": "The direct emotional circuit controlling fear comprises:", "options": {"a": "Eye, thalamus, sensory cortex, hippocampus, prefrontal cortex", "b": "Eye, thalamus, amygdala, hypothalamus", "c": "Eye, hypothalamus, visual cortex, striatum", "d": "Eye, geniculate nucleus, hippocampus, prefrontal cortex"}, "correct": "b", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is b: the direct fear circuit runs eye, thalamus, amygdala, hypothalamus. Joseph LeDoux (1996), in The Emotional Brain, traced two routes by which danger reaches the amygdala, an almond-shaped group of nuclei in the medial temporal lobe. The low road goes from the sensory thalamus straight to the amygdala. It is fast but crude: it carries only a rough sketch of the stimulus, enough to raise the alarm. The high road goes from the thalamus to the sensory cortex and only then to the amygdala. It is slower but detailed, so it can confirm or cancel the alarm. LeDoux showed in rats that fear conditioning to a simple tone survived even after the auditory cortex was removed, because the thalamus-to-amygdala route alone was enough. Signals enter the amygdala's lateral nucleus and leave through its central nucleus to the hypothalamus, which sets off the bodily fear response: the sympathetic surge (racing heart, fast breathing) and the stress hormone cascade, while the periaqueductal grey of the midbrain produces freezing. Example: Kabir walks through a field at dusk and sees a curved shape on the path. He jumps back before he knows what it is (low road); a moment later his visual cortex reports that it is a rope, and he relaxes (high road). Option b is right because it lists the direct, subcortical pathway that bypasses the cortex, eye to thalamus to amygdala, with the hypothalamus producing the physical reaction. Exam trap: options that include the sensory cortex, hippocampus or prefrontal cortex describe the slower, indirect high road and the later regulation of fear, not the direct circuit.", "topic": "LeDoux low road of fear"}, {"q": "A man of 40 years old met with a road traffic accident. Few days later he was having difficulty in execution of plans. He may have sustained injury to:", "options": {"a": "Inferior Frontal Cortex", "b": "Dorsolateral Prefrontal Cortex", "c": "Orbitofrontal Cortex", "d": "Medial Prefrontal Cortex"}, "correct": "b", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is b: difficulty executing plans after a head injury points to damage in the dorsolateral prefrontal cortex. The prefrontal cortex is the front part of the frontal lobe, and its regions do different jobs. The dorsolateral prefrontal cortex (DLPFC), on the upper outer surface, runs the executive functions: setting goals, planning steps, holding information in working memory, switching strategy when a rule changes and checking progress. Damage produces what Alan Baddeley and Barbara Wilson (1988) called the dysexecutive syndrome: the person can often say what needs doing but cannot organise, start, sequence or finish it. Brenda Milner (1963) showed that patients with dorsolateral frontal lesions kept sorting by an old rule on the Wisconsin Card Sorting Test (perseveration), and Tim Shallice (1982) used the Tower of London task to show their planning problems. Other frontal regions give different pictures. Damage to the orbitofrontal cortex, just above the eyes, causes disinhibition, poor social judgement and personality change, as in Phineas Gage, whose tamping-iron injury was described by John Harlow (1848). Damage to the medial prefrontal cortex and anterior cingulate causes apathy and low drive (abulia). The left inferior frontal gyrus holds Broca's area for speech production. In the stem, the man may plan to pay a bill, go to the bank and buy medicines, yet get stuck after the first step or do them in a muddled order, although his memory and speech are fine. Option b is right because planning and carrying out goal-directed sequences is the defining job of the dorsolateral prefrontal cortex. Exam trap: road accidents often bruise the orbitofrontal cortex, so examiners hope you choose it, but orbitofrontal damage changes personality and conduct, not plan execution.", "topic": "Dorsolateral prefrontal executive function"}, {"q": "Damage to the cerebellum would most likely result in:", "options": {"a": "Visuospatial disorientation", "b": "Visual blindness", "c": "Loss of coordination", "d": "Personality changes"}, "correct": "c", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is c: damage to the cerebellum most often causes loss of coordination (ataxia). The cerebellum, or little brain, sits at the back of the hindbrain behind the pons and holds more than half of all the brain's neurons. It does not start movements. It compares the movement the motor cortex intended with feedback from muscles, joints and the inner ear, and corrects errors so that movement is smooth, accurate and well timed. It also keeps balance and posture and helps us learn motor skills. Gordon Holmes (1917) mapped its signs in soldiers with gunshot wounds of the cerebellum in the First World War. The classic signs are ataxia (an unsteady, wide-based, drunken gait), intention tremor (shaking that grows as the hand nears a target), dysmetria (over- or under-shooting, seen on the finger-nose test), dysdiadochokinesia (clumsy rapid alternating movements), nystagmus and slurred, scanning speech. The signs appear on the same side of the body as the damage. Alcohol affects the cerebellum early, which is why police roadside tests ask drivers to walk a straight line. Example: after a stroke in his right cerebellum, Arjun reaches for a glass of water with his right hand, overshoots it, and his hand shakes more the closer it gets. Option c is right because coordinating, timing and correcting movement is the cerebellum's main job, so its damage shows up as clumsy, unsteady action rather than paralysis. Exam trap: resting tremor and slowness belong to the basal ganglia (Parkinson's disease), whereas the cerebellum gives intention tremor. Jeremy Schmahmann (1998) also described a cerebellar cognitive affective syndrome with subtle planning, spatial and mood changes, but the most likely and classic result is still loss of coordination.", "topic": "Cerebellar damage and ataxia"}, {"q": "The statistical method developed by Spearman to assess whether intelligence is underpinned by one or many components is:", "options": {"a": "Multivariate analysis", "b": "Analysis of variance", "c": "Factor analysis", "d": "Cluster analysis"}, "correct": "c", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is c: Charles Spearman (1904) developed factor analysis to test whether intelligence rests on one general ability or many. In his 1904 paper on general intelligence, Spearman noticed that children's marks in very different subjects, such as classics, French, English, mathematics and even pitch discrimination, all correlated positively with one another. This pattern is called the positive manifold. Factor analysis is a statistical method that takes a table of correlations among many tests and asks how few underlying dimensions (factors) can explain them. Spearman's answer was the two-factor theory: every test draws on a general factor, g, shared by all tests, plus a specific factor, s, unique to that test. Tests that correlate most with all the others, such as reasoning tests, have high g loadings. Others expanded his method. Louis Thurstone (1938) used multiple factor analysis and found seven primary mental abilities, such as verbal comprehension, number and spatial ability, challenging a single g. Raymond Cattell (1963) split g into fluid and crystallised intelligence, and John Carroll (1993) combined these ideas into a three-stratum model, the basis of today's CHC theory. Example: Riya scores well in mathematics, Hindi and science, and classmates who do well in one subject tend to do well in the others; Spearman would say they share a high g. Option c is right because factor analysis is the technique built to find the common dimensions behind correlated test scores, which is exactly how Spearman argued for g. Exam trap: Spearman's name is also on Spearman's rank correlation (rho, 1904) and the Spearman-Brown prophecy formula (1910), so examiners swap these ideas in, but the method for the structure of intelligence is factor analysis.", "topic": "Spearman and factor analysis"}, {"q": "Attitudinal scales use different measurement scales which one is not one of them?", "options": {"a": "Ratio Scale", "b": "Nominal Scale", "c": "Interval Scale", "d": "Ordinal Scale"}, "correct": "b", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is b: the nominal scale is not one of the measurement levels used by attitude scales. Stanley Smith Stevens (1946) described four levels of measurement, remembered as NOIR. A nominal scale only names categories with no order (religion, blood group). An ordinal scale ranks things without equal gaps (first, second, third). An interval scale has equal gaps but no true zero (temperature in Celsius). A ratio scale has equal gaps and a true zero (height, reaction time). An attitude scale measures how favourable or unfavourable a person is towards something, so it must show degree, which needs at least order. Louis Thurstone (1928) built the method of equal-appearing intervals, aiming at interval data. Rensis Likert (1932) used summated ratings on a five-point agree to disagree format: each item is ordinal, and the total is usually treated as interval. Louis Guttman (1944) built cumulative scalogram scales, which are ordinal. Charles Osgood (1957) built the semantic differential, seven-point bipolar adjective pairs such as good to bad, treated as interval. Ratio-type methods exist too, such as constant-sum scales, where a person divides 100 points among options. Example: Kabir marks 4 (agree) on the Likert item 'Online classes help me learn'; the number shows how strongly he agrees, which a mere label could never show. Option b is right because a nominal scale cannot express more or less of an attitude; it only sorts people into named boxes. Exam trap: students know attitudes have no true zero and rush to pick ratio, but the question wants the level that cannot show degree at all. Key note: many textbooks say attitude scales give ordinal or interval data and almost never ratio data, because attitudes have no true zero, so a can be argued. The paper's answer b stands: a nominal scale only names categories and cannot show degree of agreement, which is what an attitude scale measures.", "topic": "Measurement levels in attitude scales"}, {"q": "Multi trait multi method matrix design is for analysis of:", "options": {"a": "Concurrent validity", "b": "Predictive validity", "c": "Convergent validity", "d": "Contact validity"}, "correct": "c", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is c: the multitrait-multimethod matrix was designed to analyse convergent validity, together with its partner, discriminant validity. Donald Campbell and Donald Fiske (1959) asked a simple question: does a score reflect the trait, or only the way it was measured? In their design, two or more traits are each measured by two or more methods, such as self-report, peer rating and observation, and every score is correlated with every other score in one large table, the matrix. Monotrait-heteromethod correlations (the same trait measured by different methods) should be high; this is convergent validity, and these values form the validity diagonal. Heterotrait-monomethod correlations (different traits measured by the same method) should be lower; if they are high, the shared method is driving the scores, which is called method variance. Heterotrait-heteromethod correlations should be the lowest of all. Low correlations between different traits show discriminant (divergent) validity. Both kinds of evidence support construct validity, the idea from Lee Cronbach and Paul Meehl (1955) that a test measures the theoretical construct it claims to. Example: a researcher measures Meera's anxiety and her sociability, each by a questionnaire and by a friend's rating. Her two anxiety scores should agree closely (convergent), while her anxiety and sociability scores should not, even when both come from the questionnaire (discriminant). Option c is right because the matrix's central test is whether different methods measuring the same trait converge. Exam trap: concurrent sounds like convergent, and both involve correlating two measures taken at about the same time, but concurrent validity correlates a test with an outside criterion, while convergent validity correlates different measures of the same construct.", "topic": "Multitrait-multimethod matrix"}, {"q": "Cronbach's alpha is a measure of:", "options": {"a": "Interrater consistency", "b": "Internal consistency", "c": "Test-retest reliability", "d": "Construct validity"}, "correct": "b", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is b: Cronbach's alpha measures internal consistency, how well the items of a test hang together in measuring one thing. Lee Cronbach (1951) introduced coefficient alpha. The idea is simple: if all the items of a scale tap the same attribute, a person who scores high on one item should tend to score high on the others. Alpha summarises these inter-item relationships in one number, usually between 0 and 1. It equals the average of all possible split-half coefficients, so it avoids the problem that different ways of splitting a test give different answers. It generalises the Kuder-Richardson formula 20 (Frederic Kuder and Marion Richardson, 1937), which works only for items scored right or wrong, to items with several response points, such as Likert items. Only one administration of the test is needed. Values of about .70 and above are usually treated as acceptable for research, and higher values are expected for clinical decisions about individuals. Alpha rises as items are added, so a long test can have a high alpha even if it measures more than one thing; a high alpha does not prove the scale is unidimensional. Example: Arjun builds a 20-item exam stress questionnaire and gives it once to 200 students; an alpha of .86 tells him the items move together. Option b is right because alpha is calculated from the relationships among items within a single sitting, which is the definition of internal consistency reliability. Exam trap: Cronbach's name is also on the classic construct validity paper with Paul Meehl (1955), so students drift to validity, but alpha is a reliability coefficient.", "topic": "Cronbach's alpha"}, {"q": "People having high cholesterol suffer from more hypertension. Appropriate statistical test utilized here:", "options": {"a": "ANOVA", "b": "T-test", "c": "Pearson's Correlation coefficient", "d": "Descriptive statistics"}, "correct": "c", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is c: Pearson's correlation coefficient is the right test, because the stem is about the relationship between two continuous variables. Francis Galton (1888) introduced the idea of co-relation, and Karl Pearson worked out the product-moment correlation coefficient, r, in the 1890s. Pearson's r gives the direction and strength of a linear relationship between two variables measured on interval or ratio scales. It runs from minus 1 (perfect negative) through 0 (no linear relationship) to plus 1 (perfect positive). Squaring r gives the proportion of variance the two variables share: an r of .60 means 36 percent shared variance. The test assumes a roughly linear relationship, approximately normal data and no extreme outliers; when these fail, Spearman's rank correlation is used instead. Correlation never proves cause: cholesterol might raise blood pressure, or both might come from a third factor such as diet. Example: Riya, a health psychology researcher in Pune, records the cholesterol level and systolic blood pressure of 150 adults. A Pearson's r of .45 would show that people with higher cholesterol tend to have higher blood pressure. Option c is right because the stem links the amount of one measured variable (cholesterol) to the amount of another (blood pressure), and Pearson's r is the standard test of association between two such measures. Exam trap: the word 'suffer' tempts students to see two groups (hypertensive or not) and pick a t-test, but the stem asks whether the two variables vary together. Key note: if hypertension were coded only as present or absent, you would compare cholesterol between the two groups (t-test) or use a point-biserial correlation; with cholesterol level and blood pressure both measured on continuous scales, Pearson's r is the test of association, so c stands.", "topic": "Choosing Pearson's correlation"}, {"q": "The Flynn effect is:", "options": {"a": "The average IQ score of people is slowly but steadily going down over time", "b": "The average IQ score of people is slowly but steadily going up over time", "c": "An individual's IQ score can be used to predict his/her future performance", "d": "People with high IQ scores have been shown to be happier than people with low IQ scores"}, "correct": "b", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is b: the Flynn effect is the slow but steady rise in average IQ test scores over time. James Flynn (1984) showed that Americans gained about 3 IQ points per decade between 1932 and 1978, and in 1987 he reported similar or larger gains in 14 nations. Because IQ tests are normed so that the average is 100 at the time of standardisation, a rising population makes old norms too easy: a person tested on norms 20 years old scores about 6 points higher than on fresh norms. The gains are largest on tests of fluid, abstract reasoning such as Raven's Progressive Matrices, and smaller on vocabulary and general knowledge. They are far too fast to be genetic. Suggested causes include better nutrition and health, more and longer schooling, smaller families, a more cognitively complex environment and growing familiarity with test-like thinking. The effect has practical weight: publishers must re-norm tests regularly, and an outdated test can lift a child above the cut-off for intellectual disability. Since about the 1990s some countries, such as Norway and Denmark, have shown a small decline, called the negative or reverse Flynn effect. Example: Kabir takes an intelligence test whose norms were set 20 years ago and scores 108; on a newly normed version he might score about 102. Option b is right because it states exactly what Flynn documented: a gradual, generation-by-generation increase in average scores. Exam trap: examiners reverse the direction in option a, which describes the recent reverse Flynn effect in a few countries, not the Flynn effect itself.", "topic": "Flynn effect"}, {"q": "Hold subtests of the Wechsler Intelligence Scale are the following except:", "options": {"a": "Object Assembly", "b": "Vocabulary", "c": "Picture Completion", "d": "Digit Span"}, "correct": "d", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is d: Digit Span is a don't-hold subtest, so it is the exception among the hold subtests. David Wechsler (1939) built the Wechsler-Bellevue scale and noticed that subtests age differently. Hold subtests resist ageing and brain damage because they draw on old, overlearned knowledge and recognition: Vocabulary, Information, Object Assembly and Picture Completion. Don't-hold subtests decline because they need speed, sustained attention, working memory and new problem solving: Digit Span, Digit Symbol (Coding), Similarities and Block Design. In the terms of Raymond Cattell (1963), hold tests lean on crystallised ability and don't-hold tests on fluid ability. Wechsler used the gap to estimate loss: the deterioration index (or quotient) is the hold score minus the don't-hold score, divided by the hold score. A large gap suggests decline beyond normal ageing, for example from dementia, brain injury or chronic psychiatric illness. Clinicians today also estimate premorbid ability with reading tests such as the National Adult Reading Test (Hazel Nelson, 1982), but the principle is the same, and Vocabulary is the best single hold test. Example: Meera, a retired teacher of 72, still defines words and answers general knowledge questions well, but repeats only short strings of digits and works slowly on Coding; her hold scores are high and her don't-hold scores have dropped. Option d is right because repeating digits forwards and backwards depends on attention and working memory, among the first abilities to fall with age and brain damage. Exam trap: Object Assembly and Picture Completion are performance subtests, so students assume they must decline, while Similarities looks verbal and seems safe; in Wechsler's lists the first two hold and Similarities does not.", "topic": "Wechsler hold and don't-hold subtests"}, {"q": "All are clinical scales of MMPI-II except:", "options": {"a": "Schizophrenia", "b": "Mania", "c": "Psychopathic deviate", "d": "Psychasthenia"}, "correct": "b", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is b: Mania is not an MMPI-2 clinical scale; scale 9 is Hypomania (Ma). The Minnesota Multiphasic Personality Inventory was built by Starke Hathaway and J. Charnley McKinley (1943) at the University of Minnesota by empirical criterion keying: an item was kept if patients with a given diagnosis answered it differently from normal adults, whatever its content. The revised MMPI-2 (1989) has 567 true or false items and keeps the same 10 clinical scales: 1 Hypochondriasis (Hs), 2 Depression (D), 3 Hysteria (Hy), 4 Psychopathic Deviate (Pd), 5 Masculinity-Femininity (Mf), 6 Paranoia (Pa), 7 Psychasthenia (Pt), 8 Schizophrenia (Sc), 9 Hypomania (Ma) and 0 Social Introversion (Si). Validity scales such as L (lie), F (infrequency) and K (defensiveness) check how the person approached the test. Raw scores become T scores, and on the MMPI-2 a T of 65 or more is clinically raised. Clinicians read the code type, the two highest scales together. Several names are old diagnostic labels kept for history: Psychasthenia was the term of Pierre Janet (1903) for what we now call obsessive-compulsive and anxiety problems. Example: Arjun's MMPI-2 profile peaks on scales 2 and 7, a 2-7 code type that suggests an anxious, worried depression. Option b is right because the ninth scale was built from patients in milder, hypomanic states and is named Hypomania, not Mania. Exam trap: the abbreviation Ma looks like Mania, and examiners rely on that; the scale measures overactivity, excitement and flight of ideas at a hypomanic level.", "topic": "MMPI-2 clinical scales"}, {"q": "Test of Frontal Lobe Function:", "options": {"a": "Bender Gestalt Test", "b": "Wisconsin Card Sorting Test", "c": "Nahor Benson Test", "d": "Albert Test"}, "correct": "b", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is b: the Wisconsin Card Sorting Test (WCST) is the classic test of frontal lobe function. David Grant and Esta Berg (1948) designed it. The person sees four stimulus cards that differ in the colour, shape and number of their symbols, and must sort a deck of response cards to match them. The examiner never states the rule and only says right or wrong after each card. After a run of correct sorts the rule changes without warning, say from colour to shape, and the person must notice the feedback and shift. The test therefore measures abstract reasoning, concept formation, use of feedback and, above all, set shifting (cognitive flexibility). Its main error is the perseverative error: continuing to sort by the old rule despite being told it is wrong. Brenda Milner (1963) showed that patients with dorsolateral frontal lesions made many more perseverative errors than patients with damage elsewhere, which made the WCST the standard executive function test; Robert Heaton (1981) later published its scoring manual. Example: after a frontal head injury, Kabir keeps sorting by colour for many trials after the rule has changed to shape, even while saying he knows he is getting them wrong. Option b is right because the WCST taps planning, flexibility and rule shifting, the executive functions of the dorsolateral prefrontal cortex. Exam trap: the Bender Gestalt is the most famous organicity test, so students pick it, but it is a general visuomotor copying test and does not single out the frontal lobes.", "topic": "Frontal lobe tests: WCST"}, {"q": "From the Big Five personality dimensions, behaviours such as high level of discipline, methodical planning, perseverance is:", "options": {"a": "Agreeableness", "b": "Openness", "c": "Extraversion", "d": "Conscientiousness"}, "correct": "d", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is d: discipline, methodical planning and perseverance are the marks of conscientiousness. The Big Five, or Five-Factor Model, grew from the lexical approach: if a trait matters, everyday language will have words for it. Ernest Tupes and Raymond Christal (1961) found five recurring factors in trait ratings, Warren Norman (1963) confirmed them, Lewis Goldberg (1981) named them the Big Five, and Paul Costa and Robert McCrae measured them with the NEO Personality Inventory, revised as the NEO-PI-R in 1992. The five are remembered as OCEAN: Openness, Conscientiousness, Extraversion, Agreeableness and Neuroticism. Conscientiousness is the tendency to be organised, dependable, self-disciplined, dutiful and achievement-oriented; the low end is careless and disorganised. Its six NEO-PI-R facets are competence, order, dutifulness, achievement striving, self-discipline and deliberation (thinking before acting). Murray Barrick and Michael Mount (1991) showed in a meta-analysis that conscientiousness is the Big Five trait that best predicts job performance across all kinds of jobs, and it also predicts better health habits and a longer life. Example: Riya, preparing for her entrance exam, makes a weekly timetable, revises every evening even when friends go out, and keeps going after a poor mock score; she is high in conscientiousness. Option d is right because each behaviour in the stem, discipline (self-discipline), methodical planning (order and deliberation) and perseverance (achievement striving), is a facet of conscientiousness. Exam trap: perseverance makes some students think of grit (Angela Duckworth, 2007) or of low neuroticism, but within the Big Five it belongs to conscientiousness.", "topic": "Big Five: conscientiousness"}, {"q": "The following statements are true of illness behaviour, except:", "options": {"a": "It can be described in terms of stages", "b": "It's culturally determined", "c": "Contact with a doctor is necessary to legitimise illness", "d": "It can be equated with learned helplessness"}, "correct": "c", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is c: the claim that contact with a doctor is necessary to legitimise illness is not true of illness behaviour. David Mechanic (1962) defined illness behaviour as the ways people perceive, evaluate and act (or fail to act) on bodily symptoms. It covers noticing a symptom, deciding what it means, talking to family, trying home remedies, resting, taking leave and, only sometimes, seeing a doctor. Most symptoms never reach a doctor at all, so illness behaviour begins before, and often without, any medical contact. Edward Suchman (1965) divided it into five stages: symptom experience, assumption of the sick role, medical care contact, the dependent-patient role, and recovery or rehabilitation. Culture shapes every stage. Mark Zborowski (1952) found in a New York hospital that patients of Jewish and Italian background expressed pain openly, while Irish and Old American patients were stoical. Illness behaviour can also turn abnormal, as when a person stays passive and dependent long after recovery, which some writers liken to learned helplessness (Martin Seligman, 1967). Example: Arjun wakes with a fever, decides it is only the weather, takes a home remedy his mother suggests and stays home from college; he is already showing illness behaviour, with no doctor involved. Option c is right because illness behaviour is defined by how the person responds to symptoms, not by a doctor's confirmation. Exam trap: the sick role of Talcott Parsons (1951) makes the doctor the gatekeeper who legitimises sickness, and students mistake that role for illness behaviour itself. Key note: Talcott Parsons' sick role (1951) does make the doctor the one who legitimises sickness, which is why c is tempting to call true; and d (learned helplessness, Martin Seligman, 1967) is also not a standard description of illness behaviour, so d can be argued. The paper's answer c stands.", "topic": "Illness behaviour"}, {"q": "Mrs. A was found missing for 10 days. She was found in a village 500 kilometres away from her native and was completely unaware of her previous identity. She was working there as a field worker. She is probably suffering from:", "options": {"a": "Dissociative amnesia", "b": "Dissociative fugue", "c": "Dissociative-Identity Disorder", "d": "Factitious disorder"}, "correct": "b", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is b: Mrs. A has dissociative fugue, sudden travel away from home with loss of memory for her identity and the start of a new life. Fugue comes from the Latin fugere, to flee. In the classic description (DSM-IV, and ICD-10 F44.1), the person suddenly and unexpectedly leaves home or work, travels purposefully, cannot recall her past, and is confused about who she is or takes on a partly or wholly new identity. During the fugue she looks normal to strangers; she may take a job, rent a room and talk sensibly. Fugues usually follow severe stress such as a family crisis, debt, war or a threat, and they are rare. They often end suddenly: the person wakes up, as it were, in a strange place, recovers her old identity, and then has amnesia for the fugue period itself. Treatment offers a safe setting, gentle psychotherapy to recover memories and attention to the stress behind the flight. Each part of the stem fits: missing for 10 days, found 500 kilometres away, unaware of her previous identity, and working as a field worker in a new life. Option b is right because purposeful travel plus loss of personal identity plus a new role is the defining picture of fugue, which goes beyond the memory gap of simple amnesia. Exam trap: dissociative identity disorder also involves identity, but there several personality states alternate again and again, whereas a fugue is a single episode of flight with one forgotten identity. Key note: DSM-5 (2013) made dissociative fugue a specifier of dissociative amnesia ('with dissociative fugue'), so a is close; ICD-10 (F44.1) and the classic texts keep fugue as its own diagnosis, and the travel plus new identity is the fugue picture, so b stands.", "topic": "Dissociative fugue"}, {"q": "Most common type of obsession:", "options": {"a": "Blasphemous", "b": "Sexual", "c": "Contamination", "d": "Doubts"}, "correct": "c", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is c: fear of contamination is the most common obsession in obsessive-compulsive disorder (OCD). An obsession is a recurrent, intrusive, unwanted thought, image or urge that causes marked anxiety; a compulsion is a repetitive act or mental ritual done to reduce that anxiety. Steven Rasmussen and Jane Eisen (1992) catalogued symptoms in a large clinic sample and found contamination obsessions in about half of patients: fear of germs, dirt, bodily fluids, chemicals or disease. Pathological doubt came next (did I lock the door, did I hurt someone?), followed by somatic obsessions, the need for symmetry, aggressive obsessions and sexual obsessions; religious (blasphemous) obsessions were less frequent. Most patients have more than one type. Contamination obsessions usually pair with washing and cleaning compulsions and with avoidance of dirty objects. The most common compulsion, however, is checking, which pairs with pathological doubt. Treatment is exposure and response prevention, pioneered by Victor Meyer (1966), along with selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors. Example: Meera, a 24-year-old student, cannot touch door handles or the bus rail without feeling dirty; she washes her hands 40 times a day until her skin cracks, and feels relief for only a few minutes. Option c is right because contamination tops the list of obsession types in large clinical surveys. Exam trap: examiners separate obsessions from compulsions; the most common obsession is contamination, but the most common compulsion is checking, and doubt is the second most common obsession, so d tempts students who mix up the two lists.", "topic": "Most common OCD obsession"}, {"q": "La belle indifference is seen in:", "options": {"a": "Conversion disorder", "b": "Borderline-personality disorder", "c": "Depression", "d": "OCD"}, "correct": "a", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is a: la belle indifference is classically linked with conversion disorder. The French phrase means beautiful indifference: a surprising lack of concern about a symptom that looks serious, such as paralysis, blindness or loss of speech. Pierre Janet, writing on hysteria in the 1890s, described it, and the classic texts treated it as a sign that the symptom solved an inner conflict, giving primary gain (relief from anxiety) and secondary gain (attention, escape from duties). Conversion disorder, called functional neurological symptom disorder in DSM-5 (2013), means altered motor or sensory function, such as weakness, abnormal movements, non-epileptic seizures or numbness, that clinical findings show to be incompatible with recognised neurological disease. Hoover's sign, where a 'paralysed' leg pushes down when the other leg is lifted, is one such finding. DSM-5 warns that la belle indifference is not specific to conversion disorder and should not be used to make the diagnosis: a systematic review by Jon Stone and colleagues (2006) found it about as often in patients with real organic disease, and many conversion patients are visibly distressed. Example: Riya, 19, wakes on the morning of her board exams unable to move her legs; in the ward she chats and smiles calmly while her parents are frantic. Option a is right because la belle indifference is the textbook feature of conversion (hysterical) symptoms, even though modern criteria no longer rely on it. Exam trap: la belle indifference is not the flat or blunted affect of schizophrenia; it is a selective calm about one dramatic symptom in a person who is otherwise emotionally responsive.", "topic": "La belle indifference"}, {"q": "Pseudo-dementia is a feature of:", "options": {"a": "Alcoholism", "b": "Depression", "c": "Alzheimer's disease", "d": "Seizure disorder"}, "correct": "b", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is b: pseudodementia is a feature of depression, especially in older adults. Leslie Kiloh (1961) popularised the term for cognitive impairment that looks like dementia but has a psychiatric cause, most often depression; it is also called depressive pseudodementia or the dementia syndrome of depression. Depression slows thinking, saps effort and disturbs attention, so the person does badly on memory and concentration tests. Several clues separate it from true dementia. The onset is fairly quick and can be dated, often after a loss, and low mood appears before or with the memory problems. The person complains loudly about poor memory, highlights failures, gives up easily, answers 'I don't know' and performs unevenly from task to task. In early Alzheimer's disease the onset is slow, the person often hides or plays down the deficits, tries hard, gives near-miss answers or confabulates, and mood problems come later. Most importantly, pseudodementia improves when the depression is treated, although some patients later develop a true dementia, so follow-up matters. Example: Ramesh, 68, became forgetful and withdrawn within weeks of his wife's death; he tells the doctor 'my memory is finished' and says 'I don't know' to simple questions, and after three months of treatment for depression his test scores return to normal. Option b is right because depression is the classic and most common cause of reversible, dementia-like impairment. Exam trap: Ganser syndrome (Sigbert Ganser, 1898), with its approximate answers, was once called hysterical pseudodementia, but in exams pseudodementia means depression.", "topic": "Depressive pseudodementia"}, {"q": "The following personality disorder is characterized by marked impulsive, hostile, manipulative behaviours without any feeling of guilt or remorse for such behaviours:", "options": {"a": "Histrionic personality", "b": "Narcissistic personality", "c": "Borderline personality", "d": "Dissocial personality"}, "correct": "d", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is d: dissocial personality disorder is marked by impulsive, hostile and manipulative behaviour without guilt or remorse. Dissocial personality disorder is the ICD-10 name (F60.2); DSM-5 calls the same pattern antisocial personality disorder. ICD-10 describes callous unconcern for the feelings of others, gross and lasting irresponsibility and disregard for social rules, inability to keep lasting relationships, very low tolerance of frustration and a low threshold for aggression and violence, inability to feel guilt or learn from punishment, and a marked tendency to blame others. DSM-5 requires a pervasive disregard for and violation of the rights of others since age 15, shown by deceit, impulsivity, irritability and aggression, reckless disregard for safety, irresponsibility and lack of remorse; the person must be at least 18 and must have shown conduct disorder before 15. Hervey Cleckley (1941), in The Mask of Sanity, described the related psychopath, superficially charming but without remorse or shame, and Robert Hare (1980) built the Psychopathy Checklist, revised in 1991, to measure psychopathy. Most psychopaths meet criteria for antisocial personality disorder, but many people with antisocial personality disorder are not psychopaths. Example: Kabir, 26, borrows money from friends with invented stories, gets into fights when crossed, and when caught says, 'They were fools to trust me'. Option d is right because the stem's mix of impulsivity, hostility, manipulation and absent guilt is the core of dissocial (antisocial) personality disorder. Exam trap: dissocial sounds like dissociative, but it has nothing to do with dissociation; it means acting against society, that is, antisocial.", "topic": "Dissocial (antisocial) personality"}, {"q": "Piaget's stages of play was:", "options": {"a": "Exercise play/language play/games with rules", "b": "Constructive play/rough-and-tumble play/games with rules", "c": "Exercise play/language play/Symbolic play", "d": "Functional play/Symbolic play/games with rules"}, "correct": "d", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is d: Jean Piaget (1951) described three stages of play: functional play, symbolic play and games with rules. In Play, Dreams and Imitation in Childhood, Piaget treated play as assimilation, the child bending reality to fit the schemes it already has, so the form of play follows the stage of cognitive development. Functional play (also called practice, exercise or sensorimotor play) comes first, in the sensorimotor stage of infancy: simple actions repeated for the pleasure of mastering them, such as shaking a rattle, banging a spoon or dropping toys again and again. Symbolic play (pretend play) appears with the symbolic function of the preoperational stage, from about 2 to 7 years: one thing stands for another, as when a banana becomes a phone or a doll is fed. Games with rules dominate the concrete operational stage, from about 7 to 11 years, when children can agree on rules in advance, follow them and judge fairness: kho-kho, ludo, gully cricket. Sara Smilansky (1968) later added constructive play, building or making things. Example: at one year Meera bangs a steel spoon on a plate for the joy of the noise; at three she serves imaginary tea to her teddy; at eight she argues over the rules of ludo with her cousins. Option d is right because it lists Piaget's three forms in his own order, matched to the sensorimotor, preoperational and concrete operational stages. Exam trap: Mildred Parten (1932) described social stages of play (such as solitary, parallel, associative and cooperative play), which examiners mix with Piaget's cognitive stages of play.", "topic": "Piaget's stages of play"}, {"q": "What is narrative analysis?", "options": {"a": "A literary approach to documents", "b": "An approach that is sensitive to questions that concern how people choose to sequence and represent people and events", "c": "A form of thematic analysis", "d": "A method of improving the quality of interview material"}, "correct": "b", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is b: narrative analysis is an approach that is sensitive to how people choose to sequence and represent people and events in the stories they tell. Catherine Kohler Riessman (1993), in Narrative Analysis, set out the method, and the stem is close to the definition in Social Research Methods by Alan Bryman (2001). People make sense of their lives through stories: they pick events, put them in order, give them a beginning, a turning point and an ending, cast themselves and others as characters, and tell the story for a purpose and an audience. Narrative analysis keeps each story whole rather than cutting it into coded fragments, and asks why the story is told this way. Researchers look at what is told (thematic narrative analysis), how it is told (structural analysis) and to whom and why (dialogic or performance analysis). William Labov and Joshua Waletzky (1967) gave a well-known structural model: abstract, orientation, complicating action, evaluation, resolution and coda. Example: Arjun, recovering from depression, tells his story as 'everything was fine until my father fell ill, I fell apart in second year, and therapy was the turning point'. A narrative analyst studies why he starts with his father, what he leaves out, and how he presents himself as someone who recovered. Option b is right because attention to sequence and representation, how events and people are ordered and portrayed, is the defining feature of narrative analysis. Exam trap: thematic analysis (Virginia Braun and Victoria Clarke, 2006) looks for themes across many accounts and breaks stories into codes, the opposite of keeping each story intact; the fact that narrative analysis has a thematic variant is what makes option c tempting.", "topic": "Narrative analysis"}, {"q": "Which of these supports the James-Lange theory of emotion?", "options": {"a": "Emotion is a visceral response producing a behavioural response", "b": "Artificial induction of visceral changes does not necessarily produce emotion", "c": "The viscera are 'Insensitive structures'", "d": "Visceral changes are the same in many emotions"}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: the statement that emotion is a visceral response producing a behavioural response supports the James-Lange theory. William James (1884), in his essay 'What is an emotion?', and the Danish physician Carl Lange (1885) independently proposed that the bodily change comes first and the feeling follows. The sequence is a stimulus, then automatic visceral and muscular changes (racing heart, trembling, running), and then the brain's perception of those changes, which is the emotion. In James's words, we feel sorry because we cry, angry because we strike, afraid because we tremble. Walter Cannon (1927) attacked the theory with five criticisms: cutting the viscera off from the brain does not abolish emotional behaviour; the same visceral changes occur in very different emotions and in non-emotional states such as fever; the viscera are relatively insensitive structures; visceral changes are too slow to produce feelings; and artificially inducing visceral changes, for example with adrenaline injections (Gregorio Marañón, 1924), produces only 'as if' feelings rather than real emotion. Cannon and Philip Bard (1928) proposed instead that the thalamus signals the cortex and the body at the same time. Modern support for the body-first idea comes from facial feedback studies and the somatic marker hypothesis of Antonio Damasio (1994). Example: a stray dog charges at Kabir; his heart pounds and he runs, and only then does he feel afraid, as he notices his body's panic. Option a is right because it puts the visceral response first and treats emotion as arising from that bodily reaction, the heart of James-Lange. Exam trap: options b, c and d are all true statements, but they are Cannon's criticisms, which support Cannon-Bard and argue against James-Lange.", "topic": "James-Lange theory of emotion"}, {"q": "A cognitive process that starts with simple processes and builds to the more-complex higher levels is known as:", "options": {"a": "Lateral processing", "b": "Internal processing", "c": "Bottom-up processing", "d": "Top-down processing"}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: bottom-up processing starts with simple sensory features and builds up to complex, higher-level interpretations. It is also called data-driven processing, because information flows upward from the senses. Receptors pick up raw features such as lines, edges, angles, colours and movement; these are combined into shapes, the shapes into objects, and finally the object is recognised. David Hubel and Torsten Wiesel (1959) supported this idea when they found feature detector cells in the cat's visual cortex that fired to lines at particular angles. The Pandemonium model of Oliver Selfridge (1959) and the recognition-by-components theory of Irving Biederman (1987), which builds objects from simple 3D shapes called geons, are bottom-up models, and the direct perception of James Gibson (1979) is the classic bottom-up theory of perception. Top-down processing runs the other way: it is conceptually driven, using knowledge, expectation and context to interpret input, as in the view of Richard Gregory (1966) that a percept is a best-guess hypothesis, or in perceptual set. Real perception uses both at once. Example: Arjun copies an unfamiliar Tamil word from the board line by line and curve by curve, because he cannot read the script; that is bottom-up. His classmate Meera, who reads Tamil, takes in the same word at a glance even when a letter is smudged, because her knowledge fills the gap; that adds top-down processing. Option c is right because the stem describes the direction of bottom-up processing exactly: simple units first, complex meaning last. Exam trap: examiners swap the direction; top-down processing starts from knowledge at the higher level and works down to the details, so students who remember only that the two are opposites can pick d by mistake.", "topic": "Bottom-up processing"}, {"q": "The phenomenon whereby an individual in a group will tend to end up agreeing with a strong majority opinion despite their own judgement is known as:", "options": {"a": "Normative influence", "b": "Informative influence", "c": "Critical mass influence", "d": "Political influence"}, "correct": "a", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is a: going along with a strong majority against your own judgement is normative influence. Morton Deutsch and Harold Gerard (1955) split social influence into two kinds. Normative influence is conformity driven by the wish to be liked and accepted and the fear of ridicule or rejection; it usually produces public compliance, where the person gives the group's answer while privately disagreeing. Informational influence is conformity driven by the wish to be right: when a situation is ambiguous, other people are taken as evidence about reality, and this produces private acceptance. The classic evidence is Solomon Asch (1951, 1956). Students judged which of three lines matched a standard line, a task so easy that people tested alone erred on under 1 percent of judgements. With a group of confederates who all gave the same wrong answer, participants conformed on about 37 percent of critical trials, and about three quarters conformed at least once. When participants could write answers privately, conformity fell sharply, which shows the pressure was social, not a change in what they saw. Contrast Muzafer Sherif (1935): in the dark room of the autokinetic effect a still light seems to move, nobody knows the true answer, and people converge on a group norm through informational influence. Example: Arjun finds a film boring, but when all six hostel friends praise it he says it was 'great' to avoid standing out. Option a is right because the stem stresses a strong majority overriding the person's own judgement, which is Asch's normative pressure, not uncertainty. Exam trap: examiners swap the two; informational influence needs ambiguity and changes private belief, while normative influence works even when the answer is obvious and changes only public behaviour.", "topic": "Normative social influence"}, {"q": "Collectivist cultures are characterized by members:", "options": {"a": "Developing a strong sense of self", "b": "Giving priority to group goals", "c": "Discovering how they are different from the group", "d": "Achieving personal goals"}, "correct": "b", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is b: members of collectivist cultures give priority to group goals over personal goals. Geert Hofstede (1980) surveyed IBM employees in 40 countries and found that cultures differ on a few measurable dimensions; one of them is individualism versus collectivism. In individualist cultures (the United States, Britain, Australia) the self is defined as an independent 'I', people are expected to look after themselves, and personal goals, rights and uniqueness come first. In collectivist cultures (much of Asia, Africa and Latin America) the self is defined by the 'we' of family, community or workplace; loyalty, duty, harmony and fitting in are prized, and when personal and group goals clash the group usually wins. Harry Triandis (1995) refined the idea, and Hazel Markus and Shinobu Kitayama (1991) described the matching self-construals: independent in individualist cultures, interdependent in collectivist ones. The difference shows up in research too: Rod Bond and Peter Smith (1996), pooling 133 Asch-type conformity studies, found more conformity in collectivist countries. Example: Meera wins a seat at a college in another city but chooses one near home, because her family needs her help with a younger brother and her parents' wishes weigh more than her own preference. India is usually described as leaning collectivist, though with strong individualist streaks in cities. Option b is right because putting group goals first is the defining feature of collectivism in Hofstede's and Triandis's accounts. Exam trap: the three wrong options all describe individualism (an independent self, uniqueness, personal achievement), so spotting the odd one out points to b; do not confuse collectivism with Hofstede's separate power distance dimension, which is about accepting unequal power.", "topic": "Individualism versus collectivism"}, {"q": "NDPS Act is associated with:", "options": {"a": "Substances abuse", "b": "Child abuse", "c": "Testamentary Capacity", "d": "Disability in schizophrenia"}, "correct": "a", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is a: the NDPS Act deals with substance abuse. The Narcotic Drugs and Psychotropic Substances Act, 1985 is India's central law on drugs of abuse. It came into force in November 1985, replaced older laws such as the Opium Acts and the Dangerous Drugs Act, 1930, and brought India in line with the United Nations conventions on narcotic drugs (1961) and psychotropic substances (1971). It prohibits the cultivation, production, possession, sale, purchase, transport and use of narcotic drugs (opium, cannabis, coca and products such as heroin and cocaine) and psychotropic substances (certain sedatives and stimulants), except for medical or scientific purposes. Since the 2001 amendment, punishment is graded by the quantity involved: small, intermediate or commercial. The Narcotics Control Bureau was set up under it in 1986. For clinical psychologists the treatment provisions matter most: section 64A gives immunity from prosecution to a person charged with consumption, or with a small quantity, who volunteers for de-addiction treatment at a government or recognised centre and completes it, and the government may set up centres to identify, treat and rehabilitate addicts. The 2014 amendment eased access to essential narcotic drugs such as morphine for pain relief. Example: Kabir, a college student, is caught with a small amount of ganja; if he seeks and completes treatment at a recognised de-addiction centre, he can avoid prosecution. Option a is right because the whole purpose of the Act is controlling drugs of abuse and dealing with the people who use them. Exam trap: match each Indian law to its subject: NDPS Act 1985 (drugs), POCSO Act 2012 (child sexual abuse), Rights of Persons with Disabilities Act 2016 (disability), Mental Healthcare Act 2017 (rights and treatment of persons with mental illness).", "topic": "NDPS Act 1985"}, {"q": "Who has formulated the stages of grief?", "options": {"a": "Rollo May", "b": "Elisabeth Kubler-Ross", "c": "Carol Gilligan", "d": "Lawrence Kohlberg"}, "correct": "b", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is b: Elisabeth Kübler-Ross formulated the stages of grief. Elisabeth Kübler-Ross (1969), a Swiss-born psychiatrist working in the United States, interviewed terminally ill patients for her book On Death and Dying and described five stages a person may pass through on learning they are dying: denial ('this cannot be happening to me'), anger ('why me?'), bargaining ('let me live until my son's wedding'), depression (sadness and withdrawal as losses mount) and acceptance (a calm, often tired, coming to terms). The mnemonic is DABDA. She herself said the stages are not a fixed ladder; people move back and forth, skip stages or hold several at once, and later research found little support for a strict sequence. The model was first about the dying and was later stretched to the bereaved and to any major loss. Other models of loss: John Bowlby (1980) described four phases of mourning in the bereaved (numbness, yearning and searching, disorganisation and despair, reorganisation); William Worden (1982) set out four tasks of mourning; and Margaret Stroebe and Henk Schut (1999) proposed the dual process model, in which the griever oscillates between loss-oriented and restoration-oriented coping. Example: when Meera's grandmother is told she has advanced cancer, she first insists the reports are wrong, then is angry with her doctors, then prays for more time, then grows quiet and sad, and finally plans calmly for her family. Option b is right because the five stages of grief are Kübler-Ross's. Exam trap: her stages describe the dying person and Bowlby's phases the bereaved; Stroebe and Schut describe oscillation, not stages; and Lawrence Kohlberg and Carol Gilligan tempt only because they are famous 'stage' theorists of moral development.", "topic": "Kübler-Ross stages of grief"}, {"q": "Smallest unit of speech perception is:", "options": {"a": "Phonemes", "b": "Syntax", "c": "Morphemes", "d": "Semantics"}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: the phoneme is the smallest unit of speech perception. Language is built in layers. At the bottom is the phoneme, the smallest unit of sound that can change the meaning of a word, though it has no meaning of its own: /p/ and /b/ are different phonemes in English because 'pat' and 'bat' are different words. English has roughly 40 to 45 phonemes, and every language picks its own set. Above phonemes come morphemes, the smallest units of meaning (free like 'cat', bound like the plural '-s'), then words, then syntax (the rules for combining words into sentences) and semantics (meaning). Charles Hockett (1960) called this duality of patterning: meaningless sounds combine into meaningful units. Listeners perceive phonemes categorically. Alvin Liberman and colleagues (1957) varied a speech sound in equal acoustic steps and found that people heard a sudden switch from one phoneme to another, not a smooth change, and could barely tell apart sounds within one category. Richard Warren (1970) showed phonemic restoration: when a cough replaced a sound in a sentence, listeners still 'heard' the missing phoneme. Example: Riya, a Hindi speaker, easily hears the difference between 'pal' (moment) and 'phal' (fruit), because aspiration is a phoneme contrast in Hindi; her English-speaking friend hears both as the same 'p', since in English aspiration never changes meaning. Option a is right because the phoneme is the minimal sound unit that listeners pick out and recognise in speech. Exam trap: the smallest unit of sound is the phoneme and the smallest unit of meaning is the morpheme; examiners swap them, and add syntax and semantics, which are rule systems and levels of meaning, not units.", "topic": "Phoneme as speech unit"}, {"q": "Separation anxiety is a part of:", "options": {"a": "Attachment in making phase", "b": "Clear-cut attachment", "c": "Pre-attachment phase", "d": "Formation of reciprocal relationship"}, "correct": "b", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is b: separation anxiety appears in Bowlby's clear-cut attachment phase. John Bowlby (1969), in Attachment and Loss, argued that attachment is an evolved system that keeps a helpless infant close to a caregiver, and that it unfolds in four phases. In pre-attachment (birth to about 6 weeks) built-in signals such as crying, grasping, gazing and smiling bring any adult near, and the baby does not mind being left with a stranger. In attachment-in-the-making (6 weeks to 6 to 8 months) the infant responds differently to familiar people, smiling and babbling more for the mother, but still does not protest when she leaves. In clear-cut attachment (6 to 8 months to 18 months to 2 years) the baby has a definite attachment figure, uses her as a secure base for exploring, follows her once he can crawl, and shows separation anxiety (crying and protest when she leaves) and wariness of strangers. In formation of a reciprocal relationship (18 months to 2 years onwards) language and an understanding of the parent's plans let the child negotiate, so protest falls; Bowlby called this the goal-corrected partnership. Rudolph Schaffer and Peggy Emerson (1964), following Glasgow infants at home, found similar timing: specific attachments at about 7 months, with fear of strangers about a month later. Example: nine-month-old Kabir happily went to any relative at four months, but now screams whenever his mother leaves the room and clings to her when a visitor arrives. Option b is right because separation protest is the marker of the clear-cut phase. Exam trap: attachment-in-the-making sounds as if attachment is already in place, but the baby only prefers familiar people then; protest at separation and stranger wariness belong to clear-cut attachment.", "topic": "Bowlby's phases of attachment"}, {"q": "Freud's stage of latency is corroborating with _____ stage of Erikson's psychosocial development.", "options": {"a": "Initiative Vs Guilt", "b": "Identity Vs Role confusion", "c": "Industry Vs Inferiority", "d": "Intimacy Vs Isolation"}, "correct": "c", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is c: Freud's latency stage matches Erikson's industry versus inferiority. Sigmund Freud (1905), in Three Essays on the Theory of Sexuality, described five psychosexual stages: oral (birth to about 1), anal (about 1 to 3), phallic (about 3 to 6), latency (about 6 to puberty) and genital (puberty onwards). In latency the sexual drive is quiet, and energy goes into school learning, skills, same-sex friendships and hobbies. Erik Erikson (1950), in Childhood and Society, kept Freud's timetable for childhood but asked what social task each age poses. His eight psychosocial crises are trust versus mistrust (oral), autonomy versus shame and doubt (anal), initiative versus guilt (phallic), industry versus inferiority (latency), identity versus role confusion (adolescence, Freud's genital stage), and then three adult stages Freud never described: intimacy versus isolation, generativity versus stagnation, and integrity versus despair. In industry versus inferiority (about 6 to 12 years) the child learns the tools of the culture at school; success with teachers and peers builds the virtue of competence, while repeated failure or harsh comparison leaves a sense of inferiority. Erikson even labelled this stage 'school age (latency)'. Example: nine-year-old Arjun spends evenings building model aeroplanes and learning to bowl; when his teacher praises his science project he feels capable, but if he is always compared with his topper cousin he may come to feel he is no good at anything. Option c is right because latency and industry versus inferiority cover the same years and the same focus on skills and schooling. Exam trap: count the stages carefully; initiative versus guilt matches the phallic (play age) stage, and identity versus role confusion matches Freud's genital stage, not latency.", "topic": "Freud and Erikson stages"}, {"q": "'Beyond the Pleasure Principle' author is:", "options": {"a": "Carl Gustav Jung", "b": "Alfred Adler", "c": "Sigmund Freud", "d": "Anna Freud"}, "correct": "c", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is c: Sigmund Freud wrote Beyond the Pleasure Principle. Sigmund Freud (1920) wrote this essay to explain facts his earlier theory could not. Until then he held that the mind is ruled by the pleasure principle: the id seeks immediate gratification and release of tension, and the ego, guided by the reality principle, merely delays pleasure to obtain it safely. But soldiers back from the First World War kept reliving their traumas in dreams, patients kept repeating painful patterns in therapy, and his small grandson replayed the distress of his mother leaving in the 'fort-da' game, throwing away a cotton reel and pulling it back. Freud called this urge to repeat unpleasant experience the repetition compulsion. To explain it he proposed a second basic drive beside the life instincts (Eros, whose energy is libido): the death instinct, an unconscious pull toward decay and a return to the inanimate state. Turned outward it appears as aggression; turned inward, as self-destruction. His followers called it Thanatos, a word Freud did not use in his writings. The essay opened his late theory, followed by The Ego and the Id (1923), which introduced the structural model of id, ego and superego. Example: after a road accident, Riya keeps having the same nightmare of the crash; the pleasure principle cannot explain why the mind would replay pain, which is exactly the puzzle Freud tackled. Option c is right because the book is Freud's, and it introduced the death instinct and the repetition compulsion. Exam trap: examiners pair famous titles with the wrong author; The Ego and the Mechanisms of Defence (1936) is Anna Freud's, and Psychological Types (1921) is Carl Gustav Jung's.", "topic": "Beyond the Pleasure Principle"}, {"q": "All are mature defence mechanisms except:", "options": {"a": "Humour", "b": "Altruism", "c": "Suppression", "d": "Projection"}, "correct": "d", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is d: projection is not a mature defence; it is an immature one. Defence mechanisms are unconscious ego strategies that protect a person from anxiety; Sigmund Freud described them and Anna Freud (1936) catalogued them. George Vaillant (1977), in Adaptation to Life, followed the Harvard men of the Grant Study for decades and ranked defences by maturity. Psychotic level: delusional projection, psychotic denial, distortion. Immature level: projection, fantasy, hypochondriasis, passive aggression and acting out. Neurotic (intermediate) level: repression, displacement, reaction formation, intellectualisation and dissociation. Mature level: altruism, humour, suppression, sublimation and anticipation. Men who relied on mature defences had better work, marriages and health in midlife. Projection means attributing your own unacceptable feelings or impulses to someone else. It distorts reality about other people, so it sits low on the ladder; in its fixed, delusional form, as in paranoid delusions, it is psychotic level. The mature defences let feelings in while handling them well: humour finds the funny side without hurting anyone, altruism turns your own need into service to others, suppression is a conscious decision to set a worry aside until later, sublimation channels an impulse into an acceptable outlet, and anticipation plans ahead for future distress. Example: Kabir resents his new manager but tells everyone the manager hates him (projection); a more mature Kabir notices his annoyance, decides to deal with it after the deadline (suppression) and jokes about it with friends (humour). Option d is right because projection belongs to Vaillant's immature group. Exam trap: suppression sounds like repression, but suppression is conscious and mature while repression is unconscious and neurotic level, so do not pick suppression as the odd one out.", "topic": "Vaillant's mature defences"}, {"q": "Token economy is behaviour modification technique based on the following learning principles:", "options": {"a": "Discrimination learning", "b": "Negative reinforcement", "c": "Positive reinforcement", "d": "Classical conditioning"}, "correct": "c", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is c: the token economy rests on positive reinforcement. A token economy is an operant programme built on B. F. Skinner's (1938) principle that behaviour followed by a rewarding consequence becomes more likely. Teodoro Ayllon and Nathan Azrin (1968, The Token Economy) ran the first full programme at Anna State Hospital in Illinois: long-stay psychiatric patients earned tokens for target behaviours such as self-care, grooming and ward jobs, and exchanged them for back-up reinforcers such as privileges, snacks or outings. Self-care and work rose sharply while tokens were given and fell when they were withdrawn. The tokens are secondary (conditioned) reinforcers: worthless in themselves, they gain value because they can be exchanged, as money does, and because they buy many things they work as generalised reinforcers that resist satiation. A good token economy defines target behaviours clearly, gives tokens immediately and consistently, sets a clear exchange rate, and gradually replaces tokens with social praise so gains last outside the programme. Some programmes add response cost, removing tokens for problem behaviour, which is negative punishment, but the core is positive reinforcement. Token economies are used on psychiatric wards, in classrooms, for children with ADHD or autism, and in correctional homes. Example: eight-year-old Arjun earns a star each time he finishes homework without reminders; ten stars buy a Sunday trip to the park, and homework completion climbs. Option c is right because giving a token after the desired behaviour, in order to increase it, is positive reinforcement. Exam trap: the token economy is operant, not classical conditioning, and it works by adding something pleasant (positive reinforcement), not by removing something unpleasant (negative reinforcement).", "topic": "Token economy reinforcement"}, {"q": "Insulin is released by:", "options": {"a": "Adrenal medulla", "b": "Thyroid gland", "c": "Pancreas", "d": "Pituitary gland"}, "correct": "c", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is c: insulin is released by the pancreas. The pancreas is both an exocrine gland, sending digestive enzymes into the gut through a duct, and an endocrine gland, releasing hormones straight into the blood. Its endocrine part is the islets of Langerhans, described by Paul Langerhans (1869). Beta cells in the islets make insulin; alpha cells make glucagon. After a meal blood glucose rises, and insulin lets muscle, fat and liver cells take up glucose and store the surplus as glycogen and fat, so blood sugar falls. Between meals glucagon does the opposite, releasing glucose from the liver. Frederick Banting and Charles Best (1921) extracted insulin in Toronto, and in 1922 it was first used to treat diabetes. Too little insulin, or cells that stop responding to it, gives diabetes mellitus. Insulin matters to psychologists in three ways. In hunger, insulin rises even before food arrives (the cephalic phase, triggered by the sight and smell of food), and high insulin increases hunger and fat storage. Clinically, low blood sugar from too much insulin causes shakiness, sweating, irritability and confusion that can mimic a panic attack. Historically, Manfred Sakel (1933) induced comas with insulin to treat schizophrenia (insulin coma therapy), a method later abandoned. Example: after Riya eats a plate of rice, her blood glucose climbs and her pancreas releases insulin; if she were diabetic and injected too much insulin, she might feel shaky and panicky an hour later. Option c is right because insulin is made by the beta cells of the pancreatic islets. Exam trap: the pituitary is called the master gland, so examiners offer it for every hormone, but insulin release is driven mainly by blood glucose itself, not by a pituitary hormone.", "topic": "Insulin and the pancreas"}, {"q": "For Skinner, the basic issue is how reinforcement sustains and controls responding rather than:", "options": {"a": "Which stimulus evokes a response", "b": "Which response is helpful", "c": "Which stimulus can be generalized", "d": "Which stimulus can be discriminated"}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: for B. F. Skinner the question was how reinforcement controls responding, not which stimulus evokes a response. B. F. Skinner (1938), in The Behavior of Organisms, separated two kinds of behaviour. Respondent behaviour is elicited by a known stimulus: food in the mouth produces salivation, a puff of air produces a blink. This is the reflex behaviour Ivan Pavlov (1927) studied in classical conditioning, where the scientist's task is to find which stimulus evokes which response. Operant behaviour is emitted: a rat in a Skinner box presses a lever, a child raises a hand, and no stimulus forces the act. What controls operant behaviour is its consequences. Responses followed by reinforcement become more frequent; responses followed by punishment or by nothing become less frequent. Skinner measured the rate of responding on a cumulative recorder, and with Charles Ferster (1957) showed how schedules of reinforcement (fixed and variable ratio and interval) shape the pattern and persistence of responding. Stimuli still matter, but as discriminative stimuli that signal when reinforcement is available; they set the occasion for a response rather than trigger it. This is the three-term contingency: discriminative stimulus, response, consequence. Example: Arjun raises his hand in class not because anything makes him, but because his teacher has praised his answers before; when a new teacher ignores raised hands, his hand-raising fades. Option a is right because asking which stimulus evokes a response is the respondent (Pavlovian) question that Skinner set aside. Exam trap: generalisation and discrimination sound like stimulus questions Skinner ignored, but he studied both within operant stimulus control; what he dropped was the search for an eliciting stimulus.", "topic": "Skinner's operant versus respondent"}, {"q": "Jung used the term \"Collective Unconscious\" to indicate:", "options": {"a": "The unconscious tendencies inherited by an individual from primordial racial tendencies", "b": "All the factors that together constitute to strengthen an individual's unconscious", "c": "The unconscious potential of a group or mob", "d": "The effect of all the unconscious urges which made a person's behaviour problematic"}, "correct": "a", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is a: Jung's collective unconscious is a layer of inherited unconscious tendencies that comes from humanity's ancestral past. Carl Gustav Jung (1916), after his break with Sigmund Freud in 1913, described the psyche in three layers. The ego is the conscious mind. Below it lies the personal unconscious, made of forgotten and repressed experiences of one's own life and organised into complexes, emotionally charged clusters such as a mother complex. Deepest of all is the collective unconscious, which is not built from personal experience at all: it is inherited and shared by all human beings, the residue of the experiences of our ancestors. It contains archetypes, inborn tendencies to perceive and respond in typical ways, which appear as universal images in myths, religions, fairy tales and dreams: the persona (social mask), the shadow (the disowned dark side), the anima and animus (the feminine side of a man and masculine side of a woman), the Great Mother, the wise old man, the hero and the self (the archetype of wholeness, often shown as a mandala). Jung's evidence was the recurrence of the same motifs in the myths of cultures that never met, and in the dreams and delusions of patients who could not have known those myths. Older textbooks call this inheritance 'racial', meaning the human race as a whole, not any ethnic group. Example: Meera, who has never read mythology, dreams of a flood that destroys the world and a single boat that survives; Jung would point to the same motif in the story of Manu in India and of Noah in the Bible. Option a is right because it describes inherited, primordial tendencies, which is exactly the collective unconscious. Exam trap: examiners offer crowd psychology or Freud's repressed urges, but those concern groups or personal history, while Jung's collective unconscious is universal and inherited.", "topic": "Jung's collective unconscious"}, {"q": "A child has two alleles of every gene in his body, one from his mother and one from his father. If the alleles from both the parents differ, he is said to be:", "options": {"a": "Heterozygous", "b": "Homozygous", "c": "An identical twin", "d": "A fraternal twin"}, "correct": "a", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is a: when the two alleles of a gene differ, the person is heterozygous for that gene. Gregor Mendel (1866), crossing pea plants, showed that each trait depends on a pair of inherited factors, now called genes, one from each parent. A gene sits at a fixed place (locus) on a chromosome, and humans carry 23 pairs of chromosomes, so every gene on the non-sex chromosomes comes in two copies. The alternative forms of a gene are alleles. If both copies are the same (AA or aa) the person is homozygous; if they differ (Aa) the person is heterozygous. The genotype is the set of alleles carried; the phenotype is the trait that shows. In a heterozygote a dominant allele is expressed and masks a recessive one, so a recessive trait shows only in the homozygote (aa), and heterozygotes for a recessive disorder are healthy carriers. Example: beta-thalassaemia, common in India, is recessive. Arjun inherits a thalassaemia allele from his mother and a normal allele from his father; he is heterozygous, with thalassaemia trait and at most mild anaemia. If he marries Meera, who is also a carrier, each child has a 1 in 4 chance of being homozygous and having thalassaemia major, a 1 in 2 chance of being a carrier, and a 1 in 4 chance of being unaffected. This is why carrier screening before marriage is encouraged. The same logic applies to phenylketonuria (PKU), in which a homozygous child cannot convert phenylalanine into tyrosine. Option a is right because 'hetero' means different: two different alleles at the same locus. Exam trap: homozygous and heterozygous describe the two alleles inside one person, while monozygotic (identical) and dizygotic (fraternal) describe how twins form; the shared root 'zygous' is the confusion examiners use.", "topic": "Heterozygous and homozygous alleles"}, {"q": "The ability to perceive and understand a situation or incident from another person's point of view is called:", "options": {"a": "Projection", "b": "Social-foresight", "c": "Imagination", "d": "Illusion"}, "correct": "b", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is b: of the options given, understanding a situation from another person's point of view is called social foresight. The standard name for this ability is perspective taking, or role taking. George Herbert Mead (1934), in Mind, Self and Society, argued that the self grows out of 'taking the role of the other': the child learns to see herself as others see her, first in play with single roles, then in organised games that need everyone's viewpoint (the generalised other). Jean Piaget and Bärbel Inhelder (1956) showed with the three mountains task that young preoperational children are egocentric: asked what a doll on the far side of a model sees, they describe their own view. Robert Selman (1976, 1980) traced social perspective taking through five levels using dilemmas such as Holly, who has promised not to climb trees but sees a friend's kitten stuck in one; children move from an egocentric level (about 3 to 6) to a societal level in adolescence. Perspective taking underlies empathy, theory of mind, moral reasoning and helping. Example: when his friend Riya snaps at him, Kabir thinks of her sick mother and her exam stress, sees why she is short-tempered, and does not take it personally. Option b is right because it is the only option about grasping another person's viewpoint; the others concern one's own feelings or perception. Exam trap: projection sounds social but runs the other way, putting your own feelings into someone else instead of understanding theirs. Key note: 'social foresight' is not a standard psychological term; textbooks call this ability perspective taking or role taking (George Herbert Mead, Robert Selman) or empathy. Since it is the only option about understanding another person's viewpoint, b stands.", "topic": "Perspective taking"}, {"q": "Perception without sensory stimulus is called:", "options": {"a": "Illusion", "b": "Hallucination", "c": "Phenomenon", "d": "Perceptual constancy"}, "correct": "b", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is b: perception without a sensory stimulus is a hallucination. Jean-Étienne Esquirol (1838), the French psychiatrist, gave the modern definition: a hallucination is a perception without an object, and he separated it from an illusion, the misperception of a real object. A true hallucination has the full qualities of a normal perception: it is vivid, it seems to come from outer objective space, and the person cannot stop it by will. Karl Jaspers (1913), in General Psychopathology, contrasted it with the pseudohallucination, which is experienced in inner subjective space ('inside my head') and is recognised as not quite real. Hallucinations occur in every sense. Auditory ones are the most common in schizophrenia, and voices talking about the patient in the third person or keeping up a running commentary are among Kurt Schneider's (1959) first-rank symptoms. Visual hallucinations suggest delirium, drug states or other organic causes; tactile ones such as formication (insects crawling under the skin) occur with cocaine use and alcohol withdrawal. Hypnagogic hallucinations (while falling asleep) and hypnopompic hallucinations (while waking) are common in healthy people. Example: Arjun, alone in a silent room, hears a man's voice saying 'now he is picking up his pen'; nothing is there, so this is a hallucination. If instead he heard the hum of the ceiling fan as whispering, that would be an illusion, because a real sound is being misread. Option b is right because the defining feature of a hallucination is the absence of any external stimulus. Exam trap: examiners swap illusion and hallucination; an illusion needs a real stimulus that is misinterpreted, a hallucination needs none, and a delusion is a false belief, not a perception at all.", "topic": "Hallucination versus illusion"}, {"q": "\"It is not a different process, it is just attention to irrelevant stimuli that are not a part of the main assigned task\", then what is it?", "options": {"a": "Distraction", "b": "Span of attention", "c": "Shifting of attention", "d": "Division of attention"}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: attention drawn to irrelevant stimuli outside the main task is distraction. Attention, in William James's (1890) words, is the mind taking possession, in clear and vivid form, of one out of several possible objects or trains of thought. Because the brain's capacity is limited, attention must select. Classical texts list its characteristics as selectivity, shifting, a limited span, fluctuation (attention to an unchanging object waxes and wanes) and division between tasks. Distraction is not a separate mental process added to these. It is ordinary attention, using the same mechanisms, that happens to settle on stimuli irrelevant to the assigned task. The pull can be external, from intense, novel, moving or changing stimuli such as a sudden noise or a phone notification, or internal, from worries, hunger or daydreams. Donald Broadbent's (1958) filter model treats distraction as unwanted input getting past the selective filter, and Neville Moray (1959) showed that some unattended input, such as one's own name, breaks through. Distraction can also be used on purpose: in James Gross's (1998) model of emotion regulation it is an attentional deployment strategy, and therapists teach it for pain and craving. Example: Kabir sits down to revise statistics, but his attention keeps going to WhatsApp pings and the cricket commentary from the next room; nothing is wrong with his attention, it is simply aimed at the wrong things. Option a is right because the stem defines distraction itself: the same attentional process directed at stimuli that are not part of the main task. Exam trap: shifting of attention also involves moving focus, but shifting means moving to a new object, often deliberately; distraction specifically means being pulled away to something irrelevant.", "topic": "Distraction and attention"}, {"q": "Which of the following is not one of the 4 major approaches to qualitative research?", "options": {"a": "Ethnography", "b": "Phenomenology", "c": "Case study", "d": "Non-experimental"}, "correct": "d", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is d: 'non-experimental' is a type of quantitative research design, not one of the qualitative approaches. Qualitative research studies meaning, experience and process through words, observation and documents, analysed by interpretation rather than statistics. It is usually grouped into four (sometimes five) traditions. Ethnography describes a culture or group from the inside through long immersion, mainly participant observation and key informants; Bronisław Malinowski (1922), living among the Trobriand Islanders, set its model. Phenomenology, from the philosopher Edmund Husserl (1913), describes the lived experience of a phenomenon as participants experience it, with the researcher bracketing (setting aside) their own assumptions. Grounded theory, from Barney Glaser and Anselm Strauss (1967), builds theory upward from the data through coding and constant comparison instead of testing a theory decided in advance. The case study gives an intensive, in-depth account of one bounded case, such as a person, family, ward or institution (Robert Yin, 1984; Robert Stake, 1995). John Creswell (1998) adds narrative research, the study of life stories, to make five. Non-experimental research, by contrast, is a broad quantitative category in which the researcher measures variables without manipulating them: correlational studies, surveys and causal-comparative (ex post facto) designs. Example: Meera wants to know what it is like to care for a son with schizophrenia; she interviews eight mothers in depth and describes the essence of their experience (phenomenology). If she instead gave 300 caregivers a burden scale and correlated scores with income, that would be a non-experimental quantitative study. Option d is right because non-experimental is a quantitative design label, not a qualitative tradition. Exam trap: 'non-experimental' sounds qualitative because qualitative researchers never manipulate variables, but the term belongs to the classification of quantitative designs.", "topic": "Qualitative research traditions"}, {"q": "In \"phenomenology\", a well written report will be highly descriptive of the participants' experiences and will often elicit in the reader a feeling that they feel as though they are experiencing the phenomenon themselves. This experience is called:", "options": {"a": "A phenomenal experience", "b": "A vicarious experience", "c": "A significant experience", "d": "A dream"}, "correct": "b", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is b: the reader's sense of living through the phenomenon is called a vicarious experience. Phenomenology studies lived experience: what it is like, from the inside, to go through something such as grief, chronic pain or a first episode of psychosis. The researcher interviews people who have lived the experience, brackets their own assumptions, identifies significant statements and themes, and writes a description of the essence of the experience. Max van Manen (1990), in Researching Lived Experience, argued that the writing is itself part of the method: a good phenomenological text is rich, concrete and evocative, so that the reader recognises the experience and seems to live it at second hand. That second-hand living is vicarious experience, 'vicarious' meaning experienced through another person rather than directly. It is also how qualitative findings travel: Robert Stake (1995) argued that readers of a case study make naturalistic generalisations from vicarious experience, applying what they felt in the description to their own settings, and Egon Guba and Yvonna Lincoln (1994) list vicarious experience as one way knowledge grows in the constructivist paradigm. A flat list of themes would lose this quality. Example: Riya reads a phenomenological study of mothers of children with autism, full of their own words about sleepless nights, stares in the market and small victories; she finishes it feeling that for a moment she has been inside their days. Option b is right because the stem describes experiencing a phenomenon through someone else's account, which is what vicarious means. Exam trap: 'phenomenal experience' tempts because it echoes the word phenomenology, but it is not the term for the reader's response; and do not mix this up with Albert Bandura's (1977) vicarious experience, a source of self-efficacy gained by watching others succeed.", "topic": "Vicarious experience in phenomenology"}, {"q": "Phenomenology has its disciplinary origins in:", "options": {"a": "Philosophy", "b": "Anthropology", "c": "Sociology", "d": "Many disciplines"}, "correct": "a", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is a: phenomenology began in philosophy. Edmund Husserl (1859 to 1938), a German philosopher and mathematician, founded phenomenology in the early 1900s, in Logical Investigations (1900 to 1901) and Ideas (1913). He wanted a rigorous way to study consciousness by describing things exactly as they appear in experience, 'back to the things themselves'. His method was the epoché, or bracketing: suspending everyday assumptions and theories about what causes an experience, so that its essential structure can be described. Martin Heidegger (1927), his former assistant, argued in Being and Time that we are always already immersed in the world, so all understanding is interpretation; this became hermeneutic (interpretive) phenomenology. Only later did phenomenology spread into other fields. Alfred Schutz (1932) carried it into sociology with The Phenomenology of the Social World, which in turn fed Peter Berger and Thomas Luckmann's The Social Construction of Reality (1966). In psychology, Amedeo Giorgi (1970) developed a descriptive phenomenological method, and Jonathan Smith (1996) created interpretative phenomenological analysis (IPA), now common in health and clinical psychology. Humanistic psychology, from Carl Rogers (1951) and Abraham Maslow (1954), also draws on its focus on subjective experience. Example: Riya plans an IPA study of how Indian students experience failing an entrance exam; her method traces back through Smith and Heidegger to Husserl's philosophy of consciousness. Option a is right because phenomenology was founded as a philosophical movement by Husserl before any social science adopted it. Exam trap: it is now used in many disciplines, so 'many disciplines' tempts, but the stem asks for its disciplinary origin; ethnography's origin is anthropology and grounded theory's is sociology, which is why b and c are offered.", "topic": "Origins of phenomenology"}, {"q": "Which of the following is not a form of non-random sampling?", "options": {"a": "Snowball sampling", "b": "Convenience sampling", "c": "Quota sampling", "d": "They are all forms of non-random sampling"}, "correct": "d", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is d: snowball, convenience and quota sampling are all forms of non-random sampling, so none of them is the exception. In probability (random) sampling every member of the population has a known, non-zero chance of selection, which lets the researcher estimate sampling error and generalise with stated confidence. The probability methods are simple random, systematic, stratified random, cluster and multistage sampling. In non-probability (non-random) sampling the researcher's judgement or convenience, not chance, decides who is included, so each person's chance of selection is unknown and bias can creep in. The main non-random methods are convenience (accidental) sampling, taking whoever is easiest to reach, such as students in one's own class; purposive (judgemental) sampling, choosing people who fit the study's purpose; quota sampling, fixing numbers for each subgroup to mirror the population (say 60 men and 40 women) and then letting interviewers fill each quota with anyone available; and snowball (chain-referral) sampling, in which a few participants recruit others like them, the route into hidden populations with no list, such as drug users or sex workers (Leo Goodman, 1961, analysed it formally). The cost of a biased sample showed in the 1936 Literary Digest poll, which predicted that Alf Landon would beat Franklin Roosevelt because its huge sample came largely from telephone and car owners. Example: Arjun studies stigma among families of people who inject drugs; he finds three families through a de-addiction clinic, and they introduce him to others, which is snowball sampling. Option d is right because a, b and c are all textbook non-probability methods. Exam trap: quota sampling looks like stratified random sampling because both divide the population into subgroups, but in quota sampling selection within each subgroup is not random, so it remains non-probability.", "topic": "Non-probability sampling"}, {"q": "Illnesses that seem to result from an interaction of physical and psychological factors are called:", "options": {"a": "Somatic", "b": "Hysterical", "c": "Psychosomatic", "d": "Conversion disorder"}, "correct": "c", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is c: illnesses that come from an interaction of physical and psychological factors are called psychosomatic. 'Psychosomatic' joins psyche (mind) and soma (body); the term is credited to the German physician Johann Christian Heinroth (1818). A psychosomatic disorder is a real physical disease, with measurable tissue damage or physiological change, whose onset or course is worsened by psychological factors such as stress, conflict or emotion. Franz Alexander (1950), in Psychosomatic Medicine, proposed that a specific unconscious conflict acting on a vulnerable organ produced each of seven classic psychosomatic disorders, the 'holy seven': peptic ulcer, essential hypertension, bronchial asthma, rheumatoid arthritis, ulcerative colitis, neurodermatitis and thyrotoxicosis. The specificity idea failed (most ulcers proved to be caused by Helicobacter pylori bacteria), but the broader point survived: chronic stress raises cortisol, suppresses immunity and worsens many diseases. George Engel (1977) widened this into the biopsychosocial model. DSM-5 now calls the category 'psychological factors affecting other medical conditions', and ICD-10 codes it F54. Example: Kabir has genuine asthma, confirmed by lung function tests; his attacks become much more frequent in the weeks before his board exams and after quarrels at home. The disease is real, and stress is making it worse. Option c is right because the stem stresses an interaction of physical and psychological factors, which defines psychosomatic illness. Exam trap: psychosomatic does not mean imaginary or 'all in the mind'; there is real bodily disease. In conversion disorder, by contrast, the symptom (paralysis, blindness, seizures) has no physical disease behind it, which is why examiners pair the two.", "topic": "Psychosomatic disorders"}, {"q": "A part of the brain that sends signals \"alert\" to higher centres of the brain in response to incoming message is:", "options": {"a": "Amygdala", "b": "Hippocampus", "c": "Limbic system", "d": "Reticular formation"}, "correct": "d", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is d: the reticular formation alerts the higher centres of the brain to incoming messages. The reticular formation ('reticulum' means little net) is a net-like column of neurons running through the core of the brainstem, from the medulla through the pons to the midbrain. Sensory pathways heading for the cortex send side branches (collaterals) into it, so it samples nearly all incoming information. Its ascending part, the reticular activating system (RAS), projects through the thalamus to the whole cortex and keeps it awake and alert. The classic evidence came from Giuseppe Moruzzi and Horace Magoun (1949): electrically stimulating the brainstem reticular formation in a drowsy or anaesthetised cat changed the slow, synchronised EEG of sleep into the fast, desynchronised EEG of alert waking. Earlier, Frédéric Bremer (1935) had shown that cutting the brainstem at the midbrain (cerveau isolé) left the forebrain in permanent sleep-like EEG, while cutting lower, between medulla and spinal cord (encéphale isolé), left normal sleep and waking. Damage to the RAS causes coma. The system also filters input, passing on novel or important signals and damping repetitive ones, and its descending fibres adjust muscle tone and reflexes. Example: Meera dozes through the drone of the fan in a lecture, but jolts awake the moment the teacher calls her name; her reticular formation passed on the important signal and roused her cortex. Option d is right because sending an 'alert' signal to the cortex in response to incoming input is the defining job of the reticular activating system. Exam trap: the limbic system and amygdala also respond to incoming signals, but they add emotional meaning; general arousal and wakefulness belong to the reticular formation.", "topic": "Reticular activating system"}, {"q": "The _____ lobe is to hearing as the occipital lobe is to vision.", "options": {"a": "Cerebellum", "b": "Parietal", "c": "Temporal", "d": "Frontal"}, "correct": "c", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is c: the temporal lobe is to hearing as the occipital lobe is to vision. Each cerebral hemisphere has four lobes: frontal, parietal, temporal and occipital. The primary auditory cortex lies in the temporal lobe, on the upper surface of the superior temporal gyrus, tucked inside the lateral (Sylvian) fissure, in the transverse temporal gyri named after the Austrian anatomist Richard Heschl (1878): Heschl's gyrus, Brodmann areas 41 and 42. The pathway runs from the cochlea through the auditory nerve, brainstem nuclei and the medial geniculate nucleus of the thalamus to Heschl's gyrus. The auditory cortex is tonotopically organised: like keys on a piano, neighbouring strips respond to neighbouring pitches. Around it lies auditory association cortex, and on the left, at the back of the superior temporal gyrus, Wernicke's area (Carl Wernicke, 1874) for understanding speech. Each ear sends input to both hemispheres, so damage to one auditory cortex does not cause deafness; damage on both sides can produce cortical deafness or auditory agnosia. The parallel for vision is the primary visual cortex (V1, striate cortex, Brodmann area 17) around the calcarine sulcus of the occipital lobe, fed by the lateral geniculate nucleus of the thalamus. Example: when Arjun's phone rings, the sound reaches his temporal lobes within milliseconds, and recognising it as his own ringtone draws on the nearby auditory association areas. Option c is right because the temporal lobe holds the primary auditory cortex, just as the occipital lobe holds the primary visual cortex. Exam trap: the cerebellum is not a cerebral lobe at all, and the parietal lobe handles touch and body sense (somatosensory cortex), which examiners offer because it lies next to the auditory region.", "topic": "Temporal lobe and hearing"}, {"q": "What is the role of positive and negative reinforcement?", "options": {"a": "To increase the likelihood that responses preceding both will be repeated", "b": "To decrease the likelihood that responses preceding negative reinforcement will be repeated", "c": "To increase the likelihood that responses preceding only negative reinforcement will be repeated", "d": "To ensure that there are no negative consequences following the behaviour"}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: both positive and negative reinforcement increase the likelihood that the response before them will be repeated. In operant conditioning, B. F. Skinner (1938) classified consequences by their effect on the behaviour they follow, building on Edward Thorndike's (1898) law of effect. Reinforcement is any consequence that makes a behaviour more likely; punishment is any consequence that makes it less likely. 'Positive' and 'negative' do not mean good and bad; they mean adding and removing. Positive reinforcement adds something pleasant after the response (praise, money, a sweet). Negative reinforcement removes something unpleasant after the response (pain stops, nagging ends, an alarm goes quiet). Both strengthen the response. The matching pair for weakening behaviour is positive punishment, adding something unpleasant (a scolding), and negative punishment, removing something pleasant (time-out, or response cost such as a fine). Negative reinforcement drives escape learning (ending an aversive stimulus already present) and avoidance learning (preventing it from arriving), and Orval Hobart Mowrer (1947) used it in his two-factor theory to explain why phobic avoidance and compulsions persist: each avoidance brings relief, which reinforces it. Example: Riya finishes her homework and her mother lets her watch television (positive reinforcement); on another day Riya finishes her homework so that her mother stops nagging (negative reinforcement). Either way she becomes more likely to finish her homework in future. Option a is right because reinforcement, positive or negative, by definition increases the probability of the preceding response. Exam trap: negative reinforcement is not punishment; it increases behaviour by taking away something aversive, while punishment decreases behaviour, and examiners rely on students reading 'negative' as 'bad'.", "topic": "Positive and negative reinforcement"}]};
window.PREVIEW_DEEP = Object.assign(window.PREVIEW_DEEP||{}, {"rinpas-2024": [{"q": "According to Masters and Johnson, following is the correct sequence of human sexual response:", "concept": "The answer is b: Masters and Johnson's sequence of human sexual response is excitement, plateau, orgasm, resolution. William Masters and Virginia Johnson (1966) published Human Sexual Response after observing and measuring the bodily reactions of nearly 700 adult volunteers in the laboratory, recording heart rate, blood pressure, breathing and genital blood flow. They described a four-phase cycle, often remembered by the letters EPOR. In excitement, blood flows into the genitals (vasocongestion), the penis becomes erect, the vagina lubricates and heart rate rises. In plateau, arousal climbs further and then levels off near its peak, with rising muscle tension and faster breathing. Orgasm is the brief peak, with rhythmic muscle contractions and release of tension. In resolution, the body returns to its unaroused state; men enter a refractory period in which another orgasm is not possible, while many women can return to orgasm without one. Option b is right because it is the only option that keeps their four phase names in their true order, with plateau between excitement and orgasm rather than after orgasm. Exam trap: Helen Singer Kaplan (1979) added desire as a first phase in her triphasic model (desire, excitement, orgasm), so a sequence that opens with desire or arousal is borrowing from a later model. \"Arousal\" is not one of the four Masters and Johnson phase names at all; it is the everyday word for what happens across excitement and plateau. Also watch for the refractory period being slipped in as a fifth phase; it lies inside the male resolution phase.", "traps": {"a": "This puts plateau after orgasm. In Masters and Johnson's model plateau is the levelled-off peak of arousal that comes before orgasm, so the order is wrong.", "c": "It replaces plateau with \"arousal\", which is not one of Masters and Johnson's phase names; arousal is the general process running through excitement and plateau.", "d": "It opens with arousal and drops plateau. A phase before excitement is the idea behind Helen Singer Kaplan's (1979) desire phase, not part of Masters and Johnson's four-phase cycle."}}, {"q": "Amit met with an accident and later turned into impulsive, irresponsible and less concerned with the consequences of his actions. He must have suffered damage in:", "concept": "The answer is b: impulsive, irresponsible behaviour with little concern for consequences after a head injury points to damage in the frontal lobe. The frontal lobe sits behind the forehead and is the largest lobe of the brain. Its front part, the prefrontal cortex, plans actions, weighs their outcomes, holds back urges and adjusts behaviour to social rules; together these are called executive functions. The underside of the prefrontal cortex, the orbitofrontal region just above the eye sockets, is especially tied to self-control and social judgement. The classic case is Phineas Gage, a railway foreman whose left frontal lobe was pierced by an iron tamping rod in a blasting accident in 1848. He survived and could still walk, talk and remember, but his physician John Harlow (1848) reported the case and, in an 1868 follow-up, described him as fitful, profane, impatient and unable to keep to his plans, so that friends said he was \"no longer Gage\". Damage to the orbitofrontal area gives this disinhibited, impulsive picture; damage to the dorsolateral prefrontal area disrupts planning and flexible thinking; damage to the medial frontal area leaves the person apathetic. Option b is right because Amit's change is in personality and self-control, not in sensation, vision or memory, and regulating behaviour is the frontal lobe's job. Exam trap: the temporal lobe is tempting because temporal damage can change emotion, as in the placid, hypersexual syndrome that Heinrich Klüver and Paul Bucy (1937) produced in monkeys by removing both temporal lobes; but the textbook pairing of an accident with impulsivity and poor judgement is the frontal lobe and Phineas Gage.", "traps": {"a": "The parietal lobe handles touch, body position and spatial attention; its damage causes sensory loss, apraxia or neglect of one side of space, not a change into an impulsive, careless personality.", "c": "The occipital lobe is the visual cortex; its damage causes loss of part of the visual field or visual agnosia, not disinhibited behaviour.", "d": "The temporal lobe serves hearing, language comprehension (Wernicke's area) and memory (hippocampus); its damage gives aphasia, amnesia or Klüver-Bucy changes, while impulsivity and poor judgement are the frontal signature."}}, {"q": "Reflexes are usually controlled by the:", "concept": "The answer is b: reflexes are usually controlled by the spinal cord. A reflex is a fast, automatic, unlearned response to a stimulus, such as pulling your hand off a hot pan. Charles Sherrington (1906), in The Integrative Action of the Nervous System, described the pathway behind it, the reflex arc. It has five links: a receptor in the skin picks up the stimulus; a sensory (afferent) neuron carries the signal to the spinal cord; an interneuron in the grey matter of the cord passes it on; a motor (efferent) neuron carries the command out; and an effector, a muscle or gland, acts. Because the loop closes inside the spinal cord, the brain is left out of the decision. Messages do travel up to the brain, but they arrive after the reflex has already happened, which is why you feel the pain a moment after your hand has jerked away. The simplest arc, the knee-jerk (patellar) reflex, has no interneuron at all: the sensory neuron synapses straight onto the motor neuron, a monosynaptic reflex. When Riya touches a hot iron, her hand pulls back before she has consciously registered the heat; the spinal cord has acted first and told the brain afterwards. Option b is right because the spinal cord is the centre of the classic reflex arc for the limb and body reflexes. Exam trap: some reflexes, such as coughing, sneezing, gagging and swallowing, are run by the medulla in the brainstem, so d looks attractive; but the word \"usually\" points to the spinal cord, which handles the typical reflexes taught in textbooks.", "traps": {"a": "The hypothalamus controls homeostasis (hunger, thirst, body temperature) and the pituitary gland; it regulates drives and hormones, not the fast reflex arc.", "c": "The frontal lobe holds the primary motor cortex and plans voluntary, deliberate movement; reflexes by definition bypass this conscious control.", "d": "The medulla does run a few brainstem reflexes (coughing, sneezing, gagging, swallowing) and the vital rhythms of breathing and heart rate, but most reflexes in the body close their loop in the spinal cord."}}, {"q": "Which one option is not a compulsive behaviour with obsessive-compulsive disorder?", "concept": "The answer is a: tapping is the option that is not a typical compulsion of obsessive-compulsive disorder (OCD). OCD has two parts. Obsessions are unwanted, intrusive thoughts, images or urges that cause anxiety, such as a fear of germs or the doubt that the door is locked. Compulsions are repetitive behaviours or mental acts that the person feels driven to perform in response to an obsession, to reduce the anxiety or prevent a feared event, even though the act is excessive or not realistically connected to it. The classic compulsions are washing or cleaning, checking, ordering and arranging, counting and repeating, plus silent mental rituals such as saying a set phrase. Arjun fears his house will burn down, so he checks the gas knob twenty times before leaving; each check eases the dread for a few minutes, which is exactly why the habit grows. In DSM-5 (2013), OCD moved out of the anxiety disorders into a new chapter, obsessive-compulsive and related disorders. Option a is right because simple tapping is a repetitive motor act more typical of tics or the fidgeting of ADHD than of an anxiety-driven ritual tied to an obsession. Exam trap: hoarding looks like the odd one out because DSM-5 made it a separate diagnosis, but older texts and OCD symptom checklists list hoarding among compulsive behaviours, while washing and checking are the two best known compulsions. Key note: touching and tapping rituals do occur in OCD (especially tic-related OCD), and since DSM-5 (2013) hoarding is its own disorder (hoarding disorder) rather than a symptom of OCD, so b can be argued by DSM-5. The paper's answer is tapping (a), as a repetitive motor act more typical of tics or ADHD fidgeting than of anxiety-driven compulsions, and it stands.", "traps": {"b": "Hoarding (saving useless items and being unable to discard them) was traditionally counted as an OCD compulsion; DSM-5 (2013) now lists hoarding disorder separately in the related disorders, but the paper still treats it as a compulsive behaviour.", "c": "Washing or cleaning is the classic compulsion, usually driven by contamination obsessions, the most common obsession in OCD.", "d": "Checking (locks, stoves, switches) is the other classic compulsion, driven by pathological doubt, so it is a core OCD behaviour."}}, {"q": "Most human characteristics determined by many sets of genes; they are called:", "concept": "The answer is c: most human characteristics, determined by many sets of genes, are called polygenic. A gene is a stretch of DNA on a chromosome that carries the code for a protein. Some traits follow a single gene, as Gregor Mendel (1866) showed with the colour and shape of his garden peas, and as in phenylketonuria or Huntington's disease in humans; such traits fall into clear either-or categories. Most human characteristics are different: height, skin colour, weight, intelligence and most personality traits vary smoothly along a continuum. Ronald Fisher (1918) showed mathematically that this continuous, bell-shaped variation is exactly what results when many genes, each with a small effect, add their contributions together. That is polygenic inheritance (\"poly\" means many). When the environment also plays a part, as it almost always does, the trait is called multifactorial. Take Kabir's height: no single \"height gene\" decides it; a great many gene variants each add a little, and his childhood nutrition adds more, so he ends up somewhere along the normal curve rather than in a \"tall\" or \"short\" box. The same logic explains why disorders such as schizophrenia run in families without following simple Mendelian ratios: risk builds up from many small genetic effects plus stress, as the diathesis-stress model proposes. Option c is right because polygenic is the standard genetic term for traits shaped by many genes acting together. Exam trap: \"multigenic\" sounds like a perfect translation of \"many genes\" and is occasionally used loosely, but the textbook term examiners want is polygenic; biogenic and sociogenic describe where a cause comes from (biology or society), not how many genes are involved.", "traps": {"a": "Biogenic means arising from biological causes, as in the biogenic view that a mental disorder has an organic origin; it says nothing about how many genes shape a trait.", "b": "Multigenic is sometimes used loosely for the same idea, but it is not the standard term; textbooks and exams use polygenic for traits controlled by many genes.", "d": "Sociogenic means caused by social factors, as in the social causation (sociogenic) hypothesis of schizophrenia; it is the opposite of a genetic explanation."}}, {"q": "Mongolism occurs due to:", "concept": "The answer is b: the condition is caused by an extra chromosome on pair 21. Humans normally have 46 chromosomes in 23 pairs; pairs 1 to 22 are autosomes and pair 23 is the sex chromosomes (XX or XY). The English physician John Langdon Down (1866) first described the condition, and in 1959 Jérôme Lejeune, with Marthe Gautier and Raymond Turpin, found its cause: a third copy of chromosome 21, giving 47 chromosomes in all. This is trisomy 21. \"Mongolism\", the word in the stem, is an old, offensive term based on a racial comparison and is no longer used; the condition is Down syndrome. About 95 percent of cases arise from nondisjunction, when the two copies of chromosome 21 fail to separate during meiosis, usually in the egg; the rest come from translocation or mosaicism. The risk rises sharply with the mother's age, especially after about 35. Typical features are intellectual disability (usually mild to moderate), a flat facial profile, upward slanting eyes, a single crease across the palm, low muscle tone, heart defects and, later in life, early Alzheimer's disease, because chromosome 21 carries the amyloid precursor protein gene. A baby girl, Anaya, born to a 41-year-old mother, may be picked up by prenatal screening and then confirmed by a karyotype showing three copies of chromosome 21. Option b is right because Down syndrome is trisomy 21, an extra autosome on pair 21. Exam trap: pair 23 is the sex chromosome pair, so an extra chromosome there gives conditions such as Klinefelter syndrome (47,XXY), not Down syndrome; and an extra Y gives 47,XYY.", "traps": {"a": "Pair 23 is the sex chromosome pair; an extra chromosome there produces sex chromosome conditions such as Klinefelter syndrome (47,XXY) or triple X (47,XXX), not Down syndrome.", "c": "No classic syndrome comes from an extra chromosome 20; the well-known autosomal trisomies seen in live births are 21 (Down), 18 (Edwards) and 13 (Patau).", "d": "An extra Y chromosome gives 47,XYY syndrome in males, usually with tall stature and few other signs; it is a sex chromosome condition, not Down syndrome."}}, {"q": "Which of the following are symptoms seen in a child who has experienced trauma?", "concept": "The answer is d: self-blame is a characteristic symptom in a child who has experienced trauma. Trauma here means exposure to actual or threatened death, serious injury or sexual violence, by experiencing it, witnessing it or learning that it happened to someone close. In posttraumatic stress disorder (PTSD), DSM-5 (2013) groups the symptoms into four clusters: intrusions (memories, nightmares, flashbacks), avoidance of reminders, negative changes in thinking and mood, and changes in arousal and reactivity. Criterion D, the thinking and mood cluster, includes distorted beliefs about the cause or consequences of the event that lead the person to blame themselves or others. Children are especially prone to this: because young children think egocentrically, they often decide the bad event happened because of them. Kabir, 8, saw his father beat his mother and now insists it happened because he was naughty that evening; he feels guilty and ashamed. Other childhood signs include repetitive play that re-enacts the event, frightening dreams without clear content, clinging, regression in toileting or speech, irritability, poor concentration and falling school marks. DSM-5 also has a separate preschool subtype for children aged 6 and under. Option d is right because self-blame is an internal, trauma-specific symptom named in the DSM-5 criteria, while the other options describe either healthy functioning or a general behaviour problem. Exam trap: options b and c describe good adjustment (doing well at school, playing with peers), which are protective signs, not symptoms. Key note: aggression (a) is also a common trauma reaction in children; the paper wants the most characteristic internal symptom, so d stands.", "traps": {"a": "Aggression and irritable outbursts do appear in traumatised children (the DSM-5 arousal cluster), but they are common in many other problems too; the paper wants the trauma-specific internal symptom, self-blame.", "b": "Good academic performance is a sign of healthy functioning; trauma more often brings poor concentration and falling school marks.", "c": "A tendency to play with other children shows normal social engagement; traumatised children more often withdraw, cling or play out the trauma repetitively."}}, {"q": "Which of the following may occur in Expressive Language Disorder?", "concept": "The answer is d: limited speech, difficulty learning new words and difficulty finding the right word can all occur in expressive language disorder. Language has two sides. Receptive language is understanding what others say; expressive language is putting your own thoughts into words and sentences. In DSM-IV (1994), expressive language disorder meant that a child's expressive language was clearly below both their nonverbal intelligence and their understanding of language, enough to interfere with school or social life. Its typical signs are a small vocabulary and a limited amount of speech, short and simplified sentences, errors in grammar and tense, trouble recalling or finding words, and slowness in learning new words. Comprehension stays relatively intact, which separates it from the mixed receptive-expressive type. DSM-5 (2013) merged both types into a single diagnosis, language disorder, within the communication disorders, defined by reduced vocabulary, limited sentence structure and impaired discourse (the ability to link sentences to explain or describe something). Meera, 5, understands instructions perfectly and follows stories, but speaks in two or three word phrases, often says \"that thing\" because she cannot retrieve a name, and needs many repetitions to learn a new word such as \"umbrella\"; her hearing and intelligence are normal. Option d is right because limited speech, difficulty learning new words and word-finding difficulty are each listed features of expressive language disorder. Exam trap: do not confuse expressive language disorder with speech sound disorder (trouble producing the sounds of words) or with stuttering (trouble with the flow of speech); expressive language disorder is about vocabulary and sentence building, not pronunciation or fluency.", "traps": {"a": "A limited amount of speech is a true feature, since these children say less, in short, simple sentences; but it is only one of the listed features, so \"all of these\" is better.", "b": "Difficulty learning new words (slow vocabulary growth) is also a recognised feature, so choosing it alone misses the other correct options.", "c": "Word-finding difficulty is a classic feature of expressive language disorder too, so choosing it alone ignores the other two."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is NOT a physical cause often associated with Phonological disorder?", "concept": "The answer is c: small frontal lobes are not a recognised physical cause of phonological disorder. Phonological disorder was the DSM-IV (1994) name; DSM-5 (2013) calls it speech sound disorder. It means persistent difficulty producing the sounds of speech, so that speech is hard to understand: sounds are left out (\"ca\" for \"cat\"), swapped (\"wabbit\" for \"rabbit\") or distorted, beyond what is expected for the child's age. Most cases have no known physical cause and are thought to reflect difficulty learning the sound rules of the language. But criterion D of DSM-5 speech sound disorder names the congenital or acquired conditions that can themselves produce speech sound problems: cerebral palsy (poor muscle control of the lips, tongue and jaw), cleft palate (a gap in the roof of the mouth that lets air escape through the nose), deafness or hearing loss (the child cannot hear sounds clearly enough to copy them) and traumatic brain injury. Arjun, 6, says \"tat\" for \"cat\" and \"wed\" for \"red\"; before diagnosing, the clinician checks his hearing, examines his palate and rules out a motor problem such as cerebral palsy. Option c is right because \"small frontal lobes\" is not a recognised physical cause of speech sound problems in any diagnostic system, while the other three are standard examples. Exam trap: all three real causes involve the ear, the mouth or the motor control of the mouth; an option built on a vague claim about brain size is the odd one out. Key note: the printed answer in the coaching copy is d (cerebral palsy), but DSM-5 lists cerebral palsy among the physical conditions behind speech sound problems, so c is used here.", "traps": {"a": "Hearing impairment is a recognised cause: a child who cannot hear speech sounds clearly cannot learn to copy them, and DSM-5 names deafness or hearing loss.", "b": "Cleft palate is a recognised structural cause; the gap in the roof of the mouth lets air escape through the nose and distorts consonants.", "d": "Cerebral palsy is named in DSM-5 among the conditions behind speech sound problems, because it weakens control of the lips, tongue and jaw (dysarthria), so it is a real physical cause."}}, {"q": "Stuttering is a disturbance in the normal fluency and time patterning of speech that is inappropriate for the individual's age. It involves which of the following?", "concept": "The answer is d: stuttering involves repetitions or prolongations of sounds, pauses within words and filled or unfilled pauses, so all of these. DSM-5 (2013) calls stuttering childhood-onset fluency disorder. Fluency is the smooth, effortless flow and timing of speech. DSM-5 lists seven kinds of disturbance, one or more of which must occur often: repetitions of sounds and syllables (\"b-b-ball\"); prolongations of sounds (\"sssssun\"); broken words, that is, pauses within a word (\"pen ... cil\"); audible or silent blocking, which are filled or unfilled pauses in speech; circumlocutions, swapping in other words to avoid a feared one; words produced with excess physical tension; and repetitions of whole one-syllable words (\"I-I-I want\"). The problem must cause anxiety about speaking or limit communication, school or work, must begin in the early developmental period, and must not be better explained by a motor or sensory problem or another condition. Onset is usually between 2 and 7 years; most children recover, and boys are affected more often than girls, especially in cases that persist. Stuttering often worsens under pressure, such as speaking to strangers, and tends to ease when singing, reading in unison or talking to a pet. Arjun, 7, gets stuck on the first sound of his name when the teacher calls the register, then starts avoiding answering at all; that mix of blocks and avoidance is typical. Option d is right because each of options a, b and c is one of the DSM-5 symptoms of fluency disorder. Exam trap: a pause within a word (a broken word) and a silent block sound like ordinary hesitation rather than stuttering, but both are listed DSM-5 features, so do not drop them.", "traps": {"a": "Frequent repetitions or prolongations of sounds are the best known features of stuttering, but they are only part of the DSM-5 list, so a alone is incomplete.", "b": "Pauses within words (broken words) are also a DSM-5 feature of fluency disorder, so choosing b alone ignores the others.", "c": "Filled or unfilled pauses (audible or silent blocking) are another DSM-5 feature; correct, but incomplete on its own."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is an example of an open-ended question as it relates to asking about family history?", "concept": "The answer is c: \"What were things like for you growing up?\" is the open-ended question. An open-ended question cannot be answered with a single word such as yes or no; it invites the client to tell their story in their own words. A closed question asks for a specific fact or a yes or no reply. Both have a place in a clinical interview. Open questions, which usually begin with what, how or \"tell me about\", are used early to build rapport and let important material surface; closed questions come later to pin down details such as dates, frequency or risk. Allen Ivey (1968), whose microcounselling approach trains interviewers one skill at a time, made open and closed questioning two of the basic attending skills. Family history is a sensitive area, so a good opening question is broad and neutral: it avoids jargon, does not assume anything happened and does not push the client towards a particular answer. When Riya is asked, \"What were things like for you growing up?\", she can start wherever she likes: her strict father, moving cities, a close grandmother. If she wants to mention abuse, she can, without being led there. Option c is right because it is the only question that cannot be answered with yes or no and it leaves the content entirely to the client. Exam trap: option d sounds open because it says \"any kind of abuse\", but it still invites only yes or no, and it is a leading question that suggests abuse. Option a adds a judgemental label, \"dysfunctional\", which a client may reject or misunderstand.", "traps": {"a": "This is a closed yes or no question, and it uses a loaded clinical label (\"dysfunctional\") that asks the client to judge their family rather than describe it.", "b": "This is closed and leading: it can be answered yes or no and steers the client towards abuse in the home before they have described anything.", "d": "Despite the broad wording \"any kind of abuse\", it still asks for yes or no and assumes victimhood, so it is a closed, leading question."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is NOT the specifications of the POCSO ACT, 2012?", "concept": "The answer is d: detaining the child in the police station at night is not a specification of the POCSO Act, 2012; the Act forbids it. The Protection of Children from Sexual Offences (POCSO) Act, 2012 is India's law on sexual offences against anyone under 18. Besides defining offences such as penetrative and non-penetrative sexual assault, sexual harassment and using a child for pornography, it sets out child-friendly procedures so that reporting and trial do not traumatise the child a second time. Section 24 covers recording the child's statement. It is to be recorded at the child's residence, or where the child usually lives, or at a place of the child's choice; as far as practicable by a woman police officer not below the rank of sub-inspector; and the officer must not be in uniform. The officer must make sure the child does not come into contact with the accused, and section 24 says plainly that no child is to be detained in the police station in the night for any reason. Section 33 governs the Special Court: the child should not be called repeatedly to testify, breaks are allowed, and the court must not permit aggressive questioning or character assassination of the child, so that the child's dignity is protected throughout the trial. Meera, 11, reports abuse by a neighbour; a woman sub-inspector in plain clothes records her statement at home with her mother present, and Meera sleeps at home that night. Option d is right because the Act bars night detention of a child at a police station, so d is the one statement that is not a POCSO specification. Exam trap: a, b and c all sound like mere courtesies, but each is a written provision of the Act; c comes from section 33, which covers the trial, not the police statement.", "traps": {"a": "Section 24 of POCSO requires the child's statement to be recorded at the child's residence or a place of the child's choice, so this is a specification of the Act.", "b": "Section 24 says the officer recording the child's statement must not be in uniform, to reduce fear, so this is a specification.", "c": "Section 33 bars aggressive questioning or character assassination of the child in the Special Court, so this too is part of the Act."}}, {"q": "In autistic spectrum disorder, when an individual exhibits immediate imitation of words or sounds they have just heard, this is known as:", "concept": "The answer is c: immediate imitation of words or sounds just heard is called echolalia. Echolalia (from the Greek words for echo and speech) is the repetition of another person's words or phrases. In immediate echolalia the person repeats what was just said, straight away; in delayed echolalia they repeat phrases heard hours or days earlier, such as lines from a television advertisement. Leo Kanner (1943), in the first description of autism, noted echolalia in several of his eleven children. DSM-5 (2013) lists it under criterion B1 of autism spectrum disorder as an example of stereotyped or repetitive speech, alongside idiosyncratic phrases. Echolalia is not always meaningless. Studies of autistic children have shown that echoed phrases can serve real purposes: taking a turn in conversation, saying yes, making a request or calming oneself. Ask Kabir, 4, \"Do you want juice?\" and he answers \"Do you want juice?\" when he means yes. Speech therapists often build on this by modelling the reply they want him to echo (\"Juice, please\"). Echolalia is also normal for a short time in toddlers learning to talk, and it appears in Tourette syndrome, catatonia and some dementias. Its partner for movement is echopraxia, the automatic imitation of another person's actions. Option c is right because echolalia is the established clinical term for repeating words or sounds just heard. Exam trap: \"echoastic\" sounds like a real word because it borrows \"echo\", but no such disorder exists; and do not mix up echolalia (speech) with echopraxia (movement) or with palilalia (repeating your own words).", "traps": {"a": "Echoastic disorder is not a recognised term; it is a distractor that borrows the prefix \"echo\" from echolalia.", "b": "Phonological inhibition is not a clinical term for repeating speech; phonology is the sound system of language, as in phonological (speech sound) disorder.", "d": "Grapheme dysfunction would refer to trouble with written letters (graphemes), a reading or spelling issue as in dyslexia, not to repeating heard speech."}}, {"q": "When an individual with multiple cognitive disabilities has extraordinary proficiency in one isolated skill, this is known as:", "concept": "The answer is d: extraordinary skill in one isolated area alongside wider cognitive disability is savant syndrome. A savant has a developmental disability, most often autism or intellectual disability, together with an \"island\" of ability far above both their own general level and, often, that of the general population. John Langdon Down (1887), the physician who also described Down syndrome, coined the phrase \"idiot savant\" in his lectures, using \"idiot\" in its old technical sense of severe intellectual disability; that label is now offensive and the condition is called savant syndrome. The psychiatrist Darold Treffert (1989), in his book Extraordinary People, made the condition widely known. He estimated that about half of savants are autistic and that roughly one autistic person in ten shows some savant ability. The skills fall in a narrow set of areas: calendar calculation (naming the weekday of any date), music (playing a piece after a single hearing), art (drawing a city skyline from memory), rapid arithmetic, and mechanical or spatial skill, all supported by exceptional memory. Kabir, 14, has an intellectual disability and struggles to count change, yet he can tell you instantly that 3 March 1995 was a Friday. Option d is right because savant syndrome is the accepted name for this combination of disability and isolated brilliance. Exam trap: \"Rain Man\" is the 1988 film in which Dustin Hoffman played an autistic savant, inspired partly by Kim Peek, a real man with an astonishing memory; it is a film title, not a diagnosis. And a prodigy, who excels without any disability, is not a savant.", "traps": {"a": "Rain Man is the title of a 1988 film about an autistic savant, partly inspired by Kim Peek; it is not a clinical syndrome.", "b": "Asperger ability is not a diagnostic term; Hans Asperger (1944) described autistic children with good language and intelligence, a former DSM-IV diagnosis that DSM-5 folded into autism spectrum disorder.", "c": "Intellectual isolation is not a recognised clinical term; it suggests social or academic isolation, not one outstanding skill set against disability."}}, {"q": "Autism spectrum disorder consists of the following conditions:", "concept": "The answer is d: the paper counts Asperger's syndrome, Rett's syndrome and childhood disintegrative disorder all within the autism spectrum. These three, with autistic disorder and PDD not otherwise specified (PDD-NOS), were the five pervasive developmental disorders of DSM-IV-TR (2000). Asperger's disorder, named after Hans Asperger (1944), meant autistic social difficulty and narrow interests without delay in language or intelligence. Childhood disintegrative disorder, described by Theodor Heller (1908), meant at least two years of normal development followed by marked loss of language, social, play, toileting or motor skills. Rett's disorder, described by Andreas Rett (1966), affects mainly girls: after early normal development, head growth slows, speech and purposeful hand use are lost, and constant hand-wringing movements appear. Lorna Wing and Judith Gould (1979) had argued that autistic conditions form a continuum, and DSM-5 (2013) replaced the separate boxes with one diagnosis, autism spectrum disorder (ASD), defined by deficits in social communication and interaction plus restricted, repetitive behaviour, with severity rated by level of support. Riya, 3, who loses her words and begins constant hand-wringing, would once have been placed in the pervasive developmental group. Option d is right because, under the pervasive developmental disorder grouping this question follows, all three conditions sit within the autism family. Exam trap: picking one condition alone, such as Asperger's, the best known, misses that the question lists members of the old umbrella; with \"all of these\" on offer, the examiner wants the whole group. Key note: under DSM-IV-TR the \"pervasive developmental disorders\" umbrella held five conditions (autistic disorder, Asperger's, Rett's, childhood disintegrative disorder, PDD-NOS). DSM-5 (2013) folded autistic disorder, Asperger's, CDD and PDD-NOS into ASD but dropped Rett's as a separate disorder (it is a genetic MECP2 condition). So strictly by DSM-5 Rett's is not part of ASD; the paper's \"all of these\" is the old PDD answer and stands.", "traps": {"a": "Asperger's syndrome (Hans Asperger, 1944) is part of the autism family and was folded into ASD by DSM-5, but it is only one of the three listed conditions.", "b": "Rett's syndrome (Andreas Rett, 1966) was one of the DSM-IV pervasive developmental disorders; DSM-5 left it out of ASD as a genetic MECP2 disorder, but the paper counts it in the group, so b alone is incomplete.", "c": "Childhood disintegrative disorder (Theodor Heller, 1908) was merged into ASD by DSM-5, but choosing it alone ignores the other two."}}, {"q": "Behaviours/symptoms that are not included in DSM-5 autism spectrum disorder:", "concept": "The answer is c: lack of functional play skills is the option not included in the DSM-5 description of autism spectrum disorder. DSM-5 (2013) defines autism spectrum disorder by two domains. Criterion A is persistent deficits in social communication and social interaction, in three areas: social-emotional reciprocity (A1), nonverbal communicative behaviour such as eye contact and gesture (A2), and developing, maintaining and understanding relationships (A3). A3 is where play and imagination appear: it includes difficulties in sharing imaginative play or in making friends, and absence of interest in peers. Criterion B covers restricted, repetitive behaviour, interests or activities, such as lining up toys or spinning objects, which is where unusual play also shows up. Imagination was one strand of the older triad of impairments described by Lorna Wing and Judith Gould (1979): social interaction, communication and imagination. Functional play, by contrast, is a developmental term: using an object for its intended purpose, such as pushing a toy car along the floor or holding a toy cup to a doll's lips. In typical development it comes before pretend play. Arjun, 4, spins the wheels of his toy car for long periods and never joins his cousins' game of shop; DSM-5 would place the spinning under criterion B and the missing shared pretend play under A3. Option c is right because \"functional play skills\" is not DSM-5's wording, while the imagination-related options sit close to what criterion A3 describes. Exam trap: all four options sound like autism symptoms, so look for the one built from developmental psychology vocabulary rather than DSM-5's own phrasing. Key note: none of the options quotes DSM-5 word for word and options a, b and d describe imagination problems close to A3's wording, while \"lack of functional play skills\" is not DSM-5's wording; the printed answer c stands.", "traps": {"a": "Problems with play and imagination echo DSM-5 criterion A3 (difficulties in sharing imaginative play), so this is treated as part of the ASD picture.", "b": "Impairments in imaginative play are close to DSM-5's own words in criterion A3, so this is included.", "d": "Difficulty distinguishing fantasy from reality is not a DSM-5 phrase, but the paper groups it with the imagination problems of criterion A3 and the older Wing and Gould triad, so it is not the odd one out here."}}, {"q": "All of the symptoms are symptoms of Mr. S who had damage to Wernicke's area except:", "concept": "The answer is d: a lesion in the left hemisphere is not a symptom of Mr. S's Wernicke's area damage; it is the cause. Carl Wernicke (1874) described patients with damage to the back of the left superior temporal gyrus, next to the primary auditory cortex. This region, Wernicke's area, links heard words to their meanings. When it is damaged, the result is Wernicke's (receptive or fluent) aphasia. Speech flows at a normal rate, with normal grammar and melody, but it is empty of meaning. The person produces paraphasias, wrong sounds or wrong words (\"spoon\" for \"fork\"), and neologisms, made-up words such as \"blimter\". Comprehension of spoken and written language is poor, and so is repetition. Typically the person is unaware of the problem and may be puzzled or irritated that others cannot follow. This contrasts with Broca's aphasia (Paul Broca, 1861), where speech is slow, effortful and telegraphic but comprehension is fairly good. Mr. S, after a stroke, chats fluently, says \"the gorbish is on the tebble\" and cannot follow a simple instruction such as \"close your eyes\". Option d is right because a lesion in the left hemisphere describes where the damage is, not anything Mr. S experiences or shows; it is the cause, not a symptom. Exam trap: options b and c are true symptoms, so students go hunting among them for a false detail; the trick is to notice that d is a different kind of statement altogether. Key note: strictly, \"inability to speak\" (a) is not a feature either, because Wernicke's speech is fluent; but d is not a symptom at all, it is the cause (the left temporal lesion), so d stands.", "traps": {"a": "Inability to speak is not really a Wernicke's feature, since the speech is fluent; it fits Broca's or global aphasia, but it is still a description of a symptom, whereas d is not a symptom at all.", "b": "Inability to comprehend is the core symptom of Wernicke's aphasia, because the damaged area links words to their meanings.", "c": "Neologisms (invented words) are a hallmark of the fluent, empty speech of Wernicke's aphasia, so they are a symptom."}}, {"q": "The disturbance of an ability to speak, read and write is known as:", "concept": "The answer is a: a disturbance of the ability to speak, read and write is aphasia. Aphasia is a loss or impairment of language caused by brain damage, usually a stroke in the left hemisphere, in someone whose language was previously normal. It affects language itself, not the muscles of speech, so it shows up across speaking, understanding, reading (alexia) and writing (agraphia), and even in sign language in deaf signers. Paul Broca (1861) described his patient \"Tan\", who could say only one syllable after damage to the left inferior frontal lobe: this is Broca's (expressive, non-fluent) aphasia, with slow, effortful, telegraphic speech but fair comprehension. Carl Wernicke (1874) described the opposite pattern after damage to the left posterior temporal lobe: Wernicke's (receptive, fluent) aphasia, with fluent but meaningless speech and poor comprehension. Damage to the arcuate fasciculus, the fibre bundle joining the two areas, gives conduction aphasia, in which repetition is especially poor; damage to the whole language zone gives global aphasia. Meera, 62, has a left-hemisphere stroke; she now struggles to name objects, cannot write her grocery list and finds the newspaper hard to read, although the muscles of her hand and mouth work normally. Option a is right because aphasia is the term for an acquired language disturbance that covers speech, reading and writing. Exam trap: apraxia also follows left-hemisphere damage and often coexists with aphasia, so the two are confused; but apraxia is a problem planning skilled movements (such as waving goodbye on request), not a problem with language. Do not confuse aphasia with dysarthria either, which is slurred speech from weak or poorly controlled speech muscles.", "traps": {"b": "Apraxia (Hugo Liepmann, 1900) is the inability to carry out skilled, purposeful movements on command despite intact strength and understanding; it is a movement disorder, not a language disorder.", "c": "Blindsight (Lawrence Weiskrantz, 1974) is the ability of people with damage to the primary visual cortex to respond to objects in their blind field without being aware of seeing them; it concerns vision, not language.", "d": "Spatial (hemispatial) neglect is failure to attend to one side of space, usually the left after right parietal damage, so the person ignores food on the left of the plate; it is an attention disorder, not a language one."}}, {"q": "Cognitive Behaviour Therapy works on:", "concept": "The answer is d: cognitive behaviour therapy works on both thought and behaviour. Cognitive behaviour therapy (CBT) joined two traditions. Behaviour therapy, built on the learning research of Ivan Pavlov (1927) and B. F. Skinner (1938), changed actions directly through exposure, reinforcement and skills training. Aaron Beck (1960s), working with depressed patients, found that they had automatic negative thoughts about themselves, the world and the future (the cognitive triad), and built cognitive therapy to test and change those thoughts. Albert Ellis (1955) developed a parallel approach, rational emotive behaviour therapy. The CBT model holds that thoughts, feelings, behaviour and bodily sensations influence one another in a loop. Of these, thoughts and behaviour are the parts a person can change deliberately, so they are the two targets, and mood shifts as a result. Cognitive techniques include identifying automatic thoughts, thought records, examining the evidence and behavioural experiments; behavioural techniques include activity scheduling, graded exposure and problem solving. Riya, 20, thinks \"I'm useless, I'll fail anyway\" and stops attending classes, so her mood sinks further. Her therapist helps her test the thought against her past marks and schedules short study sessions; as her thinking and behaviour change, her low mood lifts. Option d is right because CBT intervenes directly on thoughts and on behaviour, the two halves of its name. Exam trap: option c, mood state, is what clients come in wanting to change, so it is tempting; but CBT does not work on mood directly. It changes mood indirectly, by working on the thoughts and behaviours that keep the mood going.", "traps": {"a": "Thought is one target of CBT, the cognitive half that comes from Aaron Beck's cognitive therapy, but on its own it leaves out behaviour.", "b": "Behaviour is the other target, from behaviour therapy's exposure and reinforcement methods, but CBT works on thoughts as well.", "c": "Mood is the outcome CBT hopes to improve, but it changes indirectly through thoughts and behaviour rather than being worked on directly."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is considered as the central tenet of Client Centered Therapy?", "concept": "The answer is d: congruence, empathy and unconditional positive regard are all central tenets of client-centred therapy. Carl Rogers (1957) proposed that therapy works not because of techniques but because of the relationship. In his paper on the necessary and sufficient conditions of therapeutic personality change he listed six conditions; the three the therapist must provide are known as the core conditions. Congruence, or genuineness, means the therapist is real: what they feel inside matches what they show, without a professional mask. Unconditional positive regard means warm acceptance of the client as a person, without conditions of worth, so the client does not have to earn approval. Accurate empathic understanding means sensing the client's private world as if it were one's own, without losing the \"as if\" quality, and communicating that understanding back. Rogers argued that when a client experiences these conditions over time, they drop defensive distortions, accept experiences they had denied and move from incongruence towards self-actualisation. Arjun, 19, tells his counsellor he wants to leave engineering for music and expects a lecture; instead she listens closely, reflects his excitement and fear accurately, does not judge his choice and is honest about her own reactions. Feeling accepted, he begins to explore what he actually wants rather than what his parents expect. Option d is right because Rogers treated all three as essential and interdependent, so none alone is the central tenet. Exam trap: empathy is the condition most often quoted, so students pick b, and some texts single out congruence as the most basic; when \"all of these\" is offered alongside the three core conditions, it is the answer.", "traps": {"a": "Congruence (genuineness) is one of Carl Rogers' three core conditions, but choosing it alone leaves out empathy and unconditional positive regard.", "b": "Accurate empathic understanding is a core condition, and the most quoted, but it is only one of the three.", "c": "Unconditional positive regard is a core condition too, but on its own it is incomplete."}}, {"q": "Functional Analysis is considered under:", "concept": "The answer is c: functional analysis is considered under operant conditioning. B. F. Skinner (1938) showed that operant behaviour is controlled by its consequences: behaviour followed by reinforcement becomes more likely, and behaviour followed by punishment or no reward becomes less likely. Functional analysis applies this idea to a person's problem behaviour. Instead of asking only what the behaviour looks like, it asks what function it serves, that is, what keeps it going. The simplest form is the ABC model: Antecedents (what happens just before), Behaviour (described in observable terms) and Consequences (what follows). Frederick Kanfer and George Saslow (1965) expanded behavioural analysis into the SORKC model (stimulus, organism, response, contingency, consequence). Common functions are gaining attention, escaping a demand, getting a tangible item and automatic (sensory) reinforcement. Once the function is clear, treatment removes the reinforcer for the problem behaviour and teaches a better behaviour that earns the same result. Kabir, 6, is told to start his homework every evening (antecedent), throws his book (behaviour) and is sent to his room, so homework is avoided (consequence). The function is escape, so the time-out actually rewards him; the plan is to break homework into short tasks, praise effort and let him ask for a break with a card. Option c is right because functional analysis comes from Skinner's operant analysis of behaviour and its consequences. Exam trap: classical conditioning also belongs to behaviour therapy, so a looks plausible; but classical conditioning pairs stimuli to produce reflexive responses and does not analyse consequences, which are the heart of functional analysis.", "traps": {"a": "Classical conditioning (Ivan Pavlov, 1927) explains how a neutral stimulus comes to trigger a reflexive response through pairing; it looks at what comes before a response, not at the consequences that maintain it.", "b": "Humanistic principles (Carl Rogers, 1951; Abraham Maslow, 1954) focus on the self, growth and the therapeutic relationship, and do not break behaviour down into antecedents and consequences.", "d": "Adlerian principles come from Alfred Adler's (1912) individual psychology: inferiority feelings, style of life and social interest. Adlerians ask about the purpose of behaviour, but functional analysis as a method belongs to operant behaviour analysis."}}, {"q": "In psychodynamic therapy, analysis of resistance is where:", "concept": "The answer is b: analysis of resistance is where the client obstructs the analysis and the therapist explores the reasons for this. Sigmund Freud and Josef Breuer (1895), in Studies on Hysteria, noticed that patients struggled against remembering painful material even while asking to be cured. Freud called this opposition resistance: anything in the patient that blocks the work of treatment and access to the unconscious. It is the defence of repression showing itself in the consulting room. Resistance can look like arriving late, missing sessions, forgetting to pay, long silences, changing the subject just as something painful comes up, intellectualising, or suddenly feeling cured (a flight into health). Freud came to see resistance not as a nuisance but as information: what the patient avoids points to what matters most. So the analyst gently points out the obstruction and explores with the patient what it is protecting against, rather than pushing past it. Kabir, three months into therapy, arrives fifteen minutes late every time his father is due to come up; the therapist notes the pattern and wonders aloud what it might be like to talk about his father, opening the topic he has been avoiding. Option b is right because it describes both halves of the technique: the client's obstruction and the therapist's exploration of why it happens. Exam trap: the other three options are also psychoanalytic techniques, so they all sound right; match each to its name. Saying whatever comes to mind is free association, interpreting dreams is dream analysis, and redirecting feelings about significant others onto the therapist is transference.", "traps": {"a": "This is free association, Sigmund Freud's fundamental rule that the client says whatever comes to mind without censoring it; it is the basic method, not the analysis of resistance.", "c": "This is dream analysis, which Sigmund Freud (1900) called the royal road to the unconscious; the analyst interprets the hidden (latent) content behind the remembered (manifest) dream.", "d": "This is transference, which Sigmund Freud recognised in the Dora case (1905): the client redirects feelings about parents or other significant figures onto the therapist."}}, {"q": "The link between Nervous System and Endocrine System is possible through:", "concept": "The answer is b: the link between the nervous system and the endocrine system is the hypothalamus. The hypothalamus is a small structure, about the size of an almond, at the base of the brain just below the thalamus. It controls hunger, thirst, body temperature, sexual behaviour and the stress response, and it does much of this through hormones. It sits directly above the pituitary gland, often called the master gland, and is joined to it by a stalk. The link works in two ways. First, some hypothalamic neurons make oxytocin and vasopressin (antidiuretic hormone) and send them down their axons into the posterior pituitary, which releases them into the blood. Second, the hypothalamus secretes releasing and inhibiting hormones into a tiny network of blood vessels, the hypophyseal portal system, which carries them to the anterior pituitary; there they switch on or off the hormones that control the thyroid, adrenal glands, gonads and growth. Geoffrey Harris (1955) established this neural control of the anterior pituitary. A clear example is the hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal (HPA) axis. Before her board exam, Riya's hypothalamus releases corticotropin-releasing hormone, her pituitary releases adrenocorticotropic hormone (ACTH), and her adrenal cortex releases cortisol, which raises her blood sugar and keeps her alert: a nervous signal (worry) has become a hormonal one. Option b is right because the hypothalamus is the brain structure that turns neural signals into hormonal ones through its control of the pituitary. Exam trap: the pituitary is the gland that gives orders to the other glands, but it is itself ruled by the hypothalamus, so the bridge between the two systems is the hypothalamus.", "traps": {"a": "The corpus callosum is the large band of fibres joining the left and right cerebral hemispheres; it links the two halves of the brain, not the brain and the glands.", "c": "The reticular formation in the core of the brainstem controls arousal, alertness and the sleep-wake cycle (Giuseppe Moruzzi and Horace Magoun, 1949); it has no direct role in hormone release.", "d": "The cerebellum coordinates balance, posture and smooth, well-timed movement and supports motor learning; it is not part of the neuroendocrine link."}}, {"q": "In Neurons, Axons are insulated by:", "concept": "The answer is c: in neurons, axons are insulated by the myelin sheath. A neuron has three main parts: dendrites that receive signals, a cell body (soma) that holds the nucleus, and an axon, a long fibre that carries the electrical signal, the action potential, away from the cell body to other cells. Many axons are wrapped in myelin, a fatty white layer made by glial cells: oligodendrocytes in the brain and spinal cord, and Schwann cells in the peripheral nerves. Myelin is laid down in segments, with small bare gaps between them called nodes of Ranvier. Because the fatty wrapping stops current leaking out, the action potential jumps from node to node instead of creeping along every millimetre of membrane. This is saltatory conduction (from the Latin saltare, to jump). It lets thick myelinated fibres conduct at about 100 metres per second or more, many times faster than thin unmyelinated fibres, and it saves energy. Myelin is also why white matter looks white. Myelination continues through childhood and adolescence, with the prefrontal cortex finishing last. When myelin is damaged, signals slow or fail. Meera, 30, develops blurred vision in one eye and tingling legs; an MRI shows patches of lost myelin in her brain and spinal cord, the picture of multiple sclerosis, an autoimmune attack on central myelin. Option c is right because the myelin sheath is the insulating cover of the axon. Exam trap: \"Sylvian sheath\" sounds anatomical because of the Sylvian fissure (the lateral sulcus separating the temporal lobe from the frontal and parietal lobes), but no such sheath exists.", "traps": {"a": "A nerve fibre is the axon itself (or a bundle of axons in a nerve), so it is the thing being insulated, not the insulation.", "b": "A ganglion is a cluster of neuron cell bodies outside the central nervous system, such as a dorsal root ganglion; it does not wrap axons.", "d": "There is no Sylvian sheath; the name borrows from the Sylvian fissure (lateral sulcus), a deep groove on the surface of the brain."}}, {"q": "Miss. B tells her partner she needs more appreciation. What module of dialectical behaviour therapy is she working on?", "concept": "The answer is d: Miss B, asking her partner for more appreciation, is working on interpersonal effectiveness. Dialectical behaviour therapy (DBT) was developed by Marsha Linehan (1993) for chronically suicidal people with borderline personality disorder. \"Dialectical\" refers to balancing two opposites: accepting oneself as one is while working to change. DBT combines individual therapy, phone coaching, a therapist consultation team and a weekly skills group that teaches four modules. Mindfulness, the core module, teaches observing and describing the present moment without judgement and finding \"wise mind\" between reasonable mind and emotion mind. Distress tolerance teaches surviving a crisis without making it worse, through distraction, self-soothing and radical acceptance. Emotion regulation teaches naming emotions, reducing vulnerability to them and acting opposite to an unhelpful urge. Interpersonal effectiveness teaches asking for what you need and saying no while keeping both the relationship and your self-respect. Its best known skill is DEAR MAN: Describe the situation, Express feelings, Assert the request, Reinforce (explain the benefit to the other person), stay Mindful, Appear confident and Negotiate. GIVE (gentle, interested, validate, easy manner) protects the relationship, and FAST (fair, no needless apologies, stick to values, truthful) protects self-respect. Miss B might say, \"When you didn't mention the dinner I cooked, I felt invisible. I'd like you to tell me when you notice the effort I make; it would help me feel closer to you.\" Option d is right because making a clear request of another person is the heart of the interpersonal effectiveness module. Exam trap: emotion regulation (b) tempts because her need is emotional, but regulating her own feelings is internal work; telling someone else what she needs is interpersonal.", "traps": {"a": "Mindfulness is DBT's core module, observing and describing the present moment without judgement and finding wise mind; it does not involve making requests of others.", "b": "Emotion regulation teaches understanding and changing one's own emotions (for example through opposite action); it manages feelings internally rather than asking a partner for something.", "c": "Distress tolerance teaches getting through a crisis without making it worse, through distraction, self-soothing and radical acceptance; it is for surviving pain, not for negotiating a relationship."}}, {"q": "Mr. V always seems to focus on negative events in his life, even when something good happens for him. For example, when Mr. V got an A+ on his math test, he immediately assumed the test was too easy, otherwise he would not have gotten a good grade. Mr. V is most likely engaging in which type of cognitive distortion?", "concept": "The answer is b: Mr. V is disqualifying the positive, because he turns a good result (an A+) into something that does not count. A cognitive distortion is a habitual error in thinking that makes a person see events more negatively than the facts allow. Aaron Beck (1963) first described such errors in depressed patients, for example arbitrary inference, selective abstraction, overgeneralisation and magnification or minimisation. David Burns (1980), building on Beck in the self-help book Feeling Good, gave the list its everyday names, including 'disqualifying the positive'. In this distortion the person does not simply overlook good news; he actively explains it away, so it cannot challenge his negative belief about himself. 'The test was too easy', 'they were only being polite' and 'it was just luck' are typical lines. The result is that a negative self-view survives however much contrary evidence arrives, which helps keep depression going. In CBT the therapist helps the client catch the thought, weigh the evidence for and against it, and write a more balanced alternative. For example, when Riya's supervisor praises her case report and she thinks 'he says that to everyone', she is disqualifying the positive; a balanced reply would be 'he praised specific parts, so some of it really was good'. Option b is right because Mr. V meets a success and at once reinterprets it so that it says nothing good about him. Exam trap: examiners place catastrophising and all-or-nothing thinking beside it. Catastrophising predicts disaster from a small event; all-or-nothing thinking sorts things into two extremes. Neither involves dismissing a success. Also keep it apart from the mental filter, which only dwells on the negative, whereas disqualifying the positive actively converts good news into nothing.", "traps": {"a": "Catastrophising is expecting or magnifying the worst possible outcome ('if I fail, my life is over'); Mr. V is not predicting disaster, he is dismissing a success.", "c": "All-or-nothing (dichotomous) thinking sees things in two extremes, total success or total failure; Mr. V's error is explaining away good news, not black-and-white sorting.", "d": "Shaping is B. F. Skinner's (1953) operant technique of reinforcing successive approximations to a target behaviour; it is a learning procedure, not a cognitive distortion."}}, {"q": "The reality card of Rorschach:", "concept": "The answer is c: Card V is called the reality card of the Rorschach. Hermann Rorschach (1921), a Swiss psychiatrist, published ten symmetrical inkblots in Psychodiagnostik, always shown in the same fixed order. Five are achromatic (I, IV, V, VI, VII), two are black and red (II, III) and three are multicoloured (VIII, IX, X). The person says what each blot might be, and the examiner then scores location, determinants, content, form quality and whether the response is a popular one. Card V is a compact, solid black blot that almost everyone sees as a bat or a butterfly. It is the easiest card in the set: the outline is clear and there is no colour to unsettle the person. Because the popular response is so obvious, failing to see it, or giving a strange answer that does not fit the shape, raises a question about the person's reality testing, that is, how closely perception follows the actual stimulus. For example, if Kabir says 'a bat, here are the wings and the feelers' on Card V, his conventional perception is intact; if he says 'a cut-open stomach leaking', the clinician will look closely at his form quality on the other cards too. Option c is right because Card V draws the most common popular response, so it checks the person's hold on reality. Exam trap: other cards carry nicknames too, and examiners swap them. Card IV is the 'father card' (a large, heavy figure linked to authority), Card VII the 'mother card', and Card VI the 'sex card' because of its shape. These nicknames come from clinical tradition rather than from Rorschach's own scoring rules, so learn them as a set.", "traps": {"a": "Card VI is traditionally called the 'sex card', because its shape often draws sexual content; it is not the easiest card and is not used to check reality testing.", "b": "Card IV is the 'father card', a large dark figure often seen as a giant or monster and read for attitudes to authority; it is not the reality card.", "d": "Card VII is the 'mother card', often seen as the heads of women or children and read for attitudes to female figures; it is not the reality card."}}, {"q": "A researcher uses an experimental design to study the effect of music on memory. In this experiment, the Independent Variable is:", "concept": "The answer is a: music is the independent variable, the factor the researcher changes to see its effect. In an experiment the researcher manipulates one variable and measures another, while holding everything else constant. The independent variable (IV) is the one the experimenter manipulates or varies; its different values are called levels or conditions (for example, music versus silence, or classical versus rock versus none). The dependent variable (DV) is the outcome that is measured, the behaviour expected to 'depend' on the IV. Here the DV is memory, measured perhaps as the number of words recalled. Anything else that could affect recall, such as time of day, list difficulty or participants' age, is an extraneous variable that must be controlled, so that any difference in memory can be credited to the music. A simple rule: the IV is the cause the researcher sets, the DV is the effect the researcher records. Stems often follow the pattern 'the effect of IV on DV', and this one reads 'the effect of music (IV) on memory (DV)'. For example, Meera gives one group of students a word list to learn while Indian classical music plays and another group the same list in silence, then counts how many words each student recalls. She decided who heard music, so music is the IV; the recall scores are the DV. Option a is right because music is what the researcher manipulates, and memory is what is measured as the result. Exam trap: examiners reverse the pair and offer the DV (memory) as the IV. Another trick is 'both', which forgets that one variable cannot be both the manipulated cause and the measured outcome in the same experiment. Note too that a purely correlational study has no true IV, because nothing is manipulated.", "traps": {"b": "Memory is the dependent variable, the outcome the researcher measures (for example words recalled) to see whether the music changed it.", "c": "An experiment separates the manipulated cause from the measured effect; music and memory play different roles, so they cannot both be the IV.", "d": "This would fit only if nothing were manipulated; here music is deliberately varied (IV) and memory is measured (DV)."}}, {"q": "In a normal distribution, most scores are located:", "concept": "The answer is c: in a normal distribution most scores cluster in the centre, around the mean. The normal distribution, or normal probability curve, is a symmetrical, bell-shaped curve. Abraham de Moivre (1733) first derived it and Carl Friedrich Gauss (1809) used it for errors of measurement (hence 'Gaussian curve'). Its key properties are these: the mean, median and mode are equal and sit at the centre; the curve is symmetrical, so skewness is zero; the tails come ever closer to the baseline without touching it (asymptotic); and fixed proportions of scores fall within each standard deviation (SD) of the mean. About 68 per cent of scores lie within 1 SD either side of the mean, about 95 per cent within 2 SD and about 99.7 per cent within 3 SD. So most people are near average, and very high or very low scores are rare (on IQ, mean 100 and SD 15, about 68 per cent score 85 to 115). If Arjun's class takes a well-made test and the scores are normally distributed, most students will score near the class average, with a few very high and a few very low scorers out in the tails. Option c is right because the peak of the bell, where most cases fall, is the centre, where the mean, median and mode coincide. Exam trap: 'at the high end' or 'at the low end' describes a skewed distribution. In negative skew the scores pile up at the high end with a tail to the left (a very easy test); in positive skew they pile up at the low end with a tail to the right (a very hard test). The extremes, or tails, of a normal curve hold the fewest scores.", "traps": {"a": "Scores bunch at the high end in a negatively skewed distribution, such as results on a very easy test, not in a symmetrical normal curve.", "b": "Scores bunch at the low end in a positively skewed distribution, such as results on a very hard test; a normal curve peaks in the middle.", "d": "The extremes are the tails of the curve, beyond about 2 SD from the mean, where only around 5 per cent of scores fall in total."}}, {"q": "Damage to the hippocampus is most likely to impair:", "concept": "The answer is a: damage to the hippocampus mainly impairs memory, especially the ability to form new long-term memories. The hippocampus is a curved structure in the medial temporal lobe, named after the Greek word for seahorse, and it is part of the limbic system. Its main job is consolidation: turning new experiences and facts (declarative or explicit memory) into lasting long-term memories, which are later stored across the cortex. It also builds maps of space; John O'Keefe (1971) found place cells in the rat hippocampus that fire when the animal is in one particular location. The classic evidence is the patient H.M. (Henry Molaison). In 1953 the surgeon William Beecher Scoville removed much of both medial temporal lobes, including most of the hippocampus, to stop his epilepsy. Scoville and Brenda Milner (1957) reported severe anterograde amnesia: H.M. could not form new declarative memories, yet his intelligence, short-term memory, older memories and procedural learning were spared (he improved at mirror drawing while denying he had ever practised). So the hippocampus is needed for new explicit memories, not for every kind of memory. For example, if Kabir suffers hippocampal damage after encephalitis, he may chat normally and recall his childhood, but forget a visitor within minutes and fail to learn the route to his new ward. Option a is right because the hippocampus is the brain's key structure for consolidating new long-term memories. Exam trap: examiners offer the jobs of nearby structures. Balance belongs to the cerebellum and the vestibular system, sleep to the hypothalamus, reticular formation and pons, and breathing to the medulla. Also do not confuse the hippocampus (memory) with the amygdala (fear and emotional memory), its neighbour in the limbic system.", "traps": {"b": "Balance and coordination depend on the cerebellum and the vestibular system of the inner ear, not on the hippocampus.", "c": "Sleep and waking are controlled by the hypothalamus (including the suprachiasmatic nucleus), the reticular activating system and the pons; hippocampal damage does not mainly disturb sleep.", "d": "Breathing is run by the respiratory centre of the medulla, with help from the pons, so damage there, not to the hippocampus, threatens breathing."}}, {"q": "Which part of the limbic system is involved in an animal's feeling of hunger?", "concept": "The answer is d: the hypothalamus is the part of the limbic system that controls hunger. The hypothalamus is a small structure below the thalamus that keeps the body's internal balance (homeostasis): hunger, thirst, temperature, sex and the hormones of the pituitary. It is usually counted with the limbic system because it carries out the bodily side of emotion and motivation. Two of its regions were long taught as a dual centre for eating. Albert Hetherington and Stephen Ranson (1940) found that lesions of the ventromedial hypothalamus (VMH) made rats overeat and grow obese, so the VMH was called the satiety centre. Then the Indian physiologist Bal Krishan Anand, working with John Brobeck (1951), showed that lesions of the lateral hypothalamus (LH) made rats stop eating (aphagia) and drinking, to the point of starving unless fed by tube; stimulating the LH made them eat. So the LH became the feeding centre, and Eliot Stellar (1954) built both findings into the dual-centre theory. Modern work adds hormones that act on the hypothalamus, especially its arcuate nucleus: ghrelin from the empty stomach signals hunger, while leptin from fat stores and insulin from the pancreas signal fullness. Option d is right because hunger and satiety signals are integrated in the hypothalamus, chiefly its lateral and ventromedial regions. Exam trap: the classic pairing is easy to reverse. Lateral hypothalamus damage stops eating (it is the feeding or 'start' centre); ventromedial damage causes overeating (it is the satiety or 'stop' centre). Also, the thalamus sounds similar but is a sensory relay, not a drive centre.", "traps": {"a": "The thalamus is the sensory relay station (every sense except smell passes through it to the cortex); it does not regulate hunger.", "b": "The pons is a brainstem structure that relays signals to the cerebellum and helps control breathing and REM sleep; it is not a limbic hunger centre.", "c": "The hippocampus is the limbic structure for forming new long-term memories and spatial maps, not for hunger."}}, {"q": "Which area of the brain-stem controls heart rate and breathing?", "concept": "The answer is b: the medulla oblongata controls heart rate and breathing. The brainstem has three parts: the medulla (lowest, where it joins the spinal cord), the pons above it and the midbrain on top. The medulla holds the vital centres: the cardiovascular centre, which adjusts heart rate and blood pressure, and the respiratory centre, which generates the basic rhythm of breathing. It also runs reflexes such as swallowing, coughing, sneezing and vomiting. These centres work automatically, so the heart keeps beating and breathing continues even in deep sleep or coma. This is why damage to the medulla is the brain injury most likely to kill, and why overdoses of alcohol, opioids or barbiturates kill mainly by silencing its respiratory centre. The medulla is also where the main motor pathways (the pyramids) cross from one side to the other, so each hemisphere controls the opposite side of the body. For example, if Arjun suffers a severe blow to the base of the skull and swelling presses on the medulla, doctors watch his breathing and pulse most closely, because those functions fail first. Option b is right because the vital centres for heartbeat and respiration lie in the medulla. Exam trap: the pons also helps with breathing, since its centres fine-tune the rhythm, which tempts students to pick c. But the basic, life-sustaining control of heart rate and breathing is the medulla's. The reticular formation runs through the whole brainstem core and governs arousal and alertness, not heart rate as such.", "traps": {"a": "The reticular formation is a network through the brainstem core whose ascending part (the reticular activating system, Giuseppe Moruzzi and Horace Magoun, 1949) controls arousal and wakefulness, not the vital centres.", "c": "The pons ('bridge') relays signals from the cortex to the cerebellum and helps fine-tune breathing and REM sleep, but the basic heart and breathing centres are in the medulla.", "d": "The thalamus is a forebrain (diencephalon) relay for sensory information, not part of the brainstem, and it does not control heart rate."}}, {"q": "Which structure allows communication between the hemispheres?", "concept": "The answer is d: the corpus callosum is the band of fibres that lets the two cerebral hemispheres communicate. It is the largest commissure (crossing pathway) in the brain, a thick bundle of about 200 million axons of white matter lying in the midline above the thalamus. The smaller anterior commissure also links the two sides. Through the corpus callosum, what one hemisphere perceives or learns is quickly shared with the other. The classic evidence comes from split-brain patients. To control severe epilepsy, surgeons cut the corpus callosum. Roger Sperry and Michael Gazzaniga (1962 onwards) then flashed pictures to only one visual field. When an object was shown to the right hemisphere (left visual field), the patient could not name it, because speech is usually in the left hemisphere, yet his left hand could pick the object out by touch. Each hemisphere was now working on its own. Sperry shared the Nobel Prize in 1981 for this work. For example, if Meera, a split-brain patient, holds a key in her left hand out of sight, she can find it again among other objects with that hand but cannot say 'key', because the information stays in her right hemisphere. Option d is right because the corpus callosum is the main pathway joining and integrating the two hemispheres. Exam trap: 'contralateral bridge' is an invented term built from two real ideas (contralateral means opposite side; pons means bridge) so that it sounds plausible. The temporal lobe is a region inside each hemisphere, and the reticular formation is a brainstem arousal network; neither connects the hemispheres.", "traps": {"a": "The reticular formation is a network in the brainstem core that controls arousal, attention and sleep-wake transitions; it does not link the hemispheres.", "b": "The temporal lobe is one of the four lobes within each hemisphere, handling hearing, language comprehension and (with the hippocampus) memory; it is not a connecting pathway.", "c": "'Contralateral bridge' is not a real brain structure; the name borrows from the pons ('bridge') and from the fact that each hemisphere controls the opposite (contralateral) side."}}, {"q": "__________ is the ability to sense the position and movement of our body parts.", "concept": "The answer is b: proprioception is the sense of the position and movement of our own body parts. The word comes from Latin proprius, 'one's own', plus reception. Charles Sherrington (1906), in The Integrative Action of the Nervous System, coined the term for signals arising from within the body's own tissues. The receptors, called proprioceptors, sit in muscles (muscle spindles, which sense stretch), tendons (Golgi tendon organs, which sense tension) and joints. Their signals travel up the spinal cord in the dorsal column pathway to the thalamus and somatosensory cortex, and also to the cerebellum, which uses them to make movement smooth and accurate. Proprioception is often grouped with kinaesthesis (the sense of movement), while the vestibular sense of the inner ear adds balance and head position. Together they let us act without watching our limbs. For example, Riya can touch her nose with her eyes closed, or climb stairs while reading a message on her phone, because proprioceptors tell her brain where her hand and feet are. The neurologist's finger-to-nose test checks exactly this. People who lose proprioception through nerve damage can still move, but they must watch their limbs constantly to control them. Option b is right because proprioception is the established name for sensing body position and movement from receptors in muscles, tendons and joints. Exam trap: the other three options are invented words built on the same 'proprio-' stem to look familiar. Also do not confuse proprioception with the vestibular sense (balance and head motion from the semicircular canals and otolith organs) or with interoception (sensing the internal state of the organs, such as hunger or a racing heart).", "traps": {"a": "'Proprio-action' is not a psychological term; it borrows the stem of proprioception and adds 'action' to sound like movement.", "c": "'Proprioliction' is a made-up word with no meaning in psychology or physiology.", "d": "'Proprioprediction' is also invented; it echoes the idea of predicting movement, but it is not the name of the body-position sense."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is NOT a set of opponent colours according to opponent process theory?", "concept": "The answer is a: red and blue are not an opponent pair; the three opponent pairs are red-green, blue-yellow and black-white. Two classic theories explain colour vision. The trichromatic (Young-Helmholtz) theory, from Thomas Young (1802) and Hermann von Helmholtz (1852), says the retina has three receptor types, most sensitive to short (blue), medium (green) and long (red) wavelengths, and that colour is coded by the ratio of their activity. This works at the level of the cones. Ewald Hering (1878) noticed what this could not explain: nobody sees a 'reddish green' or a 'bluish yellow', and staring at red leaves a green afterimage. He proposed opponent processes: three channels, each signalling one of two opposing colours. A cell excited by red is inhibited by green, one excited by blue is inhibited by yellow, and a third codes black versus white (brightness). Russell De Valois (1966) later recorded such opponent cells in the lateral geniculate nucleus of monkeys. Today the two theories are joined in a dual-process view: trichromatic at the cones, opponent coding in later retinal and thalamic cells. For example, if Kabir stares at a red circle for 30 seconds and then looks at a white wall, he sees a green circle, because the fatigued red side of the channel lets its opponent, green, dominate. Option a is right because red's opponent is green, not blue, so red/blue is not one of Hering's pairs. Exam trap: red, green and blue are the three primaries of the trichromatic theory, so students mix up the two theories and pick a primary pair. Remember: trichromatic names three colours (red, green, blue); opponent process names three pairs (red-green, blue-yellow, black-white).", "traps": {"b": "Red/green is Hering's first opponent pair: cells excited by red are inhibited by green, so it is a genuine opponent set.", "c": "Yellow/blue is Hering's second opponent pair, which is why no one sees a 'bluish yellow'.", "d": "White/black is the third, achromatic opponent channel that codes brightness, so it is part of Hering's theory."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is a Central Nervous System (CNS) depressant?", "concept": "The answer is a: alcohol is a central nervous system (CNS) depressant. Psychoactive drugs are grouped by their main effect on the brain: depressants slow neural activity, stimulants speed it up, opioids relieve pain and produce euphoria, and hallucinogens distort perception. Depressants, also called sedative-hypnotics, include alcohol, benzodiazepines (such as diazepam) and barbiturates. Alcohol works mainly by enhancing the inhibitory transmitter GABA at the GABA-A receptor and by blocking excitatory glutamate (NMDA) receptors. At low doses it reduces inhibitions, so people may seem lively and talkative, which is why many people wrongly think it is a stimulant. That early 'lift' is disinhibition: alcohol first depresses the brain's restraining circuits. Higher doses bring slurred speech, poor coordination, slow reactions and memory blackouts, and very high doses bring coma and death by suppressing the medulla's breathing centre. Mixing alcohol with other depressants is especially dangerous, because their effects add up. For example, if Arjun drinks heavily at a wedding and then takes a sleeping pill (a benzodiazepine), both drugs boost GABA, and his breathing can slow to a dangerous level. Option a is right because alcohol slows CNS activity through GABA enhancement, the defining feature of a depressant. Exam trap: the cheerful, talkative phase of drinking makes alcohol look like a stimulant, but it is disinhibition, not stimulation. Caffeine and nicotine are stimulants, and LSD is a hallucinogen. Note also that 'depressant' means slowing the nervous system, not causing clinical depression.", "traps": {"b": "Nicotine is a stimulant: it acts on nicotinic acetylcholine receptors and releases dopamine, raising heart rate and alertness.", "c": "LSD (lysergic acid diethylamide, first made by Albert Hofmann in 1938) is a hallucinogen acting on serotonin 5-HT2A receptors; it distorts perception rather than slowing the CNS.", "d": "Caffeine is a stimulant that blocks adenosine receptors, so it reduces drowsiness and increases alertness."}}, {"q": "_____ waves are to wakefulness as _____ waves are to deep sleep.", "concept": "The answer is c: alpha waves go with (relaxed) wakefulness and delta waves with deep sleep. The electroencephalogram (EEG) records the brain's electrical activity through electrodes on the scalp. Hans Berger (1929) recorded the first human EEG and named the alpha rhythm, the regular wave seen when a person is awake but relaxed with eyes closed (about 8 to 12 Hz; some texts give 8 to 13). When the eyes open or the person concentrates, alpha gives way to faster, low-amplitude beta waves (about 13 to 30 Hz) of alert, active thinking. As sleep begins (N1), theta waves (4 to 7 Hz) appear; N2 shows sleep spindles and K-complexes; and in N3, the deepest slow-wave sleep, the EEG is dominated by large, slow delta waves (below 4 Hz). REM sleep returns to a fast, low-voltage, waking-like pattern. So the analogy pairs the classic waking rhythm with the classic deep-sleep rhythm. For example, when Meera lies back with her eyes closed before an exam, her EEG shows alpha; an hour into the night, in deep sleep, it shows slow delta waves and she is very hard to wake. Option c is right because alpha is the hallmark rhythm of waking rest and delta is the hallmark of deep (N3) sleep. Exam trap: theta (option d) belongs to drowsiness and light N1 sleep, not deep sleep, and option a pairs two waking rhythms. Key note: beta is also a waking rhythm, so option b (beta, delta) can be argued, since alert wakefulness shows beta. The paper's pairing of alpha, the classic resting waking rhythm that Berger first described, with delta stands.", "traps": {"a": "Alpha and beta are both waking rhythms (relaxed and alert); beta is not a deep-sleep wave, so the second half fails.", "b": "This pairing is defensible, since beta marks alert waking and delta marks deep sleep, but the paper takes alpha, Hans Berger's classic waking rhythm, as the intended first term (see Key note).", "d": "Beta marks waking, but theta (4 to 7 Hz) marks drowsiness and light N1 sleep, not deep sleep."}}, {"q": "The body's primary circadian pacemaker is the:", "concept": "The answer is c: the suprachiasmatic nucleus (SCN) is the body's primary circadian pacemaker. A circadian rhythm (Latin circa diem, 'about a day') is a cycle of roughly 24 hours, such as sleep and waking, body temperature and hormone release. The SCN is a tiny pair of nuclei in the hypothalamus, sitting just above the optic chiasm, which gives it its name. Light reaches it directly from the retina along the retinohypothalamic tract, so daylight resets the clock each day; such resetting cues are called zeitgebers ('time givers'). The SCN in turn signals the pineal gland to release melatonin in darkness, which promotes sleep. The evidence is strong. In 1972 two teams (Robert Moore with Victor Eichler, and Friedrich Stephan with Irving Zucker) found that destroying the SCN in rats abolished their daily rhythms: the rats slept the usual total amount, but at random times. Martin Ralph (1990) and colleagues transplanted SCN tissue between hamsters, and the recipients took on the donor's rhythm. Even isolated SCN cells keep firing in a daily cycle, so the clock is built in. For example, when Kabir flies from Delhi to New York, his SCN is still set to Indian time, so he is wide awake at 3 a.m.; jet lag lasts until daylight resets his clock. Option c is right because the SCN generates the master 24-hour rhythm and keeps the body's other clocks in step. Exam trap: the pineal gland is the usual distractor because it secretes melatonin, the 'darkness hormone'. But the pineal only carries out the SCN's timing signal; the pacemaker itself is the SCN.", "traps": {"a": "The pineal gland secretes melatonin at night on the SCN's signal; it is an output of the clock, not the master pacemaker.", "b": "The hippocampus is a limbic structure for consolidating memories and building spatial maps, not for timing daily rhythms.", "d": "The amygdala is the limbic centre for fear, aggression and emotional memory; it plays no pacemaker role."}}, {"q": "What did Freud call the hidden psychological content of a dream?", "concept": "The answer is c: Freud called the hidden psychological meaning of a dream its latent content. Sigmund Freud (1900), in The Interpretation of Dreams, called dreams 'the royal road to a knowledge of the unconscious'. He held that every dream is a disguised wish fulfilment. During sleep the ego's guard relaxes and repressed wishes, often sexual or aggressive, press for expression; because they would disturb sleep if shown openly, a mental censor disguises them. Freud therefore split the dream into two layers. The manifest content is the dream as the dreamer remembers and reports it, the surface story. The latent content is the hidden meaning beneath, the unconscious wishes and thoughts. The process that turns latent into manifest is the dream work: condensation (several ideas fused into one image), displacement (emotion shifted from an important object to a trivial one), symbolisation (thoughts shown as concrete images) and secondary revision (tidying the result into a coherent story). The analyst works backwards, using the dreamer's free associations to each element, to uncover the latent content. For example, Riya dreams that she misses a train while her younger brother boards it. The train story is the manifest content; in a Freudian reading the latent content might be her hidden resentment that her brother gets chances she does not. Option c is right because 'latent' means hidden, and Freud used it for the disguised unconscious wish behind the dream. Exam trap: examiners reverse the two terms. Manifest means shown openly (the remembered storyline); latent means lying hidden. 'Literal' and 'soporific' (sleep-inducing) are not Freud's dream categories at all.", "traps": {"a": "Manifest content is Freud's term for the surface of the dream, the storyline the dreamer remembers and reports, not its hidden meaning.", "b": "'Literal content' is not a term in Freud's dream theory; it loosely suggests the surface meaning, which Freud called manifest content.", "d": "'Soporific' means sleep-inducing; it is not a Freudian category of dream content."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is NOT associated with REM sleep?", "concept": "The answer is a: decreased limbic system activity is not a feature of REM sleep; in REM the limbic system, especially the amygdala, becomes more active. Eugene Aserinsky and Nathaniel Kleitman (1953) noticed bursts of rapid eye movements in sleepers and, with William Dement, linked them to dreaming: people woken from REM usually report vivid, story-like dreams. REM is called paradoxical sleep because the brain looks awake while the body is deeply asleep. Its main signs are rapid eye movements; a fast, low-voltage EEG like waking; faster and irregular heart rate, blood pressure and breathing; genital arousal (penile erection and clitoral engorgement) in almost every REM period, whatever the dream content; and atonia, near-paralysis of the skeletal muscles, which stops us acting out our dreams. Brain imaging (Pierre Maquet and colleagues, 1996) shows that in REM the limbic and paralimbic areas, including the amygdala and anterior cingulate, are highly active, while parts of the dorsolateral prefrontal cortex are quieter. This helps explain why REM dreams are emotional, often fearful, yet illogical and accepted without question. For example, if Arjun is woken during REM, his heart may be racing and he may describe a vivid chase dream; woken from deep N3 sleep, he is groggy and reports little. Option a is right because the limbic system is more, not less, active in REM sleep. Exam trap: students hear 'sleep' and assume everything slows down. That is true of N3 (slow heart rate, regular breathing, delta waves), but REM is a highly activated state. What switches off in REM is the motor output to the muscles, and partly the prefrontal cortex, not the emotional brain.", "traps": {"b": "Increased, irregular heart rate is a genuine REM feature, part of the aroused, waking-like body state of paradoxical sleep.", "c": "Genital arousal (penile and clitoral engorgement) accompanies nearly every REM period, so it is associated with REM.", "d": "Dreaming, especially vivid story-like dreaming, is most strongly linked to REM, as Aserinsky, Kleitman and William Dement showed."}}, {"q": "_____ parenting combines high responsiveness with low demandingness.", "concept": "The answer is b: permissive parenting combines high responsiveness (warmth) with low demandingness (control). Diana Baumrind (1966, 1971) observed preschool children at home and at nursery school, interviewed their parents, and described three parenting styles. Eleanor Maccoby and John Martin (1983) then placed the styles on two independent dimensions: demandingness (rules, supervision, expecting mature behaviour) and responsiveness (warmth, acceptance, attention to the child's needs). Crossing the two gives four styles. Authoritative parenting is high on both: warm, with clear rules that are explained. Authoritarian parenting is high in demands but low in warmth: strict obedience, 'because I said so', and often harsh punishment. Permissive (indulgent) parenting is high in warmth but low in demands: loving and accepting, with few rules or expectations. Rejecting-neglecting (uninvolved) parenting, added by Maccoby and Martin, is low on both. In many Western samples authoritative parenting predicts the best outcomes (self-reliance, school success, social skill); children of permissive parents tend to be more impulsive, less self-controlled and less persistent, though often confident. For example, Kabir's parents adore him and praise everything he does, but let him skip homework, stay up as late as he likes and eat whatever he wants: that is permissive parenting. Option b is right because high responsiveness with low demandingness is the exact definition of the permissive style. Exam trap: permissive and neglectful parents both set few limits, so students confuse them; the difference is warmth, high in permissive and low in neglectful. Likewise authoritative and authoritarian look alike on the page: authoritative is high on both dimensions, authoritarian is high demand with low warmth.", "traps": {"a": "Authoritative parenting is high in both responsiveness and demandingness, warm but with firm, explained rules; Diana Baumrind linked it to the best outcomes.", "c": "Rejecting-neglecting (uninvolved) parenting, added by Eleanor Maccoby and John Martin (1983), is low in both responsiveness and demandingness.", "d": "Authoritarian parenting is high in demandingness but low in responsiveness, stressing obedience and punishment without warmth."}}, {"q": "The \"strange situation\" is a test of:", "concept": "The answer is a: the Strange Situation is a laboratory test of the attachment between an infant and a caregiver. John Bowlby (1969) argued that infants are biologically prepared to form an attachment, a lasting emotional bond, to a caregiver who serves as a secure base. Mary Ainsworth (1969), who had worked with Bowlby, devised the Strange Situation with Barbara Wittig to measure the quality of that bond; the full account came in Patterns of Attachment (1978). A 12 to 18 month old passes through eight short episodes of about three minutes each: exploring a playroom with the caregiver, meeting a stranger, being left with the stranger, a reunion, being left alone, the stranger returning, and a second reunion. Observers score exploration, distress at separation, reaction to the stranger and, above all, behaviour at reunion. Ainsworth found three patterns: secure (type B, about two thirds of her sample: upset when the mother leaves, quickly comforted on her return), insecure-avoidant (type A: little distress, ignores the mother on reunion) and insecure-resistant or ambivalent (type C: very distressed, seeks contact yet angrily resists it). Mary Main and Judith Solomon (1986) added a disorganised pattern (type D). For example, if baby Meera cries when her mother leaves but crawls to her and settles within seconds on reunion, she is classed as securely attached. Option a is right because the procedure was designed specifically to classify the quality of infant attachment. Exam trap: two distractors are Jean Piaget's (1936) terms. Assimilation and accommodation are processes of cognitive adaptation and have nothing to do with the Strange Situation. Also, the Strange Situation is Ainsworth's, not Bowlby's or Harry Harlow's (1958), even though all three studied attachment.", "traps": {"b": "Accommodation is Jean Piaget's process of changing an existing scheme to fit new information; it is a cognitive concept, not what the Strange Situation measures.", "c": "Assimilation is Piaget's process of fitting new information into an existing scheme; it belongs to cognitive development, not attachment.", "d": "Arbitration is a legal or negotiation term for settling a dispute through a neutral third party; it has no link to infant testing."}}, {"q": "Piaget believed that object permanence is typically attained during which stage?", "concept": "The answer is d: Piaget believed object permanence is attained in the sensorimotor stage, from birth to about 2 years. Jean Piaget (1936, 1954) described four stages of cognitive development: sensorimotor (birth to 2), preoperational (2 to 7), concrete operational (7 to 11) and formal operational (11 or 12 onwards). Object permanence is the understanding that objects go on existing when they cannot be seen, heard or touched. Piaget tested it by hiding a toy under a cloth while the infant watched. Before about 8 months, an infant who sees a toy covered acts as if it has vanished ('out of sight, out of mind'). In substage 4 (8 to 12 months) the infant searches for a fully hidden object but makes the A-not-B error: after finding the toy under cloth A several times, she keeps searching at A even after watching it being moved to B. By substage 6 (18 to 24 months), with mental representation, she can follow invisible moves and the concept is complete. Later work, notably Renée Baillargeon's (1987) looking-time studies, found signs of object permanence as early as 3 to 4 months, suggesting Piaget underestimated infants; but the stage he placed it in is still the sensorimotor. For example, at 6 months baby Arjun stops reaching when his rattle is covered with a towel; at 10 months he pulls the towel off to get it. Option d is right because object permanence is the major achievement of Piaget's sensorimotor period. Exam trap: examiners offer preoperational because object permanence is completed near age 2, the boundary. Conservation, not object permanence, is the hallmark of the concrete operational stage, and abstract hypothetical thinking marks formal operations.", "traps": {"a": "Formal operations (11 or 12 years onwards) is the stage of abstract, hypothetical and propositional reasoning; object permanence was achieved about a decade earlier.", "b": "Concrete operations (7 to 11 years) is marked by conservation, reversibility and classification, not object permanence.", "c": "The preoperational stage (2 to 7) begins with object permanence already in place; it is marked by symbolic play, egocentrism and lack of conservation."}}, {"q": "Fluid intelligence tends to _____ with age while crystallized intelligence tends to _____ with age.", "concept": "The answer is c: fluid intelligence tends to decrease with age, while crystallised intelligence tends to increase. Raymond Cattell (1963), later with his student John Horn (1966), split general intelligence into two broad abilities. Fluid intelligence (Gf) is the capacity to reason and solve new problems that do not depend on learned knowledge: seeing patterns, abstract reasoning, working out unfamiliar tasks. It is measured by tests such as John Raven's Progressive Matrices (1938). It relies heavily on processing speed and working memory, peaks in early adulthood (around the early twenties) and then declines gradually. Crystallised intelligence (Gc) is the store of knowledge, vocabulary and skills gained through education and experience, measured by vocabulary and general information tests. It keeps rising into late middle age and holds up well into the sixties. In Cattell's investment theory, fluid ability is 'invested' in learning, and what is learned crystallises as Gc. Longitudinal work such as K. Warner Schaie's Seattle Longitudinal Study (begun 1956) broadly supports the pattern, though most declines come later and are smaller than early cross-sectional studies suggested. For example, Meera's 68-year-old grandfather beats her easily at crosswords and knows far more history, but she solves a new puzzle app or a matrix-reasoning test much faster. Option c is right because Gf falls from early adulthood while Gc continues to grow with experience. Exam trap: the pair is easy to reverse; remember 'fluid flows away, crystals build up'. Option d is tempting because Gc does level off and finally dips in very old age, but across most of adulthood it rises.", "traps": {"a": "This reverses the pattern: fluid reasoning falls after early adulthood, and crystallised knowledge accumulates rather than declines.", "b": "Fluid intelligence does not increase across adulthood; it peaks early and then declines.", "d": "The first half is right, but crystallised intelligence keeps rising into late middle age rather than staying flat."}}, {"q": "If a proposed intelligence test turned out to really be a test of patience rather than intelligence, it would be criticized for having:", "concept": "The answer is b: a test that claims to measure intelligence but really measures patience has low construct validity. Validity is the degree to which a test measures what it claims to measure. Lee Cronbach and Paul Meehl (1955) introduced construct validity for tests of abstract qualities (constructs) such as intelligence, anxiety or self-esteem, which cannot be observed directly. A test has construct validity when its scores behave as the theory of the construct predicts: they correlate with other measures of the same construct (convergent validity), do not correlate strongly with measures of different constructs (discriminant validity, Donald Campbell and Donald Fiske, 1959), and differ in expected ways between groups. If an 'intelligence' test really tracks how long people are willing to persist, its scores will correlate with patience measures rather than reasoning measures, so it captures the wrong construct. The test may still be highly reliable, because a patient person will score high every time. Reliability means consistency; validity means the scores mean what they claim. A test can be reliable without being valid, but not valid without being reliable. For example, suppose Kabir designs an 'IQ test' made of long, boring sorting tasks with no time limit, so those who stick at it longest score highest. Scores are stable from week to week, but they reflect persistence, not reasoning: the test is reliable yet lacks construct validity. Option b is right because the test measures a different construct (patience) from the one it claims to measure (intelligence). Exam trap: examiners offer reliability or consistency, which concern whether scores are stable, not what they mean. 'Consistent validity' is not a recognised type of validity; it blends two terms to look familiar.", "traps": {"a": "Low reliability means inconsistent scores across occasions, forms or items; a patience test could give very consistent scores and still measure the wrong thing.", "c": "Consistency (internal consistency, as in Lee Cronbach's 1951 coefficient alpha) is a form of reliability, about items hanging together, not about what the test measures.", "d": "'Consistent validity' is not a standard term in psychometrics; it blends reliability and validity vocabulary into a distractor."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is not a cause of forgetting?", "concept": "The answer is c: selective attention is not a cause of forgetting, because information that was never attended was never stored, so it cannot be forgotten. Forgetting means losing access to information that was once encoded into memory. The main explanations are these. Decay (trace decay) theory says memory traces fade with time and disuse, as in Edward Thorndike's (1914) law of disuse. Interference theory says other learning disrupts recall; John McGeoch (1932) argued that interference, not the mere passing of time, causes most forgetting. In proactive interference old learning blocks new learning; in retroactive interference new learning disrupts old. Retrieval failure, or cue-dependent forgetting (Endel Tulving, 1974), means the memory is stored but the right cue is missing, as in the tip-of-the-tongue state. Retrieval inhibition means recalling some items actively suppresses related ones; Michael Anderson (1994), with Robert and Elizabeth Bjork, called this retrieval-induced forgetting. Motivated forgetting (repression) and failed consolidation complete the list. Selective attention, by contrast, works at the very start, at encoding: it decides what enters memory in the first place, as in Donald Broadbent's (1958) filter model. Unattended material is never learned, so failing to recall it is a failure to encode, not forgetting. For example, if Riya was texting during a lecture and cannot recall its main point, she never encoded it; if she learned it well but cannot recall it a month later because of newer lectures on similar topics, that is interference. Option c is right because selective attention governs what gets encoded, not why stored information is later lost. Exam trap: retrieval inhibition sounds like an attention process, but it is a genuine forgetting mechanism that acts on memories already stored.", "traps": {"a": "Interference (John McGeoch, 1932) is a major cause of forgetting: proactive and retroactive interference make stored memories harder to recall.", "b": "Decay theory holds that unused memory traces fade over time (Edward Thorndike's law of disuse), so it is a classic explanation of forgetting.", "d": "Retrieval inhibition is a real cause of forgetting: recalling some items suppresses related ones, shown by Michael Anderson (1994) as retrieval-induced forgetting."}}, {"q": "In a 3 x 4 contingency table, using Chi-square test, the degrees of freedom associated with the Chi-square will be:", "concept": "The answer is d: a 3 x 4 contingency table has 6 degrees of freedom. The chi-square test, developed by Karl Pearson (1900), compares observed frequencies with the frequencies expected if there were no relationship. In the test of independence, two categorical variables are cross-tabulated in a contingency table, and chi-square asks whether they are associated. Degrees of freedom (df) are the number of values that are free to vary once certain totals are fixed. In a contingency table the row and column totals are fixed, so df = (rows minus 1) x (columns minus 1). Working: (3 minus 1) x (4 minus 1) = 2 x 3 = 6. The logic is that in each row, once all but one cell are filled, the last cell is set by the row total, and in each column the last cell is set by the column total, so only a 2 by 3 block of cells is free. Ronald Fisher (1922) showed that this is the correct df for a contingency table, correcting Pearson's earlier rule. For a goodness-of-fit test on one variable with k categories, df = k minus 1 instead. For example, Arjun classifies 300 students by three streams (arts, commerce, science) and four preferred study times (morning, afternoon, evening, night). His table is 3 x 4, so he looks up the critical chi-square for df = 6, which is 12.59 at the .05 level. Option d is right because (3 minus 1) x (4 minus 1) = 6. Exam trap: 12 (option a) is the number of cells, 3 x 4, not the degrees of freedom. 7 comes from adding rows and columns, and 9 from subtracting one from only one dimension (3 x 3). Always subtract one from each dimension, then multiply.", "traps": {"a": "12 is the number of cells in a 3 x 4 table; degrees of freedom subtract one from each dimension before multiplying.", "b": "9 results from subtracting one from only one dimension (3 x 3); the correct formula subtracts one from both rows and columns.", "c": "7 is the sum of rows and columns (3 plus 4); df uses the product of (rows minus 1) and (columns minus 1), not a sum."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is classed as a basic emotion?", "concept": "The answer is d: disgust is classed as a basic emotion. Basic emotions are thought to be innate, universal across cultures and quick to arise, each with its own facial expression and bodily pattern. Charles Darwin (1872), in The Expression of the Emotions in Man and Animals, first argued that emotional expressions evolved and are shared across humans and animals. Paul Ekman (1972) and Wallace Friesen tested this by showing photographs of faces to people in many countries, including the Fore of Papua New Guinea, who had little contact with outsiders. Six expressions were recognised everywhere: happiness, sadness, fear, anger, surprise and disgust; contempt was added later. Robert Plutchik (1980) proposed eight primary emotions on a wheel (joy, trust, fear, surprise, sadness, disgust, anger, anticipation). Disgust appears on both lists. It evolved to protect us from contamination and disease, with its wrinkled nose, raised upper lip and urge to pull away from rotten food. Guilt, shame and jealousy are different. They are self-conscious or complex emotions which, as Michael Lewis (1992) described, appear only from the second year of life onwards, once the child has a sense of self and some grasp of social rules, and they lack a single universal facial expression. For example, baby Kabir screws up his face at bitter medicine in his first months (disgust), but shows guilt after breaking a vase only as a toddler who understands rules. Option d is right because disgust is one of Ekman's six universal basic emotions. Exam trap: guilt and shame are strong, common feelings, so they sound basic, but they depend on self-evaluation against social standards, which makes them secondary emotions. Carroll Izard (1977) did list shame and guilt among his fundamental emotions, yet disgust is the only option found on every list.", "traps": {"a": "Guilt is a self-conscious emotion (Michael Lewis) that needs a sense of self and knowledge of rules, so it appears later in childhood and is not among Ekman's six.", "b": "Shame is a self-conscious emotion based on a negative judgement of the whole self; it is not one of Ekman's six, though Carroll Izard's (1977) longer list includes it.", "c": "Jealousy is a complex social emotion felt when a rival threatens a valued relationship; it blends fear, anger and sadness and has no universal face."}}, {"q": "Which 3 structures make up the HPA axis?", "concept": "The answer is d: the HPA axis is made up of the hypothalamus, the pituitary gland and the adrenal glands. HPA stands for hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal, and it is the body's slower, hormonal stress system. When the brain appraises a threat, the hypothalamus (its paraventricular nucleus) releases corticotropin-releasing hormone (CRH). CRH travels to the anterior pituitary, which releases adrenocorticotropic hormone (ACTH) into the blood. ACTH reaches the adrenal cortex, the outer layer of the adrenal glands above the kidneys, which releases cortisol, the main human stress hormone. Cortisol raises blood sugar, supplies energy and damps down inflammation; it then feeds back on the hypothalamus and pituitary to switch the system off (negative feedback). The faster system is the sympathetic-adrenal-medullary pathway, in which sympathetic nerves make the adrenal medulla release adrenaline within seconds; Walter Cannon (1915) called this the fight-or-flight response. Hans Selye (1936, 1956) described the general adaptation syndrome of alarm, resistance and exhaustion, and the HPA axis sustains the resistance stage. Long-term high cortisol harms health, including the immune system and the hippocampus. For example, during weeks of exam preparation Meera's HPA axis keeps her cortisol high; she copes, but she catches every cold going, because chronic cortisol suppresses immunity. Option d is right because the axis is named after its three linked glands: hypothalamus, pituitary and adrenal. Exam trap: the initials tempt students towards any option starting with H, P and A. Hippocampus, parietal lobe and parasympathetic nervous system begin with the right letters but are not stages of the hormone chain. The hippocampus helps regulate the axis by feedback, but it is not one of its three parts.", "traps": {"a": "The pineal gland secretes melatonin for sleep timing, and 'anterior gyrus' is not a gland; only the hypothalamus here belongs to the HPA axis.", "b": "The hippocampus helps switch off the stress response by feedback, but the parietal lobe (body sense and space) and 'affective cortex' are not part of the axis.", "c": "The parasympathetic nervous system calms the body after stress (rest and digest), and the adenoids are lymphoid tissue in the throat; neither forms the HPA axis."}}, {"q": "According to Eysenck, extraverts seek to _____ their arousal while introverts seek to _____ their arousal.", "concept": "The answer is c: according to Hans Eysenck, extraverts seek to increase their arousal, while introverts seek to decrease it. Hans Eysenck (1967), in The Biological Basis of Personality, linked his extraversion-introversion dimension to the brain. He proposed that the ascending reticular activating system (ARAS) in the brainstem sets each person's baseline level of cortical arousal, and that everyone feels and performs best at a moderate, optimal level, an idea close to the Yerkes-Dodson law (Robert Yerkes and John Dodson, 1908). Introverts, Eysenck argued, have a higher resting arousal, so they already sit near or above their optimum; extra stimulation such as noise, crowds or novelty overloads them, and they choose quiet settings to bring arousal down. Extraverts have a lower resting arousal, so they are under-aroused and seek parties, risk, variety and loud music to push it up. The evidence fits: in the lemon juice test, introverts salivate more than extraverts when drops of lemon juice are placed on the tongue, showing stronger physiological reactivity, and introverts are also more sensitive to pain and faint stimuli and condition more easily. Jeffrey Gray (1970) later revised the biology in his reinforcement sensitivity theory. For example, after a long week Arjun, an extravert, recharges at a crowded cricket match, while his introverted friend Kabir recharges by reading alone in a quiet room. Option c is right because extraverts are chronically under-aroused and seek stimulation to raise arousal, while introverts are over-aroused and avoid it. Exam trap: it seems natural to assume extraverts are 'more aroused' because they look lively. In Eysenck's theory the opposite is true: their lively behaviour makes up for low cortical arousal.", "traps": {"a": "This reverses Eysenck's theory: introverts already have high cortical arousal and avoid more, while extraverts are under-aroused and seek more.", "b": "'Hide' and 'reveal' describe self-disclosure, not arousal; Eysenck's theory concerns the level of cortical arousal set by the reticular activating system.", "d": "'Reveal' and 'hide' again confuse social openness with arousal; Eysenck spoke of raising or lowering arousal towards an optimal level."}}, {"q": "The 'reward centre' of brain is located in:", "concept": "The answer is c: the nucleus accumbens, in the ventral striatum, is the structure textbooks call the brain's reward centre. The idea began with James Olds and Peter Milner (1954) at McGill University. They placed electrodes in rats' brains and let the rats press a lever to stimulate themselves. With electrodes in the septal area and along the medial forebrain bundle, rats pressed thousands of times an hour, ignoring food until they were exhausted. Later work traced this effect to the mesolimbic dopamine pathway: dopamine neurons in the ventral tegmental area (VTA) of the midbrain send axons through the medial forebrain bundle to the nucleus accumbens. Food, sex, music and praise all raise dopamine there, and addictive drugs such as cocaine and amphetamine raise it far more, which is why this circuit sits at the heart of theories of addiction. Kent Berridge and Terry Robinson (1998) added that accumbens dopamine signals wanting a reward more than liking it. Example: when Kabir hears the alert sound of his favourite game, dopamine release in his nucleus accumbens rises before he even opens the app, and he feels the urge to check. Option c is right because the nucleus accumbens is the target where reward signals arrive and are turned into motivated action, and it is the structure named the reward or pleasure centre. Exam trap: the VTA and the nucleus accumbens are always named together, so students pick the starting point of the pathway (VTA) instead of its target (nucleus accumbens). Key note: the dopamine neurons start in the VTA, so a is part of the reward system too; exams name the nucleus accumbens as the reward centre, so c stands.", "traps": {"a": "The ventral tegmental area in the midbrain holds the cell bodies of the dopamine neurons that start the mesolimbic pathway. It is the source of the reward signal, not the structure exams call the reward centre, which is the accumbens it projects to.", "b": "The hypothalamus controls homeostatic drives such as hunger, thirst and temperature; the medial forebrain bundle passes through the lateral hypothalamus, so it was an early self-stimulation site. It is a drive and homeostasis centre, not the reward centre.", "d": "The prefrontal cortex receives the mesocortical dopamine pathway and weighs rewards when planning and deciding. It is the brain's executive controller, not the reward centre itself."}}, {"q": "The role of the hypothalamus is:", "concept": "The answer is b: the hypothalamus provides visceral control of the body's homeostasis. Homeostasis means keeping the internal environment steady, a term Walter Cannon (1932) popularised in The Wisdom of the Body. The hypothalamus is a small cluster of nuclei in the diencephalon, just below the thalamus, forming the floor and lower walls of the third ventricle. It works as the body's thermostat and control panel through two routes. First, it drives the autonomic nervous system, turning sympathetic and parasympathetic activity up or down. Second, it controls the endocrine system through the pituitary: it releases hormones into the portal blood that steer the anterior pituitary, and its own neurons make oxytocin and vasopressin, which the posterior pituitary stores and releases. Its nuclei regulate body temperature, hunger and satiety (the lateral and ventromedial areas), thirst and water balance, sleep and daily rhythms (the suprachiasmatic nucleus), sexual behaviour and the stress response. Students remember it by the four Fs: feeding, fighting, fleeing and mating. Example: Meera walks home in the Delhi heat; her hypothalamus senses the warmer blood and starts sweating and widening the skin's blood vessels, makes her thirsty, and releases vasopressin so her kidneys save water. Option b is right because every job the hypothalamus does is about keeping visceral (internal organ) states within safe limits. Exam trap: the thalamus and the hypothalamus sit next to each other in the diencephalon, and option a gives the thalamus's job as a sensory relay to catch anyone who mixes up the two names.", "traps": {"a": "This is the thalamus, the relay station that receives incoming sensory signals (every sense except smell) and passes them to the right area of the cerebral cortex. The hypothalamus sits below it but controls internal states rather than relaying sensation.", "c": "This describes the sensory association cortex of the temporal and parietal lobes, which compares new sights and sounds with stored memories to give them meaning. That is a cortical, learned function, not visceral control.", "d": "This describes the prefrontal association cortex, which pulls together information from the other association areas for planning, judgement and abstract thought. It is the seat of higher intellect, not of homeostasis."}}, {"q": "The direct emotional circuit controlling fear comprises:", "concept": "The answer is b: the direct fear circuit runs eye, thalamus, amygdala, hypothalamus. Joseph LeDoux (1996), in The Emotional Brain, traced two routes by which danger reaches the amygdala, an almond-shaped group of nuclei in the medial temporal lobe. The low road goes from the sensory thalamus straight to the amygdala. It is fast but crude: it carries only a rough sketch of the stimulus, enough to raise the alarm. The high road goes from the thalamus to the sensory cortex and only then to the amygdala. It is slower but detailed, so it can confirm or cancel the alarm. LeDoux showed in rats that fear conditioning to a simple tone survived even after the auditory cortex was removed, because the thalamus-to-amygdala route alone was enough. Signals enter the amygdala's lateral nucleus and leave through its central nucleus to the hypothalamus, which sets off the bodily fear response: the sympathetic surge (racing heart, fast breathing) and the stress hormone cascade, while the periaqueductal grey of the midbrain produces freezing. Example: Kabir walks through a field at dusk and sees a curved shape on the path. He jumps back before he knows what it is (low road); a moment later his visual cortex reports that it is a rope, and he relaxes (high road). Option b is right because it lists the direct, subcortical pathway that bypasses the cortex, eye to thalamus to amygdala, with the hypothalamus producing the physical reaction. Exam trap: options that include the sensory cortex, hippocampus or prefrontal cortex describe the slower, indirect high road and the later regulation of fear, not the direct circuit.", "traps": {"a": "This chain runs through the sensory cortex, hippocampus and prefrontal cortex, which is the slow high road plus the structures that add context (hippocampus) and regulate fear (prefrontal cortex). It leaves out the amygdala, the hub of fear.", "c": "This puts the hypothalamus before the visual cortex and adds the striatum, a basal ganglia structure for movement and habits. It is not a recognised fear pathway and it also misses the amygdala.", "d": "The lateral geniculate nucleus is the thalamic relay for vision, but sending its output to the hippocampus and prefrontal cortex describes memory and planning routes. Without the amygdala it cannot be the fear circuit."}}, {"q": "A man of 40 years old met with a road traffic accident. Few days later he was having difficulty in execution of plans. He may have sustained injury to:", "concept": "The answer is b: difficulty executing plans after a head injury points to damage in the dorsolateral prefrontal cortex. The prefrontal cortex is the front part of the frontal lobe, and its regions do different jobs. The dorsolateral prefrontal cortex (DLPFC), on the upper outer surface, runs the executive functions: setting goals, planning steps, holding information in working memory, switching strategy when a rule changes and checking progress. Damage produces what Alan Baddeley and Barbara Wilson (1988) called the dysexecutive syndrome: the person can often say what needs doing but cannot organise, start, sequence or finish it. Brenda Milner (1963) showed that patients with dorsolateral frontal lesions kept sorting by an old rule on the Wisconsin Card Sorting Test (perseveration), and Tim Shallice (1982) used the Tower of London task to show their planning problems. Other frontal regions give different pictures. Damage to the orbitofrontal cortex, just above the eyes, causes disinhibition, poor social judgement and personality change, as in Phineas Gage, whose tamping-iron injury was described by John Harlow (1848). Damage to the medial prefrontal cortex and anterior cingulate causes apathy and low drive (abulia). The left inferior frontal gyrus holds Broca's area for speech production. In the stem, the man may plan to pay a bill, go to the bank and buy medicines, yet get stuck after the first step or do them in a muddled order, although his memory and speech are fine. Option b is right because planning and carrying out goal-directed sequences is the defining job of the dorsolateral prefrontal cortex. Exam trap: road accidents often bruise the orbitofrontal cortex, so examiners hope you choose it, but orbitofrontal damage changes personality and conduct, not plan execution.", "traps": {"a": "The inferior frontal cortex, especially the left inferior frontal gyrus, holds Broca's area, so damage there causes non-fluent (Broca's) aphasia; on the right it affects stopping unwanted responses. Planning failure is not its signature.", "c": "The orbitofrontal cortex controls social behaviour and impulse control; damage causes disinhibition and personality change, as in Phineas Gage. Such a patient may plan well on paper but behave tactlessly and rashly.", "d": "The medial prefrontal cortex, with the anterior cingulate, supports motivation and starting actions; damage causes apathy, abulia and, when severe, akinetic mutism. That is a loss of drive rather than a specific failure to execute plans."}}, {"q": "Damage to the cerebellum would most likely result in:", "concept": "The answer is c: damage to the cerebellum most often causes loss of coordination (ataxia). The cerebellum, or little brain, sits at the back of the hindbrain behind the pons and holds more than half of all the brain's neurons. It does not start movements. It compares the movement the motor cortex intended with feedback from muscles, joints and the inner ear, and corrects errors so that movement is smooth, accurate and well timed. It also keeps balance and posture and helps us learn motor skills. Gordon Holmes (1917) mapped its signs in soldiers with gunshot wounds of the cerebellum in the First World War. The classic signs are ataxia (an unsteady, wide-based, drunken gait), intention tremor (shaking that grows as the hand nears a target), dysmetria (over- or under-shooting, seen on the finger-nose test), dysdiadochokinesia (clumsy rapid alternating movements), nystagmus and slurred, scanning speech. The signs appear on the same side of the body as the damage. Alcohol affects the cerebellum early, which is why police roadside tests ask drivers to walk a straight line. Example: after a stroke in his right cerebellum, Arjun reaches for a glass of water with his right hand, overshoots it, and his hand shakes more the closer it gets. Option c is right because coordinating, timing and correcting movement is the cerebellum's main job, so its damage shows up as clumsy, unsteady action rather than paralysis. Exam trap: resting tremor and slowness belong to the basal ganglia (Parkinson's disease), whereas the cerebellum gives intention tremor. Jeremy Schmahmann (1998) also described a cerebellar cognitive affective syndrome with subtle planning, spatial and mood changes, but the most likely and classic result is still loss of coordination.", "traps": {"a": "Visuospatial disorientation, such as getting lost or misjudging where objects are, comes mainly from right parietal (parieto-occipital) damage. The cerebellum can add subtle spatial problems, but this is not its typical sign.", "b": "Visual blindness follows damage to the eyes, the optic nerves or the primary visual cortex in the occipital lobe (cortical blindness). The cerebellum does not process vision.", "d": "Personality change is the hallmark of frontal lobe damage, especially to the orbitofrontal cortex, as in Phineas Gage. Cerebellar damage changes how smoothly a person moves, not who they are."}}, {"q": "The statistical method developed by Spearman to assess whether intelligence is underpinned by one or many components is:", "concept": "The answer is c: Charles Spearman (1904) developed factor analysis to test whether intelligence rests on one general ability or many. In his 1904 paper on general intelligence, Spearman noticed that children's marks in very different subjects, such as classics, French, English, mathematics and even pitch discrimination, all correlated positively with one another. This pattern is called the positive manifold. Factor analysis is a statistical method that takes a table of correlations among many tests and asks how few underlying dimensions (factors) can explain them. Spearman's answer was the two-factor theory: every test draws on a general factor, g, shared by all tests, plus a specific factor, s, unique to that test. Tests that correlate most with all the others, such as reasoning tests, have high g loadings. Others expanded his method. Louis Thurstone (1938) used multiple factor analysis and found seven primary mental abilities, such as verbal comprehension, number and spatial ability, challenging a single g. Raymond Cattell (1963) split g into fluid and crystallised intelligence, and John Carroll (1993) combined these ideas into a three-stratum model, the basis of today's CHC theory. Example: Riya scores well in mathematics, Hindi and science, and classmates who do well in one subject tend to do well in the others; Spearman would say they share a high g. Option c is right because factor analysis is the technique built to find the common dimensions behind correlated test scores, which is exactly how Spearman argued for g. Exam trap: Spearman's name is also on Spearman's rank correlation (rho, 1904) and the Spearman-Brown prophecy formula (1910), so examiners swap these ideas in, but the method for the structure of intelligence is factor analysis.", "traps": {"a": "Multivariate analysis is the umbrella name for any technique that handles many variables at once, such as MANOVA or multiple regression. Factor analysis is one kind, but the stem asks for Charles Spearman's specific method.", "b": "Analysis of variance (Ronald Fisher, 1925) tests whether the means of several groups differ. It compares groups; it does not uncover the abilities behind correlated test scores.", "d": "Cluster analysis (Robert Tryon, 1939) sorts people or cases into groups that resemble each other. It classifies cases rather than finding the factors underlying a set of tests."}}, {"q": "Attitudinal scales use different measurement scales which one is not one of them?", "concept": "The answer is b: the nominal scale is not one of the measurement levels used by attitude scales. Stanley Smith Stevens (1946) described four levels of measurement, remembered as NOIR. A nominal scale only names categories with no order (religion, blood group). An ordinal scale ranks things without equal gaps (first, second, third). An interval scale has equal gaps but no true zero (temperature in Celsius). A ratio scale has equal gaps and a true zero (height, reaction time). An attitude scale measures how favourable or unfavourable a person is towards something, so it must show degree, which needs at least order. Louis Thurstone (1928) built the method of equal-appearing intervals, aiming at interval data. Rensis Likert (1932) used summated ratings on a five-point agree to disagree format: each item is ordinal, and the total is usually treated as interval. Louis Guttman (1944) built cumulative scalogram scales, which are ordinal. Charles Osgood (1957) built the semantic differential, seven-point bipolar adjective pairs such as good to bad, treated as interval. Ratio-type methods exist too, such as constant-sum scales, where a person divides 100 points among options. Example: Kabir marks 4 (agree) on the Likert item 'Online classes help me learn'; the number shows how strongly he agrees, which a mere label could never show. Option b is right because a nominal scale cannot express more or less of an attitude; it only sorts people into named boxes. Exam trap: students know attitudes have no true zero and rush to pick ratio, but the question wants the level that cannot show degree at all. Key note: many textbooks say attitude scales give ordinal or interval data and almost never ratio data, because attitudes have no true zero, so a can be argued. The paper's answer b stands: a nominal scale only names categories and cannot show degree of agreement, which is what an attitude scale measures.", "traps": {"a": "A ratio scale has equal intervals and a true zero, as in weight or reaction time. Attitude scales rarely reach it, but ratio-style methods such as constant-sum and magnitude scaling are used, so it is counted among the levels here.", "c": "An interval scale has equal steps without a true zero. Louis Thurstone's equal-appearing interval scale and the semantic differential aim to produce it, and summed Likert totals are usually treated as interval.", "d": "An ordinal scale ranks responses in order, as in single Likert items and Louis Guttman's cumulative scalogram. It is the most common level of attitude data."}}, {"q": "Multi trait multi method matrix design is for analysis of:", "concept": "The answer is c: the multitrait-multimethod matrix was designed to analyse convergent validity, together with its partner, discriminant validity. Donald Campbell and Donald Fiske (1959) asked a simple question: does a score reflect the trait, or only the way it was measured? In their design, two or more traits are each measured by two or more methods, such as self-report, peer rating and observation, and every score is correlated with every other score in one large table, the matrix. Monotrait-heteromethod correlations (the same trait measured by different methods) should be high; this is convergent validity, and these values form the validity diagonal. Heterotrait-monomethod correlations (different traits measured by the same method) should be lower; if they are high, the shared method is driving the scores, which is called method variance. Heterotrait-heteromethod correlations should be the lowest of all. Low correlations between different traits show discriminant (divergent) validity. Both kinds of evidence support construct validity, the idea from Lee Cronbach and Paul Meehl (1955) that a test measures the theoretical construct it claims to. Example: a researcher measures Meera's anxiety and her sociability, each by a questionnaire and by a friend's rating. Her two anxiety scores should agree closely (convergent), while her anxiety and sociability scores should not, even when both come from the questionnaire (discriminant). Option c is right because the matrix's central test is whether different methods measuring the same trait converge. Exam trap: concurrent sounds like convergent, and both involve correlating two measures taken at about the same time, but concurrent validity correlates a test with an outside criterion, while convergent validity correlates different measures of the same construct.", "traps": {"a": "Concurrent validity is a type of criterion-related validity: test scores are correlated with a criterion measured at the same time, such as a new depression scale against a psychiatrist's diagnosis today. It needs one test and one criterion, not a matrix of traits and methods.", "b": "Predictive validity is criterion-related validity where the criterion comes later, such as an entrance test predicting first-year marks. The multitrait-multimethod design does not involve future criteria.", "d": "Contact validity is not a recognised type of validity; it is a distortion of content validity, which asks whether the items fairly sample the whole domain, as judged by experts. Content validity is not studied with a correlation matrix."}}, {"q": "Cronbach's alpha is a measure of:", "concept": "The answer is b: Cronbach's alpha measures internal consistency, how well the items of a test hang together in measuring one thing. Lee Cronbach (1951) introduced coefficient alpha. The idea is simple: if all the items of a scale tap the same attribute, a person who scores high on one item should tend to score high on the others. Alpha summarises these inter-item relationships in one number, usually between 0 and 1. It equals the average of all possible split-half coefficients, so it avoids the problem that different ways of splitting a test give different answers. It generalises the Kuder-Richardson formula 20 (Frederic Kuder and Marion Richardson, 1937), which works only for items scored right or wrong, to items with several response points, such as Likert items. Only one administration of the test is needed. Values of about .70 and above are usually treated as acceptable for research, and higher values are expected for clinical decisions about individuals. Alpha rises as items are added, so a long test can have a high alpha even if it measures more than one thing; a high alpha does not prove the scale is unidimensional. Example: Arjun builds a 20-item exam stress questionnaire and gives it once to 200 students; an alpha of .86 tells him the items move together. Option b is right because alpha is calculated from the relationships among items within a single sitting, which is the definition of internal consistency reliability. Exam trap: Cronbach's name is also on the classic construct validity paper with Paul Meehl (1955), so students drift to validity, but alpha is a reliability coefficient.", "traps": {"a": "Inter-rater consistency is agreement between two or more scorers rating the same responses, measured with Cohen's kappa (Jacob Cohen, 1960) or the intraclass correlation. Alpha looks at items, not raters.", "c": "Test-retest reliability gives the same test twice to the same people and correlates the two sets of scores, showing stability over time. Alpha needs only one sitting.", "d": "Construct validity asks whether a test measures the theoretical trait it claims to (Lee Cronbach and Paul Meehl, 1955). Alpha shows consistency, and a test can be highly consistent yet measure the wrong thing."}}, {"q": "People having high cholesterol suffer from more hypertension. Appropriate statistical test utilized here:", "concept": "The answer is c: Pearson's correlation coefficient is the right test, because the stem is about the relationship between two continuous variables. Francis Galton (1888) introduced the idea of co-relation, and Karl Pearson worked out the product-moment correlation coefficient, r, in the 1890s. Pearson's r gives the direction and strength of a linear relationship between two variables measured on interval or ratio scales. It runs from minus 1 (perfect negative) through 0 (no linear relationship) to plus 1 (perfect positive). Squaring r gives the proportion of variance the two variables share: an r of .60 means 36 percent shared variance. The test assumes a roughly linear relationship, approximately normal data and no extreme outliers; when these fail, Spearman's rank correlation is used instead. Correlation never proves cause: cholesterol might raise blood pressure, or both might come from a third factor such as diet. Example: Riya, a health psychology researcher in Pune, records the cholesterol level and systolic blood pressure of 150 adults. A Pearson's r of .45 would show that people with higher cholesterol tend to have higher blood pressure. Option c is right because the stem links the amount of one measured variable (cholesterol) to the amount of another (blood pressure), and Pearson's r is the standard test of association between two such measures. Exam trap: the word 'suffer' tempts students to see two groups (hypertensive or not) and pick a t-test, but the stem asks whether the two variables vary together. Key note: if hypertension were coded only as present or absent, you would compare cholesterol between the two groups (t-test) or use a point-biserial correlation; with cholesterol level and blood pressure both measured on continuous scales, Pearson's r is the test of association, so c stands.", "traps": {"a": "ANOVA (Ronald Fisher, 1925) compares the means of three or more groups, for example blood pressure in low, medium and high cholesterol groups. It tests group differences, not the strength of a relationship between two continuous measures.", "b": "The t-test (William Gosset, writing as Student, 1908) compares two group means, such as cholesterol in people with and without hypertension. It would fit only if hypertension were treated as a yes or no grouping.", "d": "Descriptive statistics, such as the mean and standard deviation, only summarise data. They describe each variable but cannot test whether the two are related."}}, {"q": "The Flynn effect is:", "concept": "The answer is b: the Flynn effect is the slow but steady rise in average IQ test scores over time. James Flynn (1984) showed that Americans gained about 3 IQ points per decade between 1932 and 1978, and in 1987 he reported similar or larger gains in 14 nations. Because IQ tests are normed so that the average is 100 at the time of standardisation, a rising population makes old norms too easy: a person tested on norms 20 years old scores about 6 points higher than on fresh norms. The gains are largest on tests of fluid, abstract reasoning such as Raven's Progressive Matrices, and smaller on vocabulary and general knowledge. They are far too fast to be genetic. Suggested causes include better nutrition and health, more and longer schooling, smaller families, a more cognitively complex environment and growing familiarity with test-like thinking. The effect has practical weight: publishers must re-norm tests regularly, and an outdated test can lift a child above the cut-off for intellectual disability. Since about the 1990s some countries, such as Norway and Denmark, have shown a small decline, called the negative or reverse Flynn effect. Example: Kabir takes an intelligence test whose norms were set 20 years ago and scores 108; on a newly normed version he might score about 102. Option b is right because it states exactly what Flynn documented: a gradual, generation-by-generation increase in average scores. Exam trap: examiners reverse the direction in option a, which describes the recent reverse Flynn effect in a few countries, not the Flynn effect itself.", "traps": {"a": "This is the reverse (negative) Flynn effect, a recent small decline seen in some countries such as Norway. It is the opposite of what James Flynn first documented.", "c": "This describes the predictive validity of IQ, its power to forecast school and job performance. It is a property of IQ tests, not the Flynn effect.", "d": "No named effect says this; research finds intelligence only weakly related to happiness or life satisfaction. It has nothing to do with James Flynn's finding about rising scores."}}, {"q": "Hold subtests of the Wechsler Intelligence Scale are the following except:", "concept": "The answer is d: Digit Span is a don't-hold subtest, so it is the exception among the hold subtests. David Wechsler (1939) built the Wechsler-Bellevue scale and noticed that subtests age differently. Hold subtests resist ageing and brain damage because they draw on old, overlearned knowledge and recognition: Vocabulary, Information, Object Assembly and Picture Completion. Don't-hold subtests decline because they need speed, sustained attention, working memory and new problem solving: Digit Span, Digit Symbol (Coding), Similarities and Block Design. In the terms of Raymond Cattell (1963), hold tests lean on crystallised ability and don't-hold tests on fluid ability. Wechsler used the gap to estimate loss: the deterioration index (or quotient) is the hold score minus the don't-hold score, divided by the hold score. A large gap suggests decline beyond normal ageing, for example from dementia, brain injury or chronic psychiatric illness. Clinicians today also estimate premorbid ability with reading tests such as the National Adult Reading Test (Hazel Nelson, 1982), but the principle is the same, and Vocabulary is the best single hold test. Example: Meera, a retired teacher of 72, still defines words and answers general knowledge questions well, but repeats only short strings of digits and works slowly on Coding; her hold scores are high and her don't-hold scores have dropped. Option d is right because repeating digits forwards and backwards depends on attention and working memory, among the first abilities to fall with age and brain damage. Exam trap: Object Assembly and Picture Completion are performance subtests, so students assume they must decline, while Similarities looks verbal and seems safe; in Wechsler's lists the first two hold and Similarities does not.", "traps": {"a": "Object Assembly, putting puzzle pieces together to form a familiar object, is a performance subtest that David Wechsler counted as hold. Its familiar, meaningful material makes it fairly resistant to decline.", "b": "Vocabulary is the best single hold subtest, since word knowledge is overlearned and survives ageing and early brain disease. It is often used to estimate premorbid IQ.", "c": "Picture Completion, spotting the missing part of a familiar picture, relies on stored knowledge and recognition. David Wechsler listed it as a hold subtest."}}, {"q": "All are clinical scales of MMPI-II except:", "concept": "The answer is b: Mania is not an MMPI-2 clinical scale; scale 9 is Hypomania (Ma). The Minnesota Multiphasic Personality Inventory was built by Starke Hathaway and J. Charnley McKinley (1943) at the University of Minnesota by empirical criterion keying: an item was kept if patients with a given diagnosis answered it differently from normal adults, whatever its content. The revised MMPI-2 (1989) has 567 true or false items and keeps the same 10 clinical scales: 1 Hypochondriasis (Hs), 2 Depression (D), 3 Hysteria (Hy), 4 Psychopathic Deviate (Pd), 5 Masculinity-Femininity (Mf), 6 Paranoia (Pa), 7 Psychasthenia (Pt), 8 Schizophrenia (Sc), 9 Hypomania (Ma) and 0 Social Introversion (Si). Validity scales such as L (lie), F (infrequency) and K (defensiveness) check how the person approached the test. Raw scores become T scores, and on the MMPI-2 a T of 65 or more is clinically raised. Clinicians read the code type, the two highest scales together. Several names are old diagnostic labels kept for history: Psychasthenia was the term of Pierre Janet (1903) for what we now call obsessive-compulsive and anxiety problems. Example: Arjun's MMPI-2 profile peaks on scales 2 and 7, a 2-7 code type that suggests an anxious, worried depression. Option b is right because the ninth scale was built from patients in milder, hypomanic states and is named Hypomania, not Mania. Exam trap: the abbreviation Ma looks like Mania, and examiners rely on that; the scale measures overactivity, excitement and flight of ideas at a hypomanic level.", "traps": {"a": "Schizophrenia (Sc) is clinical scale 8, covering odd thinking, alienation and unusual experiences. It is a genuine MMPI-2 scale.", "c": "Psychopathic Deviate (Pd) is clinical scale 4, covering antisocial behaviour, family conflict and impulsivity. It is a genuine MMPI-2 scale.", "d": "Psychasthenia (Pt) is clinical scale 7, covering anxiety, worry, obsessions and compulsions. It is a genuine MMPI-2 scale despite its old-fashioned name."}}, {"q": "Test of Frontal Lobe Function:", "concept": "The answer is b: the Wisconsin Card Sorting Test (WCST) is the classic test of frontal lobe function. David Grant and Esta Berg (1948) designed it. The person sees four stimulus cards that differ in the colour, shape and number of their symbols, and must sort a deck of response cards to match them. The examiner never states the rule and only says right or wrong after each card. After a run of correct sorts the rule changes without warning, say from colour to shape, and the person must notice the feedback and shift. The test therefore measures abstract reasoning, concept formation, use of feedback and, above all, set shifting (cognitive flexibility). Its main error is the perseverative error: continuing to sort by the old rule despite being told it is wrong. Brenda Milner (1963) showed that patients with dorsolateral frontal lesions made many more perseverative errors than patients with damage elsewhere, which made the WCST the standard executive function test; Robert Heaton (1981) later published its scoring manual. Example: after a frontal head injury, Kabir keeps sorting by colour for many trials after the rule has changed to shape, even while saying he knows he is getting them wrong. Option b is right because the WCST taps planning, flexibility and rule shifting, the executive functions of the dorsolateral prefrontal cortex. Exam trap: the Bender Gestalt is the most famous organicity test, so students pick it, but it is a general visuomotor copying test and does not single out the frontal lobes.", "traps": {"a": "The Bender Visual Motor Gestalt Test (Lauretta Bender, 1938) asks the person to copy nine geometric figures. It screens visuomotor integration and general brain dysfunction, not frontal executive function specifically.", "c": "The Nahor-Benson test is a figure-copying test of visuo-perceptual and visuomotor ability, used in Indian neuropsychological batteries such as the PGI Battery of Brain Dysfunction. It is not a frontal lobe test.", "d": "Albert's test (Martin Albert, 1973) is a line cancellation task in which the person crosses out lines scattered across a page. It detects unilateral (hemispatial) neglect, usually after right parietal damage."}}, {"q": "From the Big Five personality dimensions, behaviours such as high level of discipline, methodical planning, perseverance is:", "concept": "The answer is d: discipline, methodical planning and perseverance are the marks of conscientiousness. The Big Five, or Five-Factor Model, grew from the lexical approach: if a trait matters, everyday language will have words for it. Ernest Tupes and Raymond Christal (1961) found five recurring factors in trait ratings, Warren Norman (1963) confirmed them, Lewis Goldberg (1981) named them the Big Five, and Paul Costa and Robert McCrae measured them with the NEO Personality Inventory, revised as the NEO-PI-R in 1992. The five are remembered as OCEAN: Openness, Conscientiousness, Extraversion, Agreeableness and Neuroticism. Conscientiousness is the tendency to be organised, dependable, self-disciplined, dutiful and achievement-oriented; the low end is careless and disorganised. Its six NEO-PI-R facets are competence, order, dutifulness, achievement striving, self-discipline and deliberation (thinking before acting). Murray Barrick and Michael Mount (1991) showed in a meta-analysis that conscientiousness is the Big Five trait that best predicts job performance across all kinds of jobs, and it also predicts better health habits and a longer life. Example: Riya, preparing for her entrance exam, makes a weekly timetable, revises every evening even when friends go out, and keeps going after a poor mock score; she is high in conscientiousness. Option d is right because each behaviour in the stem, discipline (self-discipline), methodical planning (order and deliberation) and perseverance (achievement striving), is a facet of conscientiousness. Exam trap: perseverance makes some students think of grit (Angela Duckworth, 2007) or of low neuroticism, but within the Big Five it belongs to conscientiousness.", "traps": {"a": "Agreeableness is the tendency to be warm, trusting, cooperative and compassionate, with facets such as altruism and compliance. It concerns how a person treats others, not self-discipline.", "b": "Openness to experience is the tendency to be imaginative, curious, artistic and open to new ideas and values. A person can be highly open yet disorganised.", "c": "Extraversion is the tendency to be sociable, talkative, assertive and energetic and to feel positive emotions. It concerns seeking company and stimulation, not methodical effort."}}, {"q": "The following statements are true of illness behaviour, except:", "concept": "The answer is c: the claim that contact with a doctor is necessary to legitimise illness is not true of illness behaviour. David Mechanic (1962) defined illness behaviour as the ways people perceive, evaluate and act (or fail to act) on bodily symptoms. It covers noticing a symptom, deciding what it means, talking to family, trying home remedies, resting, taking leave and, only sometimes, seeing a doctor. Most symptoms never reach a doctor at all, so illness behaviour begins before, and often without, any medical contact. Edward Suchman (1965) divided it into five stages: symptom experience, assumption of the sick role, medical care contact, the dependent-patient role, and recovery or rehabilitation. Culture shapes every stage. Mark Zborowski (1952) found in a New York hospital that patients of Jewish and Italian background expressed pain openly, while Irish and Old American patients were stoical. Illness behaviour can also turn abnormal, as when a person stays passive and dependent long after recovery, which some writers liken to learned helplessness (Martin Seligman, 1967). Example: Arjun wakes with a fever, decides it is only the weather, takes a home remedy his mother suggests and stays home from college; he is already showing illness behaviour, with no doctor involved. Option c is right because illness behaviour is defined by how the person responds to symptoms, not by a doctor's confirmation. Exam trap: the sick role of Talcott Parsons (1951) makes the doctor the gatekeeper who legitimises sickness, and students mistake that role for illness behaviour itself. Key note: Talcott Parsons' sick role (1951) does make the doctor the one who legitimises sickness, which is why c is tempting to call true; and d (learned helplessness, Martin Seligman, 1967) is also not a standard description of illness behaviour, so d can be argued. The paper's answer c stands.", "traps": {"a": "True: Edward Suchman (1965) described illness behaviour in five stages, from symptom experience to recovery or rehabilitation. So this is not the exception.", "b": "True: culture shapes how symptoms are noticed, expressed and acted on, as Mark Zborowski (1952) showed with pain responses in different ethnic groups. So this is not the exception.", "d": "Prolonged, passive, dependent illness behaviour after a long illness is often likened to learned helplessness (Martin Seligman, 1967), so the paper treats this as true. It is a looser claim than a or b, but the clearly false statement is c."}}, {"q": "Mrs. A was found missing for 10 days. She was found in a village 500 kilometres away from her native and was completely unaware of her previous identity. She was working there as a field worker. She is probably suffering from:", "concept": "The answer is b: Mrs. A has dissociative fugue, sudden travel away from home with loss of memory for her identity and the start of a new life. Fugue comes from the Latin fugere, to flee. In the classic description (DSM-IV, and ICD-10 F44.1), the person suddenly and unexpectedly leaves home or work, travels purposefully, cannot recall her past, and is confused about who she is or takes on a partly or wholly new identity. During the fugue she looks normal to strangers; she may take a job, rent a room and talk sensibly. Fugues usually follow severe stress such as a family crisis, debt, war or a threat, and they are rare. They often end suddenly: the person wakes up, as it were, in a strange place, recovers her old identity, and then has amnesia for the fugue period itself. Treatment offers a safe setting, gentle psychotherapy to recover memories and attention to the stress behind the flight. Each part of the stem fits: missing for 10 days, found 500 kilometres away, unaware of her previous identity, and working as a field worker in a new life. Option b is right because purposeful travel plus loss of personal identity plus a new role is the defining picture of fugue, which goes beyond the memory gap of simple amnesia. Exam trap: dissociative identity disorder also involves identity, but there several personality states alternate again and again, whereas a fugue is a single episode of flight with one forgotten identity. Key note: DSM-5 (2013) made dissociative fugue a specifier of dissociative amnesia ('with dissociative fugue'), so a is close; ICD-10 (F44.1) and the classic texts keep fugue as its own diagnosis, and the travel plus new identity is the fugue picture, so b stands.", "traps": {"a": "Dissociative amnesia is an inability to recall important personal information, usually about a traumatic or stressful event, without purposeful travel or a new life. Under DSM-5 fugue is a specifier of it, but the travel and new identity in the stem point to fugue.", "c": "Dissociative identity disorder (formerly multiple personality disorder) involves two or more distinct personality states that take turns controlling behaviour, with recurrent memory gaps. It is a chronic pattern, not a single flight from home.", "d": "Factitious disorder means deliberately faking or inducing illness to take on the sick role without an outside reward; Richard Asher (1951) named its severe form Munchausen syndrome. Mrs. A is not faking anything."}}, {"q": "Most common type of obsession:", "concept": "The answer is c: fear of contamination is the most common obsession in obsessive-compulsive disorder (OCD). An obsession is a recurrent, intrusive, unwanted thought, image or urge that causes marked anxiety; a compulsion is a repetitive act or mental ritual done to reduce that anxiety. Steven Rasmussen and Jane Eisen (1992) catalogued symptoms in a large clinic sample and found contamination obsessions in about half of patients: fear of germs, dirt, bodily fluids, chemicals or disease. Pathological doubt came next (did I lock the door, did I hurt someone?), followed by somatic obsessions, the need for symmetry, aggressive obsessions and sexual obsessions; religious (blasphemous) obsessions were less frequent. Most patients have more than one type. Contamination obsessions usually pair with washing and cleaning compulsions and with avoidance of dirty objects. The most common compulsion, however, is checking, which pairs with pathological doubt. Treatment is exposure and response prevention, pioneered by Victor Meyer (1966), along with selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors. Example: Meera, a 24-year-old student, cannot touch door handles or the bus rail without feeling dirty; she washes her hands 40 times a day until her skin cracks, and feels relief for only a few minutes. Option c is right because contamination tops the list of obsession types in large clinical surveys. Exam trap: examiners separate obsessions from compulsions; the most common obsession is contamination, but the most common compulsion is checking, and doubt is the second most common obsession, so d tempts students who mix up the two lists.", "traps": {"a": "Blasphemous (religious) obsessions, such as intrusive thoughts insulting God, are part of scrupulosity. They are distressing and fairly common in religious cultures, but they rank well below contamination.", "b": "Sexual obsessions are unwanted intrusive sexual thoughts or images, found in roughly a quarter of patients in clinic samples. They are common but not the most common.", "d": "Pathological doubt is the second most common obsession and drives checking, the most common compulsion. It comes just behind contamination."}}, {"q": "La belle indifference is seen in:", "concept": "The answer is a: la belle indifference is classically linked with conversion disorder. The French phrase means beautiful indifference: a surprising lack of concern about a symptom that looks serious, such as paralysis, blindness or loss of speech. Pierre Janet, writing on hysteria in the 1890s, described it, and the classic texts treated it as a sign that the symptom solved an inner conflict, giving primary gain (relief from anxiety) and secondary gain (attention, escape from duties). Conversion disorder, called functional neurological symptom disorder in DSM-5 (2013), means altered motor or sensory function, such as weakness, abnormal movements, non-epileptic seizures or numbness, that clinical findings show to be incompatible with recognised neurological disease. Hoover's sign, where a 'paralysed' leg pushes down when the other leg is lifted, is one such finding. DSM-5 warns that la belle indifference is not specific to conversion disorder and should not be used to make the diagnosis: a systematic review by Jon Stone and colleagues (2006) found it about as often in patients with real organic disease, and many conversion patients are visibly distressed. Example: Riya, 19, wakes on the morning of her board exams unable to move her legs; in the ward she chats and smiles calmly while her parents are frantic. Option a is right because la belle indifference is the textbook feature of conversion (hysterical) symptoms, even though modern criteria no longer rely on it. Exam trap: la belle indifference is not the flat or blunted affect of schizophrenia; it is a selective calm about one dramatic symptom in a person who is otherwise emotionally responsive.", "traps": {"b": "Borderline personality disorder shows intense, unstable emotions, fear of abandonment and impulsive self-harm. Its emotional storms are the opposite of calm indifference.", "c": "Depression brings sadness, hopelessness and often loud complaints about symptoms and memory. Depressed people are weighed down by their problems, not indifferent to them.", "d": "In OCD the obsessions are ego-dystonic: the person sees them as senseless and is very distressed by them. That marked distress is the reverse of la belle indifference."}}, {"q": "Pseudo-dementia is a feature of:", "concept": "The answer is b: pseudodementia is a feature of depression, especially in older adults. Leslie Kiloh (1961) popularised the term for cognitive impairment that looks like dementia but has a psychiatric cause, most often depression; it is also called depressive pseudodementia or the dementia syndrome of depression. Depression slows thinking, saps effort and disturbs attention, so the person does badly on memory and concentration tests. Several clues separate it from true dementia. The onset is fairly quick and can be dated, often after a loss, and low mood appears before or with the memory problems. The person complains loudly about poor memory, highlights failures, gives up easily, answers 'I don't know' and performs unevenly from task to task. In early Alzheimer's disease the onset is slow, the person often hides or plays down the deficits, tries hard, gives near-miss answers or confabulates, and mood problems come later. Most importantly, pseudodementia improves when the depression is treated, although some patients later develop a true dementia, so follow-up matters. Example: Ramesh, 68, became forgetful and withdrawn within weeks of his wife's death; he tells the doctor 'my memory is finished' and says 'I don't know' to simple questions, and after three months of treatment for depression his test scores return to normal. Option b is right because depression is the classic and most common cause of reversible, dementia-like impairment. Exam trap: Ganser syndrome (Sigbert Ganser, 1898), with its approximate answers, was once called hysterical pseudodementia, but in exams pseudodementia means depression.", "traps": {"a": "Long-term heavy drinking causes real brain damage: alcohol-related dementia and the Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome from thiamine deficiency, with amnesia and confabulation. That is organic impairment, not pseudodementia.", "c": "Alzheimer's disease (Alois Alzheimer, 1906) is the true, progressive dementia that pseudodementia imitates. It is the comparison condition, not the cause.", "d": "Seizure disorders can cause brief confusion and memory gaps after a seizure (post-ictal states) and, rarely, long-term cognitive decline. They are not the classic cause of pseudodementia."}}, {"q": "The following personality disorder is characterized by marked impulsive, hostile, manipulative behaviours without any feeling of guilt or remorse for such behaviours:", "concept": "The answer is d: dissocial personality disorder is marked by impulsive, hostile and manipulative behaviour without guilt or remorse. Dissocial personality disorder is the ICD-10 name (F60.2); DSM-5 calls the same pattern antisocial personality disorder. ICD-10 describes callous unconcern for the feelings of others, gross and lasting irresponsibility and disregard for social rules, inability to keep lasting relationships, very low tolerance of frustration and a low threshold for aggression and violence, inability to feel guilt or learn from punishment, and a marked tendency to blame others. DSM-5 requires a pervasive disregard for and violation of the rights of others since age 15, shown by deceit, impulsivity, irritability and aggression, reckless disregard for safety, irresponsibility and lack of remorse; the person must be at least 18 and must have shown conduct disorder before 15. Hervey Cleckley (1941), in The Mask of Sanity, described the related psychopath, superficially charming but without remorse or shame, and Robert Hare (1980) built the Psychopathy Checklist, revised in 1991, to measure psychopathy. Most psychopaths meet criteria for antisocial personality disorder, but many people with antisocial personality disorder are not psychopaths. Example: Kabir, 26, borrows money from friends with invented stories, gets into fights when crossed, and when caught says, 'They were fools to trust me'. Option d is right because the stem's mix of impulsivity, hostility, manipulation and absent guilt is the core of dissocial (antisocial) personality disorder. Exam trap: dissocial sounds like dissociative, but it has nothing to do with dissociation; it means acting against society, that is, antisocial.", "traps": {"a": "Histrionic personality disorder shows excessive emotionality and attention seeking, with dramatic, seductive and shallow behaviour. Such people may manipulate for attention, but hostility and absent remorse are not its core.", "b": "Narcissistic personality disorder centres on grandiosity, a need for admiration and lack of empathy; such people may exploit others to feed their self-image. Impulsive aggression and rule-breaking are not its defining features.", "c": "Borderline personality disorder (in ICD-10, emotionally unstable personality, borderline type) shows unstable relationships, self-image and mood, fear of abandonment and impulsive self-harm. Anger is common, but these people usually feel intense guilt and shame afterwards."}}, {"q": "Piaget's stages of play was:", "concept": "The answer is d: Jean Piaget (1951) described three stages of play: functional play, symbolic play and games with rules. In Play, Dreams and Imitation in Childhood, Piaget treated play as assimilation, the child bending reality to fit the schemes it already has, so the form of play follows the stage of cognitive development. Functional play (also called practice, exercise or sensorimotor play) comes first, in the sensorimotor stage of infancy: simple actions repeated for the pleasure of mastering them, such as shaking a rattle, banging a spoon or dropping toys again and again. Symbolic play (pretend play) appears with the symbolic function of the preoperational stage, from about 2 to 7 years: one thing stands for another, as when a banana becomes a phone or a doll is fed. Games with rules dominate the concrete operational stage, from about 7 to 11 years, when children can agree on rules in advance, follow them and judge fairness: kho-kho, ludo, gully cricket. Sara Smilansky (1968) later added constructive play, building or making things. Example: at one year Meera bangs a steel spoon on a plate for the joy of the noise; at three she serves imaginary tea to her teddy; at eight she argues over the rules of ludo with her cousins. Option d is right because it lists Piaget's three forms in his own order, matched to the sensorimotor, preoperational and concrete operational stages. Exam trap: Mildred Parten (1932) described social stages of play (such as solitary, parallel, associative and cooperative play), which examiners mix with Piaget's cognitive stages of play.", "traps": {"a": "Exercise play is Jean Piaget's own name for practice (functional) play, and games with rules is right, but language play, playing with sounds, rhymes and words, is not one of his stages. The middle stage should be symbolic play.", "b": "Constructive play was added by Sara Smilansky (1968), and rough-and-tumble play (play fighting and chasing) comes from ethological studies of play. Neither is one of Jean Piaget's stages, although games with rules is.", "c": "This list has exercise and symbolic play from Jean Piaget but adds language play and leaves out games with rules, his final stage. So it is incomplete and partly wrong."}}, {"q": "What is narrative analysis?", "concept": "The answer is b: narrative analysis is an approach that is sensitive to how people choose to sequence and represent people and events in the stories they tell. Catherine Kohler Riessman (1993), in Narrative Analysis, set out the method, and the stem is close to the definition in Social Research Methods by Alan Bryman (2001). People make sense of their lives through stories: they pick events, put them in order, give them a beginning, a turning point and an ending, cast themselves and others as characters, and tell the story for a purpose and an audience. Narrative analysis keeps each story whole rather than cutting it into coded fragments, and asks why the story is told this way. Researchers look at what is told (thematic narrative analysis), how it is told (structural analysis) and to whom and why (dialogic or performance analysis). William Labov and Joshua Waletzky (1967) gave a well-known structural model: abstract, orientation, complicating action, evaluation, resolution and coda. Example: Arjun, recovering from depression, tells his story as 'everything was fine until my father fell ill, I fell apart in second year, and therapy was the turning point'. A narrative analyst studies why he starts with his father, what he leaves out, and how he presents himself as someone who recovered. Option b is right because attention to sequence and representation, how events and people are ordered and portrayed, is the defining feature of narrative analysis. Exam trap: thematic analysis (Virginia Braun and Victoria Clarke, 2006) looks for themes across many accounts and breaks stories into codes, the opposite of keeping each story intact; the fact that narrative analysis has a thematic variant is what makes option c tempting.", "traps": {"a": "A literary approach to documents describes literary criticism or documentary analysis of written texts. Narrative analysis borrows ideas from literary theory but works mostly with spoken life stories and interviews, and its focus is sequence and meaning.", "c": "Thematic analysis (Virginia Braun and Victoria Clarke, 2006) codes and groups themes across a whole data set. Narrative analysis can look at themes, but it is a distinct approach that keeps each story whole.", "d": "Improving the quality of interview material is a matter of interviewing skill, such as rapport, open questions and probing. It is a data collection concern, not a method of analysis."}}, {"q": "Which of these supports the James-Lange theory of emotion?", "concept": "The answer is a: the statement that emotion is a visceral response producing a behavioural response supports the James-Lange theory. William James (1884), in his essay 'What is an emotion?', and the Danish physician Carl Lange (1885) independently proposed that the bodily change comes first and the feeling follows. The sequence is a stimulus, then automatic visceral and muscular changes (racing heart, trembling, running), and then the brain's perception of those changes, which is the emotion. In James's words, we feel sorry because we cry, angry because we strike, afraid because we tremble. Walter Cannon (1927) attacked the theory with five criticisms: cutting the viscera off from the brain does not abolish emotional behaviour; the same visceral changes occur in very different emotions and in non-emotional states such as fever; the viscera are relatively insensitive structures; visceral changes are too slow to produce feelings; and artificially inducing visceral changes, for example with adrenaline injections (Gregorio Marañón, 1924), produces only 'as if' feelings rather than real emotion. Cannon and Philip Bard (1928) proposed instead that the thalamus signals the cortex and the body at the same time. Modern support for the body-first idea comes from facial feedback studies and the somatic marker hypothesis of Antonio Damasio (1994). Example: a stray dog charges at Kabir; his heart pounds and he runs, and only then does he feel afraid, as he notices his body's panic. Option a is right because it puts the visceral response first and treats emotion as arising from that bodily reaction, the heart of James-Lange. Exam trap: options b, c and d are all true statements, but they are Cannon's criticisms, which support Cannon-Bard and argue against James-Lange.", "traps": {"b": "This is one of Walter Cannon's (1927) criticisms, based on Gregorio Marañón's adrenaline injections, which produced only 'as if' emotions. It argues against James-Lange and supports Cannon-Bard.", "c": "Calling the viscera insensitive structures is another of Walter Cannon's criticisms: the organs send too little sensory feedback to produce rich feelings. It weakens James-Lange rather than supporting it.", "d": "This is Walter Cannon's point that fear, anger and even fever produce the same visceral pattern, so the viscera cannot tell emotions apart. It is evidence against James-Lange."}}, {"q": "A cognitive process that starts with simple processes and builds to the more-complex higher levels is known as:", "concept": "The answer is c: bottom-up processing starts with simple sensory features and builds up to complex, higher-level interpretations. It is also called data-driven processing, because information flows upward from the senses. Receptors pick up raw features such as lines, edges, angles, colours and movement; these are combined into shapes, the shapes into objects, and finally the object is recognised. David Hubel and Torsten Wiesel (1959) supported this idea when they found feature detector cells in the cat's visual cortex that fired to lines at particular angles. The Pandemonium model of Oliver Selfridge (1959) and the recognition-by-components theory of Irving Biederman (1987), which builds objects from simple 3D shapes called geons, are bottom-up models, and the direct perception of James Gibson (1979) is the classic bottom-up theory of perception. Top-down processing runs the other way: it is conceptually driven, using knowledge, expectation and context to interpret input, as in the view of Richard Gregory (1966) that a percept is a best-guess hypothesis, or in perceptual set. Real perception uses both at once. Example: Arjun copies an unfamiliar Tamil word from the board line by line and curve by curve, because he cannot read the script; that is bottom-up. His classmate Meera, who reads Tamil, takes in the same word at a glance even when a letter is smudged, because her knowledge fills the gap; that adds top-down processing. Option c is right because the stem describes the direction of bottom-up processing exactly: simple units first, complex meaning last. Exam trap: examiners swap the direction; top-down processing starts from knowledge at the higher level and works down to the details, so students who remember only that the two are opposites can pick d by mistake.", "traps": {"a": "Lateral processing is not a standard term for a direction of processing; it may be confused with lateral thinking (Edward de Bono, 1967), creative problem solving from new angles, or with lateral inhibition in the retina. Neither describes building from simple to complex.", "b": "Internal processing is not a technical term in cognitive psychology, since all mental processing is internal. It does not name a direction of information flow.", "d": "Top-down (conceptually driven) processing starts from knowledge, expectations and context and works down to interpret the details, as in Richard Gregory's hypothesis view of perception. It is the reverse of the process in the stem."}}, {"q": "The phenomenon whereby an individual in a group will tend to end up agreeing with a strong majority opinion despite their own judgement is known as:", "concept": "The answer is a: going along with a strong majority against your own judgement is normative influence. Morton Deutsch and Harold Gerard (1955) split social influence into two kinds. Normative influence is conformity driven by the wish to be liked and accepted and the fear of ridicule or rejection; it usually produces public compliance, where the person gives the group's answer while privately disagreeing. Informational influence is conformity driven by the wish to be right: when a situation is ambiguous, other people are taken as evidence about reality, and this produces private acceptance. The classic evidence is Solomon Asch (1951, 1956). Students judged which of three lines matched a standard line, a task so easy that people tested alone erred on under 1 percent of judgements. With a group of confederates who all gave the same wrong answer, participants conformed on about 37 percent of critical trials, and about three quarters conformed at least once. When participants could write answers privately, conformity fell sharply, which shows the pressure was social, not a change in what they saw. Contrast Muzafer Sherif (1935): in the dark room of the autokinetic effect a still light seems to move, nobody knows the true answer, and people converge on a group norm through informational influence. Example: Arjun finds a film boring, but when all six hostel friends praise it he says it was 'great' to avoid standing out. Option a is right because the stem stresses a strong majority overriding the person's own judgement, which is Asch's normative pressure, not uncertainty. Exam trap: examiners swap the two; informational influence needs ambiguity and changes private belief, while normative influence works even when the answer is obvious and changes only public behaviour.", "traps": {"b": "Informational (here 'informative') influence, from Morton Deutsch and Harold Gerard (1955), is accepting others' views as evidence about reality when you are unsure, as in Muzafer Sherif's autokinetic studies. The person in the stem has a clear judgement of their own, so this does not fit.", "c": "Critical mass is a term from sociology and the spread of innovations for the point at which enough people adopt an idea for it to spread by itself; it is not one of the recognised types of conformity pressure.", "d": "Political influence means using power, authority or lobbying to steer decisions (compare John French and Bertram Raven's bases of social power, 1959); it says nothing about an individual yielding to a majority's judgement."}}, {"q": "Collectivist cultures are characterized by members:", "concept": "The answer is b: members of collectivist cultures give priority to group goals over personal goals. Geert Hofstede (1980) surveyed IBM employees in 40 countries and found that cultures differ on a few measurable dimensions; one of them is individualism versus collectivism. In individualist cultures (the United States, Britain, Australia) the self is defined as an independent 'I', people are expected to look after themselves, and personal goals, rights and uniqueness come first. In collectivist cultures (much of Asia, Africa and Latin America) the self is defined by the 'we' of family, community or workplace; loyalty, duty, harmony and fitting in are prized, and when personal and group goals clash the group usually wins. Harry Triandis (1995) refined the idea, and Hazel Markus and Shinobu Kitayama (1991) described the matching self-construals: independent in individualist cultures, interdependent in collectivist ones. The difference shows up in research too: Rod Bond and Peter Smith (1996), pooling 133 Asch-type conformity studies, found more conformity in collectivist countries. Example: Meera wins a seat at a college in another city but chooses one near home, because her family needs her help with a younger brother and her parents' wishes weigh more than her own preference. India is usually described as leaning collectivist, though with strong individualist streaks in cities. Option b is right because putting group goals first is the defining feature of collectivism in Hofstede's and Triandis's accounts. Exam trap: the three wrong options all describe individualism (an independent self, uniqueness, personal achievement), so spotting the odd one out points to b; do not confuse collectivism with Hofstede's separate power distance dimension, which is about accepting unequal power.", "traps": {"a": "A strong, independent sense of self is the independent self-construal of Hazel Markus and Shinobu Kitayama (1991), typical of individualist cultures; collectivists define the self through relationships and groups.", "c": "Discovering how one differs from the group is the individualist emphasis on uniqueness; collectivist cultures stress fitting in and similarity to the group.", "d": "Achieving personal goals ahead of group goals is the hallmark of individualism on Geert Hofstede's (1980) dimension, the opposite of the stem."}}, {"q": "NDPS Act is associated with:", "concept": "The answer is a: the NDPS Act deals with substance abuse. The Narcotic Drugs and Psychotropic Substances Act, 1985 is India's central law on drugs of abuse. It came into force in November 1985, replaced older laws such as the Opium Acts and the Dangerous Drugs Act, 1930, and brought India in line with the United Nations conventions on narcotic drugs (1961) and psychotropic substances (1971). It prohibits the cultivation, production, possession, sale, purchase, transport and use of narcotic drugs (opium, cannabis, coca and products such as heroin and cocaine) and psychotropic substances (certain sedatives and stimulants), except for medical or scientific purposes. Since the 2001 amendment, punishment is graded by the quantity involved: small, intermediate or commercial. The Narcotics Control Bureau was set up under it in 1986. For clinical psychologists the treatment provisions matter most: section 64A gives immunity from prosecution to a person charged with consumption, or with a small quantity, who volunteers for de-addiction treatment at a government or recognised centre and completes it, and the government may set up centres to identify, treat and rehabilitate addicts. The 2014 amendment eased access to essential narcotic drugs such as morphine for pain relief. Example: Kabir, a college student, is caught with a small amount of ganja; if he seeks and completes treatment at a recognised de-addiction centre, he can avoid prosecution. Option a is right because the whole purpose of the Act is controlling drugs of abuse and dealing with the people who use them. Exam trap: match each Indian law to its subject: NDPS Act 1985 (drugs), POCSO Act 2012 (child sexual abuse), Rights of Persons with Disabilities Act 2016 (disability), Mental Healthcare Act 2017 (rights and treatment of persons with mental illness).", "traps": {"b": "Child abuse, especially child sexual abuse, is covered by the Protection of Children from Sexual Offences (POCSO) Act, 2012, along with the Juvenile Justice (Care and Protection of Children) Act, 2015.", "c": "Testamentary capacity is the mental capacity to make a valid will; in India it falls under the Indian Succession Act, 1925 (section 59 requires a person of sound mind who is not a minor), and is assessed in forensic work.", "d": "Disability arising from mental illness such as schizophrenia is certified under the Rights of Persons with Disabilities Act, 2016, using the Indian Disability Evaluation and Assessment Scale (IDEAS), not the NDPS Act."}}, {"q": "Who has formulated the stages of grief?", "concept": "The answer is b: Elisabeth Kübler-Ross formulated the stages of grief. Elisabeth Kübler-Ross (1969), a Swiss-born psychiatrist working in the United States, interviewed terminally ill patients for her book On Death and Dying and described five stages a person may pass through on learning they are dying: denial ('this cannot be happening to me'), anger ('why me?'), bargaining ('let me live until my son's wedding'), depression (sadness and withdrawal as losses mount) and acceptance (a calm, often tired, coming to terms). The mnemonic is DABDA. She herself said the stages are not a fixed ladder; people move back and forth, skip stages or hold several at once, and later research found little support for a strict sequence. The model was first about the dying and was later stretched to the bereaved and to any major loss. Other models of loss: John Bowlby (1980) described four phases of mourning in the bereaved (numbness, yearning and searching, disorganisation and despair, reorganisation); William Worden (1982) set out four tasks of mourning; and Margaret Stroebe and Henk Schut (1999) proposed the dual process model, in which the griever oscillates between loss-oriented and restoration-oriented coping. Example: when Meera's grandmother is told she has advanced cancer, she first insists the reports are wrong, then is angry with her doctors, then prays for more time, then grows quiet and sad, and finally plans calmly for her family. Option b is right because the five stages of grief are Kübler-Ross's. Exam trap: her stages describe the dying person and Bowlby's phases the bereaved; Stroebe and Schut describe oscillation, not stages; and Lawrence Kohlberg and Carol Gilligan tempt only because they are famous 'stage' theorists of moral development.", "traps": {"a": "Rollo May (1950, The Meaning of Anxiety) was an American existential psychologist known for normal versus neurotic anxiety and Love and Will (1969); he did not propose stages of grief.", "c": "Carol Gilligan (1982, In a Different Voice) proposed an ethic of care in moral development, criticising Kohlberg's justice-based stages as male-centred; her work is about moral reasoning, not grief.", "d": "Lawrence Kohlberg (1958) described three levels and six stages of moral reasoning using dilemmas such as Heinz's; these are stages of moral judgement, not of dying or grief."}}, {"q": "Smallest unit of speech perception is:", "concept": "The answer is a: the phoneme is the smallest unit of speech perception. Language is built in layers. At the bottom is the phoneme, the smallest unit of sound that can change the meaning of a word, though it has no meaning of its own: /p/ and /b/ are different phonemes in English because 'pat' and 'bat' are different words. English has roughly 40 to 45 phonemes, and every language picks its own set. Above phonemes come morphemes, the smallest units of meaning (free like 'cat', bound like the plural '-s'), then words, then syntax (the rules for combining words into sentences) and semantics (meaning). Charles Hockett (1960) called this duality of patterning: meaningless sounds combine into meaningful units. Listeners perceive phonemes categorically. Alvin Liberman and colleagues (1957) varied a speech sound in equal acoustic steps and found that people heard a sudden switch from one phoneme to another, not a smooth change, and could barely tell apart sounds within one category. Richard Warren (1970) showed phonemic restoration: when a cough replaced a sound in a sentence, listeners still 'heard' the missing phoneme. Example: Riya, a Hindi speaker, easily hears the difference between 'pal' (moment) and 'phal' (fruit), because aspiration is a phoneme contrast in Hindi; her English-speaking friend hears both as the same 'p', since in English aspiration never changes meaning. Option a is right because the phoneme is the minimal sound unit that listeners pick out and recognise in speech. Exam trap: the smallest unit of sound is the phoneme and the smallest unit of meaning is the morpheme; examiners swap them, and add syntax and semantics, which are rule systems and levels of meaning, not units.", "traps": {"b": "Syntax is the set of rules for ordering words into phrases and sentences (Noam Chomsky's Syntactic Structures, 1957); it is a rule system, not a unit of sound.", "c": "A morpheme is the smallest unit of meaning (for example 'un-', 'kind', '-ness') and is usually made of several phonemes, so it is larger than the smallest unit of speech sound.", "d": "Semantics is the study of meaning in words and sentences; like syntax it is a level of language analysis, not a perceptual unit."}}, {"q": "Separation anxiety is a part of:", "concept": "The answer is b: separation anxiety appears in Bowlby's clear-cut attachment phase. John Bowlby (1969), in Attachment and Loss, argued that attachment is an evolved system that keeps a helpless infant close to a caregiver, and that it unfolds in four phases. In pre-attachment (birth to about 6 weeks) built-in signals such as crying, grasping, gazing and smiling bring any adult near, and the baby does not mind being left with a stranger. In attachment-in-the-making (6 weeks to 6 to 8 months) the infant responds differently to familiar people, smiling and babbling more for the mother, but still does not protest when she leaves. In clear-cut attachment (6 to 8 months to 18 months to 2 years) the baby has a definite attachment figure, uses her as a secure base for exploring, follows her once he can crawl, and shows separation anxiety (crying and protest when she leaves) and wariness of strangers. In formation of a reciprocal relationship (18 months to 2 years onwards) language and an understanding of the parent's plans let the child negotiate, so protest falls; Bowlby called this the goal-corrected partnership. Rudolph Schaffer and Peggy Emerson (1964), following Glasgow infants at home, found similar timing: specific attachments at about 7 months, with fear of strangers about a month later. Example: nine-month-old Kabir happily went to any relative at four months, but now screams whenever his mother leaves the room and clings to her when a visitor arrives. Option b is right because separation protest is the marker of the clear-cut phase. Exam trap: attachment-in-the-making sounds as if attachment is already in place, but the baby only prefers familiar people then; protest at separation and stranger wariness belong to clear-cut attachment.", "traps": {"a": "Attachment-in-the-making (6 weeks to 6 to 8 months) is when the infant starts preferring familiar people but does not yet protest at being separated from them.", "c": "Pre-attachment (birth to about 6 weeks) is when innate signals such as crying and gazing attract any adult, and the baby shows no distress at being left with strangers.", "d": "Formation of a reciprocal relationship (from 18 months to 2 years) is when language and an understanding of the parent's comings and goings let the child negotiate, so separation protest declines."}}, {"q": "Freud's stage of latency is corroborating with _____ stage of Erikson's psychosocial development.", "concept": "The answer is c: Freud's latency stage matches Erikson's industry versus inferiority. Sigmund Freud (1905), in Three Essays on the Theory of Sexuality, described five psychosexual stages: oral (birth to about 1), anal (about 1 to 3), phallic (about 3 to 6), latency (about 6 to puberty) and genital (puberty onwards). In latency the sexual drive is quiet, and energy goes into school learning, skills, same-sex friendships and hobbies. Erik Erikson (1950), in Childhood and Society, kept Freud's timetable for childhood but asked what social task each age poses. His eight psychosocial crises are trust versus mistrust (oral), autonomy versus shame and doubt (anal), initiative versus guilt (phallic), industry versus inferiority (latency), identity versus role confusion (adolescence, Freud's genital stage), and then three adult stages Freud never described: intimacy versus isolation, generativity versus stagnation, and integrity versus despair. In industry versus inferiority (about 6 to 12 years) the child learns the tools of the culture at school; success with teachers and peers builds the virtue of competence, while repeated failure or harsh comparison leaves a sense of inferiority. Erikson even labelled this stage 'school age (latency)'. Example: nine-year-old Arjun spends evenings building model aeroplanes and learning to bowl; when his teacher praises his science project he feels capable, but if he is always compared with his topper cousin he may come to feel he is no good at anything. Option c is right because latency and industry versus inferiority cover the same years and the same focus on skills and schooling. Exam trap: count the stages carefully; initiative versus guilt matches the phallic (play age) stage, and identity versus role confusion matches Freud's genital stage, not latency.", "traps": {"a": "Initiative versus guilt (about 3 to 6 years, the play age) is Erik Erikson's counterpart of Freud's phallic stage, when children plan, pretend and take the lead.", "b": "Identity versus role confusion (adolescence, about 12 to 18 years) parallels Freud's genital stage at puberty, not latency.", "d": "Intimacy versus isolation (young adulthood) is one of Erik Erikson's own adult stages and has no counterpart in Freud's scheme."}}, {"q": "'Beyond the Pleasure Principle' author is:", "concept": "The answer is c: Sigmund Freud wrote Beyond the Pleasure Principle. Sigmund Freud (1920) wrote this essay to explain facts his earlier theory could not. Until then he held that the mind is ruled by the pleasure principle: the id seeks immediate gratification and release of tension, and the ego, guided by the reality principle, merely delays pleasure to obtain it safely. But soldiers back from the First World War kept reliving their traumas in dreams, patients kept repeating painful patterns in therapy, and his small grandson replayed the distress of his mother leaving in the 'fort-da' game, throwing away a cotton reel and pulling it back. Freud called this urge to repeat unpleasant experience the repetition compulsion. To explain it he proposed a second basic drive beside the life instincts (Eros, whose energy is libido): the death instinct, an unconscious pull toward decay and a return to the inanimate state. Turned outward it appears as aggression; turned inward, as self-destruction. His followers called it Thanatos, a word Freud did not use in his writings. The essay opened his late theory, followed by The Ego and the Id (1923), which introduced the structural model of id, ego and superego. Example: after a road accident, Riya keeps having the same nightmare of the crash; the pleasure principle cannot explain why the mind would replay pain, which is exactly the puzzle Freud tackled. Option c is right because the book is Freud's, and it introduced the death instinct and the repetition compulsion. Exam trap: examiners pair famous titles with the wrong author; The Ego and the Mechanisms of Defence (1936) is Anna Freud's, and Psychological Types (1921) is Carl Gustav Jung's.", "traps": {"a": "Carl Gustav Jung wrote Psychology of the Unconscious (1912, later Symbols of Transformation) and Psychological Types (1921), on the collective unconscious and introversion and extraversion; he rejected Freud's sexual and death drives.", "b": "Alfred Adler wrote The Neurotic Constitution (1912) and Understanding Human Nature (1927), on inferiority feelings and striving for superiority, not on the death instinct.", "d": "Anna Freud, Sigmund Freud's daughter, wrote The Ego and the Mechanisms of Defence (1936), which systematised the defence mechanisms; Beyond the Pleasure Principle was her father's."}}, {"q": "All are mature defence mechanisms except:", "concept": "The answer is d: projection is not a mature defence; it is an immature one. Defence mechanisms are unconscious ego strategies that protect a person from anxiety; Sigmund Freud described them and Anna Freud (1936) catalogued them. George Vaillant (1977), in Adaptation to Life, followed the Harvard men of the Grant Study for decades and ranked defences by maturity. Psychotic level: delusional projection, psychotic denial, distortion. Immature level: projection, fantasy, hypochondriasis, passive aggression and acting out. Neurotic (intermediate) level: repression, displacement, reaction formation, intellectualisation and dissociation. Mature level: altruism, humour, suppression, sublimation and anticipation. Men who relied on mature defences had better work, marriages and health in midlife. Projection means attributing your own unacceptable feelings or impulses to someone else. It distorts reality about other people, so it sits low on the ladder; in its fixed, delusional form, as in paranoid delusions, it is psychotic level. The mature defences let feelings in while handling them well: humour finds the funny side without hurting anyone, altruism turns your own need into service to others, suppression is a conscious decision to set a worry aside until later, sublimation channels an impulse into an acceptable outlet, and anticipation plans ahead for future distress. Example: Kabir resents his new manager but tells everyone the manager hates him (projection); a more mature Kabir notices his annoyance, decides to deal with it after the deadline (suppression) and jokes about it with friends (humour). Option d is right because projection belongs to Vaillant's immature group. Exam trap: suppression sounds like repression, but suppression is conscious and mature while repression is unconscious and neurotic level, so do not pick suppression as the odd one out.", "traps": {"a": "Humour is a mature defence in George Vaillant's (1977) hierarchy: it lets a person express uncomfortable feelings openly and lightly without discomfort to others.", "b": "Altruism is a mature defence in George Vaillant's (1977) hierarchy: meeting your own needs by constructively serving others.", "c": "Suppression is the mature, conscious decision to postpone attention to a conflict; it is not repression, which is unconscious and neurotic level."}}, {"q": "Token economy is behaviour modification technique based on the following learning principles:", "concept": "The answer is c: the token economy rests on positive reinforcement. A token economy is an operant programme built on B. F. Skinner's (1938) principle that behaviour followed by a rewarding consequence becomes more likely. Teodoro Ayllon and Nathan Azrin (1968, The Token Economy) ran the first full programme at Anna State Hospital in Illinois: long-stay psychiatric patients earned tokens for target behaviours such as self-care, grooming and ward jobs, and exchanged them for back-up reinforcers such as privileges, snacks or outings. Self-care and work rose sharply while tokens were given and fell when they were withdrawn. The tokens are secondary (conditioned) reinforcers: worthless in themselves, they gain value because they can be exchanged, as money does, and because they buy many things they work as generalised reinforcers that resist satiation. A good token economy defines target behaviours clearly, gives tokens immediately and consistently, sets a clear exchange rate, and gradually replaces tokens with social praise so gains last outside the programme. Some programmes add response cost, removing tokens for problem behaviour, which is negative punishment, but the core is positive reinforcement. Token economies are used on psychiatric wards, in classrooms, for children with ADHD or autism, and in correctional homes. Example: eight-year-old Arjun earns a star each time he finishes homework without reminders; ten stars buy a Sunday trip to the park, and homework completion climbs. Option c is right because giving a token after the desired behaviour, in order to increase it, is positive reinforcement. Exam trap: the token economy is operant, not classical conditioning, and it works by adding something pleasant (positive reinforcement), not by removing something unpleasant (negative reinforcement).", "traps": {"a": "Discrimination learning is learning to respond to one stimulus and not to another, as when a pigeon pecks only when a light is green; it is part of stimulus control, not the basis of the token economy.", "b": "Negative reinforcement increases a behaviour by removing an unpleasant stimulus (taking a painkiller to end a headache); tokens add a reward instead of removing something aversive.", "d": "Classical conditioning (Ivan Pavlov, 1927) pairs a neutral stimulus with one that already triggers a reflex; systematic desensitisation and aversion therapy come from it, but the token economy is operant."}}, {"q": "Insulin is released by:", "concept": "The answer is c: insulin is released by the pancreas. The pancreas is both an exocrine gland, sending digestive enzymes into the gut through a duct, and an endocrine gland, releasing hormones straight into the blood. Its endocrine part is the islets of Langerhans, described by Paul Langerhans (1869). Beta cells in the islets make insulin; alpha cells make glucagon. After a meal blood glucose rises, and insulin lets muscle, fat and liver cells take up glucose and store the surplus as glycogen and fat, so blood sugar falls. Between meals glucagon does the opposite, releasing glucose from the liver. Frederick Banting and Charles Best (1921) extracted insulin in Toronto, and in 1922 it was first used to treat diabetes. Too little insulin, or cells that stop responding to it, gives diabetes mellitus. Insulin matters to psychologists in three ways. In hunger, insulin rises even before food arrives (the cephalic phase, triggered by the sight and smell of food), and high insulin increases hunger and fat storage. Clinically, low blood sugar from too much insulin causes shakiness, sweating, irritability and confusion that can mimic a panic attack. Historically, Manfred Sakel (1933) induced comas with insulin to treat schizophrenia (insulin coma therapy), a method later abandoned. Example: after Riya eats a plate of rice, her blood glucose climbs and her pancreas releases insulin; if she were diabetic and injected too much insulin, she might feel shaky and panicky an hour later. Option c is right because insulin is made by the beta cells of the pancreatic islets. Exam trap: the pituitary is called the master gland, so examiners offer it for every hormone, but insulin release is driven mainly by blood glucose itself, not by a pituitary hormone.", "traps": {"a": "The adrenal medulla releases adrenaline (epinephrine) and noradrenaline for the fight-or-flight response; the adrenal cortex releases cortisol and aldosterone, not insulin.", "b": "The thyroid gland releases thyroxine (T4) and triiodothyronine (T3), which set the metabolic rate, and calcitonin; too little thyroid hormone in infancy causes cretinism.", "d": "The pituitary, the 'master gland', releases growth hormone, ACTH, TSH, prolactin and the gonadotropins, and stores oxytocin and ADH made in the hypothalamus; it does not release insulin."}}, {"q": "For Skinner, the basic issue is how reinforcement sustains and controls responding rather than:", "concept": "The answer is a: for B. F. Skinner the question was how reinforcement controls responding, not which stimulus evokes a response. B. F. Skinner (1938), in The Behavior of Organisms, separated two kinds of behaviour. Respondent behaviour is elicited by a known stimulus: food in the mouth produces salivation, a puff of air produces a blink. This is the reflex behaviour Ivan Pavlov (1927) studied in classical conditioning, where the scientist's task is to find which stimulus evokes which response. Operant behaviour is emitted: a rat in a Skinner box presses a lever, a child raises a hand, and no stimulus forces the act. What controls operant behaviour is its consequences. Responses followed by reinforcement become more frequent; responses followed by punishment or by nothing become less frequent. Skinner measured the rate of responding on a cumulative recorder, and with Charles Ferster (1957) showed how schedules of reinforcement (fixed and variable ratio and interval) shape the pattern and persistence of responding. Stimuli still matter, but as discriminative stimuli that signal when reinforcement is available; they set the occasion for a response rather than trigger it. This is the three-term contingency: discriminative stimulus, response, consequence. Example: Arjun raises his hand in class not because anything makes him, but because his teacher has praised his answers before; when a new teacher ignores raised hands, his hand-raising fades. Option a is right because asking which stimulus evokes a response is the respondent (Pavlovian) question that Skinner set aside. Exam trap: generalisation and discrimination sound like stimulus questions Skinner ignored, but he studied both within operant stimulus control; what he dropped was the search for an eliciting stimulus.", "traps": {"b": "Whether a response is 'helpful' is not the contrast Skinner drew; he asked how consequences change the rate of a response, whether or not the response is useful to the organism.", "c": "Stimulus generalisation, responding to stimuli similar to the training stimulus, was studied by Ivan Pavlov (1927) and by B. F. Skinner within operant stimulus control, so it is not what Skinner set aside.", "d": "Stimulus discrimination, responding only when the discriminative stimulus is present, is central to B. F. Skinner's three-term contingency, so it is something he studied, not something he replaced."}}, {"q": "Jung used the term \"Collective Unconscious\" to indicate:", "concept": "The answer is a: Jung's collective unconscious is a layer of inherited unconscious tendencies that comes from humanity's ancestral past. Carl Gustav Jung (1916), after his break with Sigmund Freud in 1913, described the psyche in three layers. The ego is the conscious mind. Below it lies the personal unconscious, made of forgotten and repressed experiences of one's own life and organised into complexes, emotionally charged clusters such as a mother complex. Deepest of all is the collective unconscious, which is not built from personal experience at all: it is inherited and shared by all human beings, the residue of the experiences of our ancestors. It contains archetypes, inborn tendencies to perceive and respond in typical ways, which appear as universal images in myths, religions, fairy tales and dreams: the persona (social mask), the shadow (the disowned dark side), the anima and animus (the feminine side of a man and masculine side of a woman), the Great Mother, the wise old man, the hero and the self (the archetype of wholeness, often shown as a mandala). Jung's evidence was the recurrence of the same motifs in the myths of cultures that never met, and in the dreams and delusions of patients who could not have known those myths. Older textbooks call this inheritance 'racial', meaning the human race as a whole, not any ethnic group. Example: Meera, who has never read mythology, dreams of a flood that destroys the world and a single boat that survives; Jung would point to the same motif in the story of Manu in India and of Noah in the Bible. Option a is right because it describes inherited, primordial tendencies, which is exactly the collective unconscious. Exam trap: examiners offer crowd psychology or Freud's repressed urges, but those concern groups or personal history, while Jung's collective unconscious is universal and inherited.", "traps": {"b": "'All the factors that strengthen an individual's unconscious' is vague and points to the personal unconscious, which Carl Gustav Jung built from one person's own forgotten and repressed experiences, not from inheritance.", "c": "The unconscious potential of a group or mob is crowd psychology, from Gustave Le Bon (1895, The Crowd), who described contagion and suggestibility in crowds; it is not Jung's concept.", "d": "Unconscious urges that make behaviour problematic describe Sigmund Freud's view of repressed impulses producing neurotic symptoms, which belongs to the personal unconscious, not the collective one."}}, {"q": "A child has two alleles of every gene in his body, one from his mother and one from his father. If the alleles from both the parents differ, he is said to be:", "concept": "The answer is a: when the two alleles of a gene differ, the person is heterozygous for that gene. Gregor Mendel (1866), crossing pea plants, showed that each trait depends on a pair of inherited factors, now called genes, one from each parent. A gene sits at a fixed place (locus) on a chromosome, and humans carry 23 pairs of chromosomes, so every gene on the non-sex chromosomes comes in two copies. The alternative forms of a gene are alleles. If both copies are the same (AA or aa) the person is homozygous; if they differ (Aa) the person is heterozygous. The genotype is the set of alleles carried; the phenotype is the trait that shows. In a heterozygote a dominant allele is expressed and masks a recessive one, so a recessive trait shows only in the homozygote (aa), and heterozygotes for a recessive disorder are healthy carriers. Example: beta-thalassaemia, common in India, is recessive. Arjun inherits a thalassaemia allele from his mother and a normal allele from his father; he is heterozygous, with thalassaemia trait and at most mild anaemia. If he marries Meera, who is also a carrier, each child has a 1 in 4 chance of being homozygous and having thalassaemia major, a 1 in 2 chance of being a carrier, and a 1 in 4 chance of being unaffected. This is why carrier screening before marriage is encouraged. The same logic applies to phenylketonuria (PKU), in which a homozygous child cannot convert phenylalanine into tyrosine. Option a is right because 'hetero' means different: two different alleles at the same locus. Exam trap: homozygous and heterozygous describe the two alleles inside one person, while monozygotic (identical) and dizygotic (fraternal) describe how twins form; the shared root 'zygous' is the confusion examiners use.", "traps": {"b": "Homozygous means the two alleles of a gene are the same (AA or aa), the opposite of the situation in the stem.", "c": "An identical (monozygotic) twin develops from one fertilised egg that splits, so the twins share virtually all their genes; this is about how twins form, not about differing alleles.", "d": "A fraternal (dizygotic) twin develops from two eggs fertilised by two sperm and shares about half its genes with the co-twin, like ordinary siblings; it says nothing about whether a person's alleles differ."}}, {"q": "The ability to perceive and understand a situation or incident from another person's point of view is called:", "concept": "The answer is b: of the options given, understanding a situation from another person's point of view is called social foresight. The standard name for this ability is perspective taking, or role taking. George Herbert Mead (1934), in Mind, Self and Society, argued that the self grows out of 'taking the role of the other': the child learns to see herself as others see her, first in play with single roles, then in organised games that need everyone's viewpoint (the generalised other). Jean Piaget and Bärbel Inhelder (1956) showed with the three mountains task that young preoperational children are egocentric: asked what a doll on the far side of a model sees, they describe their own view. Robert Selman (1976, 1980) traced social perspective taking through five levels using dilemmas such as Holly, who has promised not to climb trees but sees a friend's kitten stuck in one; children move from an egocentric level (about 3 to 6) to a societal level in adolescence. Perspective taking underlies empathy, theory of mind, moral reasoning and helping. Example: when his friend Riya snaps at him, Kabir thinks of her sick mother and her exam stress, sees why she is short-tempered, and does not take it personally. Option b is right because it is the only option about grasping another person's viewpoint; the others concern one's own feelings or perception. Exam trap: projection sounds social but runs the other way, putting your own feelings into someone else instead of understanding theirs. Key note: 'social foresight' is not a standard psychological term; textbooks call this ability perspective taking or role taking (George Herbert Mead, Robert Selman) or empathy. Since it is the only option about understanding another person's viewpoint, b stands.", "traps": {"a": "Projection, a defence described by Sigmund Freud and catalogued by Anna Freud (1936), attributes your own unacceptable feelings to others; it distorts their viewpoint instead of understanding it.", "c": "Imagination is forming mental images or ideas of things not present to the senses; it can help in perspective taking but is not itself the ability to see a situation from another person's view.", "d": "An illusion is a misperception of a real stimulus, such as the Müller-Lyer lines (Franz Müller-Lyer, 1889) looking unequal; it is a perceptual error, not social understanding."}}, {"q": "Perception without sensory stimulus is called:", "concept": "The answer is b: perception without a sensory stimulus is a hallucination. Jean-Étienne Esquirol (1838), the French psychiatrist, gave the modern definition: a hallucination is a perception without an object, and he separated it from an illusion, the misperception of a real object. A true hallucination has the full qualities of a normal perception: it is vivid, it seems to come from outer objective space, and the person cannot stop it by will. Karl Jaspers (1913), in General Psychopathology, contrasted it with the pseudohallucination, which is experienced in inner subjective space ('inside my head') and is recognised as not quite real. Hallucinations occur in every sense. Auditory ones are the most common in schizophrenia, and voices talking about the patient in the third person or keeping up a running commentary are among Kurt Schneider's (1959) first-rank symptoms. Visual hallucinations suggest delirium, drug states or other organic causes; tactile ones such as formication (insects crawling under the skin) occur with cocaine use and alcohol withdrawal. Hypnagogic hallucinations (while falling asleep) and hypnopompic hallucinations (while waking) are common in healthy people. Example: Arjun, alone in a silent room, hears a man's voice saying 'now he is picking up his pen'; nothing is there, so this is a hallucination. If instead he heard the hum of the ceiling fan as whispering, that would be an illusion, because a real sound is being misread. Option b is right because the defining feature of a hallucination is the absence of any external stimulus. Exam trap: examiners swap illusion and hallucination; an illusion needs a real stimulus that is misinterpreted, a hallucination needs none, and a delusion is a false belief, not a perception at all.", "traps": {"a": "An illusion is a misinterpretation of a real external stimulus (a fan's hum heard as a voice, the Müller-Lyer lines looking unequal), so a stimulus is present.", "c": "'Phenomenon' simply means anything observed or experienced, the root of Karl Jaspers's (1913) phenomenological method in psychopathology; it is not a symptom term.", "d": "Perceptual constancy is the tendency to see an object's size, shape, brightness and colour as stable despite changes in its retinal image; it is a normal feature of perceiving real stimuli."}}, {"q": "\"It is not a different process, it is just attention to irrelevant stimuli that are not a part of the main assigned task\", then what is it?", "concept": "The answer is a: attention drawn to irrelevant stimuli outside the main task is distraction. Attention, in William James's (1890) words, is the mind taking possession, in clear and vivid form, of one out of several possible objects or trains of thought. Because the brain's capacity is limited, attention must select. Classical texts list its characteristics as selectivity, shifting, a limited span, fluctuation (attention to an unchanging object waxes and wanes) and division between tasks. Distraction is not a separate mental process added to these. It is ordinary attention, using the same mechanisms, that happens to settle on stimuli irrelevant to the assigned task. The pull can be external, from intense, novel, moving or changing stimuli such as a sudden noise or a phone notification, or internal, from worries, hunger or daydreams. Donald Broadbent's (1958) filter model treats distraction as unwanted input getting past the selective filter, and Neville Moray (1959) showed that some unattended input, such as one's own name, breaks through. Distraction can also be used on purpose: in James Gross's (1998) model of emotion regulation it is an attentional deployment strategy, and therapists teach it for pain and craving. Example: Kabir sits down to revise statistics, but his attention keeps going to WhatsApp pings and the cricket commentary from the next room; nothing is wrong with his attention, it is simply aimed at the wrong things. Option a is right because the stem defines distraction itself: the same attentional process directed at stimuli that are not part of the main task. Exam trap: shifting of attention also involves moving focus, but shifting means moving to a new object, often deliberately; distraction specifically means being pulled away to something irrelevant.", "traps": {"b": "The span of attention is the number of objects that can be apprehended in a single brief glance, about six to eight unrelated items in tachistoscope studies, a line of work begun by William Stanley Jevons (1871) with handfuls of beans; it is a capacity, not a lapse.", "c": "Shifting of attention is moving focus from one object or task to another, often voluntarily as in task switching; it does not imply that the new object is irrelevant to the task.", "d": "Division of attention is attending to two tasks at once, such as driving while talking; it is a deliberate sharing of capacity, not being pulled off task."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is not one of the 4 major approaches to qualitative research?", "concept": "The answer is d: 'non-experimental' is a type of quantitative research design, not one of the qualitative approaches. Qualitative research studies meaning, experience and process through words, observation and documents, analysed by interpretation rather than statistics. It is usually grouped into four (sometimes five) traditions. Ethnography describes a culture or group from the inside through long immersion, mainly participant observation and key informants; Bronisław Malinowski (1922), living among the Trobriand Islanders, set its model. Phenomenology, from the philosopher Edmund Husserl (1913), describes the lived experience of a phenomenon as participants experience it, with the researcher bracketing (setting aside) their own assumptions. Grounded theory, from Barney Glaser and Anselm Strauss (1967), builds theory upward from the data through coding and constant comparison instead of testing a theory decided in advance. The case study gives an intensive, in-depth account of one bounded case, such as a person, family, ward or institution (Robert Yin, 1984; Robert Stake, 1995). John Creswell (1998) adds narrative research, the study of life stories, to make five. Non-experimental research, by contrast, is a broad quantitative category in which the researcher measures variables without manipulating them: correlational studies, surveys and causal-comparative (ex post facto) designs. Example: Meera wants to know what it is like to care for a son with schizophrenia; she interviews eight mothers in depth and describes the essence of their experience (phenomenology). If she instead gave 300 caregivers a burden scale and correlated scores with income, that would be a non-experimental quantitative study. Option d is right because non-experimental is a quantitative design label, not a qualitative tradition. Exam trap: 'non-experimental' sounds qualitative because qualitative researchers never manipulate variables, but the term belongs to the classification of quantitative designs.", "traps": {"a": "Ethnography, modelled by Bronisław Malinowski (1922), studies a culture from the inside through long participant observation; it is one of the core qualitative traditions.", "b": "Phenomenology, from Edmund Husserl (1913), studies lived experience after bracketing the researcher's assumptions; it is a core qualitative tradition.", "c": "The case study, described by Robert Yin (1984) and Robert Stake (1995), is an in-depth study of one bounded case and is listed among the qualitative traditions by John Creswell (1998)."}}, {"q": "In \"phenomenology\", a well written report will be highly descriptive of the participants' experiences and will often elicit in the reader a feeling that they feel as though they are experiencing the phenomenon themselves. This experience is called:", "concept": "The answer is b: the reader's sense of living through the phenomenon is called a vicarious experience. Phenomenology studies lived experience: what it is like, from the inside, to go through something such as grief, chronic pain or a first episode of psychosis. The researcher interviews people who have lived the experience, brackets their own assumptions, identifies significant statements and themes, and writes a description of the essence of the experience. Max van Manen (1990), in Researching Lived Experience, argued that the writing is itself part of the method: a good phenomenological text is rich, concrete and evocative, so that the reader recognises the experience and seems to live it at second hand. That second-hand living is vicarious experience, 'vicarious' meaning experienced through another person rather than directly. It is also how qualitative findings travel: Robert Stake (1995) argued that readers of a case study make naturalistic generalisations from vicarious experience, applying what they felt in the description to their own settings, and Egon Guba and Yvonna Lincoln (1994) list vicarious experience as one way knowledge grows in the constructivist paradigm. A flat list of themes would lose this quality. Example: Riya reads a phenomenological study of mothers of children with autism, full of their own words about sleepless nights, stares in the market and small victories; she finishes it feeling that for a moment she has been inside their days. Option b is right because the stem describes experiencing a phenomenon through someone else's account, which is what vicarious means. Exam trap: 'phenomenal experience' tempts because it echoes the word phenomenology, but it is not the term for the reader's response; and do not mix this up with Albert Bandura's (1977) vicarious experience, a source of self-efficacy gained by watching others succeed.", "traps": {"a": "'Phenomenal experience' is not a research term for this; in philosophy of mind 'phenomenal' refers to conscious experience itself (what it is like to see red), not to a reader's response to a report.", "c": "'Significant experience' is not a technical term; phenomenological analysis does extract 'significant statements' (Paul Colaizzi, 1978), but that is an analysis step, not the reader's experience.", "d": "A dream is a mental experience during sleep, which Sigmund Freud (1900) called the royal road to the unconscious; it has nothing to do with reading a research report."}}, {"q": "Phenomenology has its disciplinary origins in:", "concept": "The answer is a: phenomenology began in philosophy. Edmund Husserl (1859 to 1938), a German philosopher and mathematician, founded phenomenology in the early 1900s, in Logical Investigations (1900 to 1901) and Ideas (1913). He wanted a rigorous way to study consciousness by describing things exactly as they appear in experience, 'back to the things themselves'. His method was the epoché, or bracketing: suspending everyday assumptions and theories about what causes an experience, so that its essential structure can be described. Martin Heidegger (1927), his former assistant, argued in Being and Time that we are always already immersed in the world, so all understanding is interpretation; this became hermeneutic (interpretive) phenomenology. Only later did phenomenology spread into other fields. Alfred Schutz (1932) carried it into sociology with The Phenomenology of the Social World, which in turn fed Peter Berger and Thomas Luckmann's The Social Construction of Reality (1966). In psychology, Amedeo Giorgi (1970) developed a descriptive phenomenological method, and Jonathan Smith (1996) created interpretative phenomenological analysis (IPA), now common in health and clinical psychology. Humanistic psychology, from Carl Rogers (1951) and Abraham Maslow (1954), also draws on its focus on subjective experience. Example: Riya plans an IPA study of how Indian students experience failing an entrance exam; her method traces back through Smith and Heidegger to Husserl's philosophy of consciousness. Option a is right because phenomenology was founded as a philosophical movement by Husserl before any social science adopted it. Exam trap: it is now used in many disciplines, so 'many disciplines' tempts, but the stem asks for its disciplinary origin; ethnography's origin is anthropology and grounded theory's is sociology, which is why b and c are offered.", "traps": {"b": "Anthropology is the home discipline of ethnography, modelled by Bronisław Malinowski (1922), not of phenomenology.", "c": "Sociology took up phenomenology only later, through Alfred Schutz (1932); sociology is the origin of grounded theory (Barney Glaser and Anselm Strauss, 1967) and ethnomethodology (Harold Garfinkel, 1967).", "d": "Phenomenology is now used across many disciplines, including psychology, nursing and education, but it originated in one discipline, philosophy, with Edmund Husserl."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is not a form of non-random sampling?", "concept": "The answer is d: snowball, convenience and quota sampling are all forms of non-random sampling, so none of them is the exception. In probability (random) sampling every member of the population has a known, non-zero chance of selection, which lets the researcher estimate sampling error and generalise with stated confidence. The probability methods are simple random, systematic, stratified random, cluster and multistage sampling. In non-probability (non-random) sampling the researcher's judgement or convenience, not chance, decides who is included, so each person's chance of selection is unknown and bias can creep in. The main non-random methods are convenience (accidental) sampling, taking whoever is easiest to reach, such as students in one's own class; purposive (judgemental) sampling, choosing people who fit the study's purpose; quota sampling, fixing numbers for each subgroup to mirror the population (say 60 men and 40 women) and then letting interviewers fill each quota with anyone available; and snowball (chain-referral) sampling, in which a few participants recruit others like them, the route into hidden populations with no list, such as drug users or sex workers (Leo Goodman, 1961, analysed it formally). The cost of a biased sample showed in the 1936 Literary Digest poll, which predicted that Alf Landon would beat Franklin Roosevelt because its huge sample came largely from telephone and car owners. Example: Arjun studies stigma among families of people who inject drugs; he finds three families through a de-addiction clinic, and they introduce him to others, which is snowball sampling. Option d is right because a, b and c are all textbook non-probability methods. Exam trap: quota sampling looks like stratified random sampling because both divide the population into subgroups, but in quota sampling selection within each subgroup is not random, so it remains non-probability.", "traps": {"a": "Snowball (chain-referral) sampling, in which participants recruit further participants, is a non-random method used for hidden populations, so it is not the exception.", "b": "Convenience (accidental) sampling takes whoever is easiest to reach and is the most basic non-random method, so it is not the exception.", "c": "Quota sampling fills fixed quotas for subgroups but lets the interviewer choose people within each quota, so it is non-random despite resembling stratified random sampling."}}, {"q": "Illnesses that seem to result from an interaction of physical and psychological factors are called:", "concept": "The answer is c: illnesses that come from an interaction of physical and psychological factors are called psychosomatic. 'Psychosomatic' joins psyche (mind) and soma (body); the term is credited to the German physician Johann Christian Heinroth (1818). A psychosomatic disorder is a real physical disease, with measurable tissue damage or physiological change, whose onset or course is worsened by psychological factors such as stress, conflict or emotion. Franz Alexander (1950), in Psychosomatic Medicine, proposed that a specific unconscious conflict acting on a vulnerable organ produced each of seven classic psychosomatic disorders, the 'holy seven': peptic ulcer, essential hypertension, bronchial asthma, rheumatoid arthritis, ulcerative colitis, neurodermatitis and thyrotoxicosis. The specificity idea failed (most ulcers proved to be caused by Helicobacter pylori bacteria), but the broader point survived: chronic stress raises cortisol, suppresses immunity and worsens many diseases. George Engel (1977) widened this into the biopsychosocial model. DSM-5 now calls the category 'psychological factors affecting other medical conditions', and ICD-10 codes it F54. Example: Kabir has genuine asthma, confirmed by lung function tests; his attacks become much more frequent in the weeks before his board exams and after quarrels at home. The disease is real, and stress is making it worse. Option c is right because the stem stresses an interaction of physical and psychological factors, which defines psychosomatic illness. Exam trap: psychosomatic does not mean imaginary or 'all in the mind'; there is real bodily disease. In conversion disorder, by contrast, the symptom (paralysis, blindness, seizures) has no physical disease behind it, which is why examiners pair the two.", "traps": {"a": "'Somatic' simply means bodily; DSM-5 somatic symptom disorder is distressing bodily symptoms with excessive thoughts, feelings and behaviours about them, whether or not a medical disease is present.", "b": "'Hysterical' is the old label, from Hippocrates (about 400 BCE) to Josef Breuer and Sigmund Freud's Studies on Hysteria (1895), for conversion and dissociative symptoms without organic disease; DSM-III (1980) dropped it.", "d": "Conversion disorder (DSM-5 functional neurological symptom disorder) shows neurological symptoms such as paralysis or seizures that do not fit any recognised neurological disease, so there is no physical illness for psychological factors to interact with."}}, {"q": "A part of the brain that sends signals \"alert\" to higher centres of the brain in response to incoming message is:", "concept": "The answer is d: the reticular formation alerts the higher centres of the brain to incoming messages. The reticular formation ('reticulum' means little net) is a net-like column of neurons running through the core of the brainstem, from the medulla through the pons to the midbrain. Sensory pathways heading for the cortex send side branches (collaterals) into it, so it samples nearly all incoming information. Its ascending part, the reticular activating system (RAS), projects through the thalamus to the whole cortex and keeps it awake and alert. The classic evidence came from Giuseppe Moruzzi and Horace Magoun (1949): electrically stimulating the brainstem reticular formation in a drowsy or anaesthetised cat changed the slow, synchronised EEG of sleep into the fast, desynchronised EEG of alert waking. Earlier, Frédéric Bremer (1935) had shown that cutting the brainstem at the midbrain (cerveau isolé) left the forebrain in permanent sleep-like EEG, while cutting lower, between medulla and spinal cord (encéphale isolé), left normal sleep and waking. Damage to the RAS causes coma. The system also filters input, passing on novel or important signals and damping repetitive ones, and its descending fibres adjust muscle tone and reflexes. Example: Meera dozes through the drone of the fan in a lecture, but jolts awake the moment the teacher calls her name; her reticular formation passed on the important signal and roused her cortex. Option d is right because sending an 'alert' signal to the cortex in response to incoming input is the defining job of the reticular activating system. Exam trap: the limbic system and amygdala also respond to incoming signals, but they add emotional meaning; general arousal and wakefulness belong to the reticular formation.", "traps": {"a": "The amygdala, an almond-shaped nucleus in the medial temporal lobe, attaches fear and emotional significance to stimuli (Joseph LeDoux's low road, 1996); it does not set the general arousal level of the cortex.", "b": "The hippocampus, in the medial temporal lobe, consolidates new declarative memories and spatial maps; Henry Molaison (H.M.), studied by William Scoville and Brenda Milner (1957), could not form new memories after its removal.", "c": "The limbic system, built on James Papez's circuit (1937) and named by Paul MacLean (1952), is a ring of structures for emotion and memory, not the brainstem network that alerts the cortex."}}, {"q": "The _____ lobe is to hearing as the occipital lobe is to vision.", "concept": "The answer is c: the temporal lobe is to hearing as the occipital lobe is to vision. Each cerebral hemisphere has four lobes: frontal, parietal, temporal and occipital. The primary auditory cortex lies in the temporal lobe, on the upper surface of the superior temporal gyrus, tucked inside the lateral (Sylvian) fissure, in the transverse temporal gyri named after the Austrian anatomist Richard Heschl (1878): Heschl's gyrus, Brodmann areas 41 and 42. The pathway runs from the cochlea through the auditory nerve, brainstem nuclei and the medial geniculate nucleus of the thalamus to Heschl's gyrus. The auditory cortex is tonotopically organised: like keys on a piano, neighbouring strips respond to neighbouring pitches. Around it lies auditory association cortex, and on the left, at the back of the superior temporal gyrus, Wernicke's area (Carl Wernicke, 1874) for understanding speech. Each ear sends input to both hemispheres, so damage to one auditory cortex does not cause deafness; damage on both sides can produce cortical deafness or auditory agnosia. The parallel for vision is the primary visual cortex (V1, striate cortex, Brodmann area 17) around the calcarine sulcus of the occipital lobe, fed by the lateral geniculate nucleus of the thalamus. Example: when Arjun's phone rings, the sound reaches his temporal lobes within milliseconds, and recognising it as his own ringtone draws on the nearby auditory association areas. Option c is right because the temporal lobe holds the primary auditory cortex, just as the occipital lobe holds the primary visual cortex. Exam trap: the cerebellum is not a cerebral lobe at all, and the parietal lobe handles touch and body sense (somatosensory cortex), which examiners offer because it lies next to the auditory region.", "traps": {"a": "The cerebellum is a separate hindbrain structure, not a lobe of the cerebrum; it coordinates movement, balance and motor learning.", "b": "The parietal lobe holds the primary somatosensory cortex on the postcentral gyrus (touch, pain, body position) and handles spatial attention; right parietal damage causes hemispatial neglect.", "d": "The frontal lobe holds the primary motor cortex (precentral gyrus), Broca's area for speech production and the prefrontal cortex for executive functions, not hearing."}}, {"q": "What is the role of positive and negative reinforcement?", "concept": "The answer is a: both positive and negative reinforcement increase the likelihood that the response before them will be repeated. In operant conditioning, B. F. Skinner (1938) classified consequences by their effect on the behaviour they follow, building on Edward Thorndike's (1898) law of effect. Reinforcement is any consequence that makes a behaviour more likely; punishment is any consequence that makes it less likely. 'Positive' and 'negative' do not mean good and bad; they mean adding and removing. Positive reinforcement adds something pleasant after the response (praise, money, a sweet). Negative reinforcement removes something unpleasant after the response (pain stops, nagging ends, an alarm goes quiet). Both strengthen the response. The matching pair for weakening behaviour is positive punishment, adding something unpleasant (a scolding), and negative punishment, removing something pleasant (time-out, or response cost such as a fine). Negative reinforcement drives escape learning (ending an aversive stimulus already present) and avoidance learning (preventing it from arriving), and Orval Hobart Mowrer (1947) used it in his two-factor theory to explain why phobic avoidance and compulsions persist: each avoidance brings relief, which reinforces it. Example: Riya finishes her homework and her mother lets her watch television (positive reinforcement); on another day Riya finishes her homework so that her mother stops nagging (negative reinforcement). Either way she becomes more likely to finish her homework in future. Option a is right because reinforcement, positive or negative, by definition increases the probability of the preceding response. Exam trap: negative reinforcement is not punishment; it increases behaviour by taking away something aversive, while punishment decreases behaviour, and examiners rely on students reading 'negative' as 'bad'.", "traps": {"b": "Option b describes punishment, not negative reinforcement; negative reinforcement increases a response by removing an aversive stimulus, so it does not make the response less likely.", "c": "This is half right: negative reinforcement does increase responses, but so does positive reinforcement, so limiting the effect to negative reinforcement is wrong.", "d": "Reinforcement is defined by its strengthening effect on behaviour, not by preventing negative consequences; negative reinforcement in fact involves an unpleasant stimulus that is removed."}}]});
